var e={"preventive-prevention-screening":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Prevention** is any action that interrupts the natural history of disease.`,`The level of prevention is named by **where in the natural history** it acts.`,`**Screening** (CCI 1951): presumptive identification of unrecognised disease in apparently healthy people.`,`Screening **sorts, it never diagnoses**, so every positive needs a confirmatory test.`,`Screening is a form of **secondary prevention**.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Primordial**: stops the risk factor itself appearing, mainly through policy and childhood habit.`,`**Primary**: risk factor present, disease absent; health promotion plus specific protection.`,`**Secondary**: early diagnosis and prompt treatment, including screening and case finding.`,`**Tertiary**: established disease; disability limitation and rehabilitation.`,`**Quaternary** (Jamoulle, WONCA): protects the patient from overmedicalisation; the family physician's own level.`,`The level depends on the **stage of disease in that patient**, not on the drug used.`,`Health education is **a tool at every level**, not a level of its own.`]},{title:`Key concepts`,points:[`**Iceberg phenomenon**: visible cases are the tip; undiagnosed and subclinical cases lie below.`,`**DPCP** (detectable preclinical phase) must be long enough, and early treatment must help.`,`**Rose's population strategy** shifts the whole distribution and acts on causes of incidence.`,`**Rose's high-risk strategy** treats the tail and acts on causes of cases.`,`**Prevention paradox**: big community benefit, but little benefit to each individual.`,`A risk factor is causal and modifiable; a **risk marker** (age, sex) is not.`]},{title:`Classification of screening`,points:[`By use: case detection, disease control, research and educational opportunity.`,`**Mass screening** covers a whole population; **selective screening** targets high-risk groups.`,`**Multiphasic screening** applies several tests at one sitting.`,`**Opportunistic screening (case finding)** is the commonest type in family practice.`]},{title:`Test performance`,points:[`**Sensitivity and specificity** belong to the test.`,`**Predictive values** belong to the population tested; PPV falls as prevalence falls.`,`**Series testing** raises specificity and PPV; parallel testing raises sensitivity.`,`Use a sensitive test when missing a case is dangerous, for example HIV.`,`**ROC curve** with the Youden index (Sn + Sp - 1) picks the best cut-off.`,`Reliability (repeatability) is measured by **kappa**; yield is highest in the first round.`]},{title:`Screening criteria and biases`,points:[`**Wilson and Jungner (WHO 1968)** gave ten criteria: disease, test, treatment and programme.`,`**Andermann 2008** added defined target, evidence, informed choice, equity, quality assurance, benefit over harm.`,`**Lead time bias**: earlier diagnosis inflates survival without postponing death.`,`**Length time bias**: screening picks up slow tumours; aggressive ones appear as interval cancers.`,`**Overdiagnosis**: finding disease that would never have caused symptoms or death.`,`Only an **RCT of invitation** with disease-specific mortality escapes all these biases.`]},{title:`Prevention and programmes`,points:[`**NP-NCD** screens everyone aged **30 and over** at the Ayushman Arogya Mandir.`,`ASHA fills the **CBAC**; a score above 4 means priority, but all get BP and glucose.`,`Cervix: **VIA with 3-5% acetic acid**, women 30-65, every 5 years, screen-and-treat.`,`Breast: **clinical breast examination**; oral: oral visual examination; both every 5 years from 30.`,`National **single-dose quadrivalent HPV vaccine** for girls aged 14 launched 28 February 2026.`,`TB: symptom screen with **upfront NAAT**; sickle cell mission screens 7 crore aged 0-40.`,`Antenatal: HIV, syphilis, HBsAg, and GDM by **DIPSI 75 g** (2-hour 140 mg/dL or more).`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Write every measure as **level, intervention and deliverer**.`,`**Close the loop**: each positive needs a named confirmatory test, date and responsible person.`,`Suspicious cervix, oral lesion over 2 weeks or hard breast lump: **biopsy within the week**.`,`BP 180/110 or more, or RBS over 250 with symptoms: **treat the same day**.`,`A symptomatic person needs diagnosis, not screening.`,`Track coverage, follow-up completion, control rates and stage at diagnosis.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Levels of prevention - diabetes example`,columns:[`Level`,`Point in natural history`,`Mode of intervention`,`Diabetes example`],rows:[[`Primordial`,`Before the risk factor appears`,`Policy, childhood habits`,`School nutrition policy, sugar tax`],[`Primary`,`Risk factor, no disease`,`Health promotion, specific protection`,`Weight loss, 150 min/week activity`],[`Secondary`,`Early or subclinical disease`,`Early diagnosis, prompt treatment`,`CBAC and RBS at 30, metformin`],[`Tertiary`,`Established disease`,`Disability limitation, rehabilitation`,`Annual eye and foot checks`],[`Quaternary`,`Risk of overmedicalisation`,`Avoid needless tests and treatment`,`Deprescribe sulfonylurea in a frail elder`]]},{heading:`Screening tests under NP-NCD`,columns:[`Condition`,`Target and interval`,`Test`,`If positive`],rows:[[`Hypertension`,`All 30 and over, yearly`,`BP, 2 seated readings`,`140/90 or more: confirm at PHC`],[`Diabetes`,`All 30 and over, yearly`,`Random capillary glucose`,`140 mg/dL or more: fasting or HbA1c`],[`Cervical cancer`,`Women 30-65, every 5 years`,`VIA (HPV DNA if available)`,`Thermal ablation or colposcopy, biopsy`],[`Breast and oral cancer`,`30 and over, every 5 years`,`CBE; oral visual examination`,`Imaging or biopsy within 2 weeks`],[`Tuberculosis`,`All attendees, vulnerable groups`,`Symptom screen, chest X-ray`,`Upfront NAAT; notify on Ni-kshay`]]},{heading:`Biases in judging a screening programme`,columns:[`Bias`,`What happens`,`How to avoid`],rows:[[`Lead time`,`Survival looks longer, death not delayed`,`Use disease-specific mortality`],[`Length time`,`Slow tumours over-detected`,`Count interval cancers`],[`Overdiagnosis`,`Harmless disease labelled and treated`,`RCT of invitation`],[`Volunteer (selection)`,`Attenders are healthier`,`Intention-to-screen analysis`]]}],remember:[`Level is set by the **stage of disease**, not the drug: iron can be primary, secondary or tertiary.`,`**SnNout, SpPin**: a sensitive negative rules out, a specific positive rules in.`,`Sn and Sp belong to the test; **PPV falls with prevalence**.`,`Series raises specificity; **parallel raises sensitivity**.`,`Quote **Andermann 2008** after Wilson-Jungner for extra marks.`,`Screening alone never controls an NCD; it does not move the population mean.`]},updated:[`MoHFW 2026: national single-dose quadrivalent HPV vaccination (Gardasil-4) for girls aged 14 launched 28 February 2026 - replaces the 2024 'announced for 9-14' statement`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Levels of prevention and screening`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Prevention** interrupts the natural history of disease; level is named by **where in that history it acts**. **Screening** (CCI 1951) = **presumptive identification of unrecognised disease** in apparently healthy people - it **sorts, never diagnoses**.`],[`Classification - levels`,`**Primordial** (stop the risk factor appearing) -> **primary** (health promotion + specific protection) -> **secondary** (early diagnosis and prompt treatment) -> **tertiary** (disability limitation + rehabilitation) -> **quaternary** (protect from overmedicalisation, Jamoulle/WONCA).`],[`Classification - screening`,`By use: **case detection, disease control, research, education**. By scope: **mass, high-risk (selective), multiphasic, opportunistic (case finding)** - commonest in family practice.`],[`Key concepts`,`**Iceberg phenomenon**; **DPCP** (detectable preclinical phase); **Rose: population strategy** (shift the mean, causes of incidence) vs **high-risk strategy** (tail, causes of cases); **prevention paradox**; level set by **stage of disease in that patient**, not the drug.`],[`Test performance`,`**Sn = TP/(TP+FN), Sp = TN/(TN+FP)** belong to the test; **PPV, NPV** belong to the population - **PPV falls with prevalence**. **Series testing raises Sp and PPV; parallel raises Sn**. ROC + Youden index for cut-off; reliability by **kappa**.`],[`Criteria`,`**Wilson-Jungner 1968** (disease, test, treatment, programme - 10 criteria) + **Andermann 2008** (defined target, evidence, **informed choice**, equity, **quality assurance**, **benefit over harm**).`],[`Biases`,`**Lead time** (survival inflated), **length time** (slow tumours, interval cancers), **overdiagnosis**, **volunteer bias** - only an **RCT of invitation with disease-specific mortality** escapes all.`],[`India - NP-NCD`,`**PBS of everyone 30+** at Ayushman Arogya Mandir; ASHA fills **CBAC (score over 4 = priority)**; BP and **RBS** yearly; confirm at PHC; **75/25 initiative**; national NCD screening campaign Feb-Mar 2025.`],[`India - cancer screens`,`**VIA (3-5% acetic acid) women 30-65 every 5 years**, screen-and-treat (thermal ablation); **CBE** and **oral visual examination** from 30 every 5 years; WHO prefers **HPV DNA** (5-10 yearly from 30; WLHIV from 25, 3-5 yearly).`],[`Other Indian screens`,`TB symptom screen + **upfront NAAT**, CXR in TB Mukt Bharat Abhiyaan; **sickle cell (NSCAEM 2023, 7 crore aged 0-40)**; ANC **HIV, syphilis, HBsAg, DIPSI 75 g (2 h 140 mg/dL or more)**; **RBSK 4 Ds**.`],[`Family physician role`,`Write each measure as **level - intervention - deliverer**; **opportunistic screening** every visit; **close the loop** on every positive; give **informed choice**; practise **quaternary prevention**; notify on Ni-kshay/IHIP.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`**Coverage** of 30+, screen-positive rate and PPV, **follow-up completion**, control rates (BP under 140/90), stage at diagnosis, interval cancers, disease-specific mortality, QA of screeners.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Screening with no confirmatory/treatment pathway; suspicious cervix, oral lesion over 2 weeks, hard breast lump - **biopsy within the week**; **BP 180/110 or RBS over 250 with symptoms** - treat same day; symptomatic person = diagnosis, not screening.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**HPV vaccination - single dose quadrivalent for girls aged 14, national rollout 28 February 2026** (primary prevention; does not replace screening from 30).`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Health education is a **tool at every level, not a level**; same iron tablet can be primary, secondary or tertiary; **SnNout, SpPin**; Andermann earns the extra marks.`]]},flows:[{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Five levels of prevention - diabetes worked example`,steps:[{label:`Primordial - before the risk factor`,detail:`School nutrition policy, sugar tax, safe walking spaces`},{label:`Primary - risk factor, no disease`,detail:`Weight loss, 150 min/week activity in a person with family history`},{label:`Secondary - early disease`,detail:`CBAC + RBS at 30, confirm, start metformin`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Tertiary - established disease`,detail:`Annual eye and foot check, ACE inhibitor for albuminuria, rehabilitation`},{label:`Quaternary - avoid overmedicalisation`,detail:`No whole-body scans; deprescribe sulfonylurea in frail elder`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`flow`,heading:`From screen to treatment - NP-NCD pathway`,steps:[{label:`ASHA fills CBAC for everyone 30+`,detail:`Score over 4 = priority; BP and RBS checked in all`},{label:`Screen at Ayushman Arogya Mandir`,detail:`BP, RBS, OVE, CBE, VIA by CHO/ANM`},{label:`Screen positive?`,detail:`BP 140/90 or more; RBS 140 mg/dL or more; VIA acetowhite; lesion`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Confirm at PHC / district NCD clinic`,detail:`Fasting glucose or HbA1c; colposcopy, biopsy, imaging`},{label:`Lost between screen and diagnosis`,detail:`Commonest programme failure - trace via ASHA and NCD portal`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Treat by protocol and follow up`,detail:`Record on NCD portal; track control rates`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Series against parallel testing`,columns:[`Feature`,`Series (sequential)`,`Parallel (simultaneous)`],rows:[[`Rule`,`Positive only if all tests positive`,`Positive if any test positive`],[`Raises`,`Specificity and PPV`,`Sensitivity and NPV`],[`Cost`,`Small loss of sensitivity`,`More false positives`],[`Indian use`,`HIV testing strategy; TB symptom screen then NAAT`,`Multiphasic camps where cases will not return`]]}]},"preventive-national-health-programmes":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`A **national health programme** has a goal, target, standard protocol, free drugs and a reporting system.`,`The **district is the unit of implementation**; states run the programmes.`,`Vertical strategies are now delivered on **one horizontal primary care platform** under NHM.`,`**NHM** combines NRHM (2005) and NUHM (2013).`,`**National Health Policy 2017**: public health spending of 2.5% of GDP by 2025.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Communicable**: NTEP, NCVBDC, NLEP, NACP, IDSP, NRCP and UIP.`,`**Non-communicable**: NP-NCD, NMHP, NPCBVI, NPHCE, NPPC, NTCP, NPPCF and NOHP.`,`**RMNCAH+N**: JSY, JSSK, PMSMA, RBSK, RKSK and POSHAN.`,`**Health system**: NHM, Ayushman Bharat and PM-ABHIM.`,`**Control** means an acceptable level; **eradication** means global zero, as with smallpox.`,`**Elimination as a public health problem** meets a threshold, for example leprosy under 1 per 10,000.`]},{title:`The delivery platform`,points:[`IPHS 2022 norms: **ASHA 1,000, sub-centre 5,000, PHC 30,000, CHC 1,20,000**.`,`Hilly and tribal areas use lower norms: 3,000, 20,000 and 80,000.`,`Sub-centres and PHCs became **Ayushman Arogya Mandirs** (renamed 2023) with **12 service packages**.`,`The sub-centre AAM is led by a **Community Health Officer**, with e-Sanjeevani teleconsultation.`,`**PM-ABHIM** (2021) builds infrastructure: block units, district labs and critical care blocks.`,`When a programme fails, check **staff vacancy, stock-outs and the referral loop** first.`]},{title:`Communicable disease programmes`,points:[`**NTEP** pillars are **Detect, Treat, Prevent, Build**; upfront NAAT and universal drug susceptibility testing.`,`TB incidence fell from **237 to 187 per lakh** (2015 to 2024), a 21% decline.`,`**Ni-kshay Poshan Yojana** was raised to **Rs 1,000 per month** in November 2024.`,`Malaria: zero indigenous cases by **2027**, elimination by **2030**; test-treat-track strategy.`,`Kala-azar reached under 1 per 10,000 in all endemic blocks in **2023**.`,`Leprosy NSP 2023-27 aims at **zero transmission by 2027**, with single-dose rifampicin for contacts.`,`**NACP-V** targets 95-95-95, with test and treat since 2017.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Drug-sensitive TB: **2 months HRZE then 4 months HRE**, daily, by weight band.`,`Give **pyridoxine 10 mg daily** with TB treatment; the thrice-weekly regimen is abandoned.`,`TB preventive treatment: **6H** or **3HP** (12 weekly doses) after excluding active TB.`,`Drug-resistant TB: **BPaLM for 6 months** for MDR or RR-TB aged 14 and over.`,`Leprosy MDT uses three drugs: **6 months for PB and 12 months for MB**.`,`HIV first line: **TLD once daily**, started the same day regardless of CD4.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Hypertension (IHCI): start **amlodipine 5 mg** or telmisartan 40 mg once daily.`,`Step up every 4 weeks, then add **chlorthalidone 12.5 mg**; refer if still above 140/90.`,`Diabetes: **metformin 500 mg** with meals, up to 1 g twice daily, then add glimepiride.`,`Severe malaria: give **pre-referral rectal or IM artesunate** at the PHC and refer.`,`Kala-azar: **single-dose liposomal amphotericin B 10 mg/kg** IV; PKDL needs miltefosine for 12 weeks.`]},{title:`NCD, mental health and RMNCAH+N`,points:[`**NP-NCD** screens everyone 30 and over; the **75/25 initiative** targets 75 million on standard care.`,`**Tele-MANAS** helpline is **14416**; Mental Healthcare Act 2017 decriminalised attempted suicide.`,`NTCP enforces **COTPA 2003**; tobacco quitline is 1800-112-356.`,`**PMSMA** gives an assured antenatal check on the **9th of every month**.`,`**RBSK** screens 0-18 years for the 4 Ds; **RKSK** serves adolescents 10-19 years.`,`PM-JAY covers **Rs 5 lakh per family per year**; everyone aged 70 and over since October 2024.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`The family physician's five duties: **detect, treat by protocol, refer, record and notify**.`,`Notify TB on **Ni-kshay** (legal duty since 2018), leprosy on Nikusth, outbreaks on IHIP.`,`Jaundice on anti-TB drugs: **stop all hepatotoxic drugs** and refer.`,`Leprosy **grade 2 disability** or a child case means late detection and ongoing transmission.`,`NTEP treatment success target is **90% or more**; NP-NCD tracks the proportion controlled.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Programme drugs`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`HRZE fixed-dose combination`,`Oral, 2-6 tablets daily by weight band`,`2 months HRZE, then 4 months HRE`,`Hepatitis, neuropathy (H), optic neuritis (E), gout (Z)`],[`Leprosy MDT (adult)`,`R 600 + C 300 mg monthly; C 50 + D 100 mg daily`,`PB 6 months, MB 12 months`,`Skin pigmentation (C), haemolysis and DRESS (D)`],[`Single-dose rifampicin (leprosy PEP)`,`600 mg oral, 15 years and over`,`Once`,`Exclude leprosy and TB first`],[`Chloroquine plus primaquine (P. vivax)`,`CQ 25 mg/kg; PQ 0.25 mg/kg daily`,`CQ 3 days; PQ 14 days`,`No PQ in pregnancy, under 6 months, G6PD deficiency`],[`Artesunate plus SP (P. falciparum)`,`AS 4 mg/kg daily; SP on day 1`,`3 days; PQ 0.75 mg/kg day 2`,`AL used in north-east; SP rash`],[`IV artesunate (severe malaria)`,`2.4 mg/kg at 0, 12, 24 h, then daily`,`Until oral intake, then full ACT`,`Rare delayed haemolysis`],[`Liposomal amphotericin B (kala-azar)`,`10 mg/kg IV infusion`,`Single dose`,`Infusion reactions`],[`DEC plus albendazole (filaria MDA)`,`DEC 100, 200 or 300 mg by age; albendazole 400 mg`,`Single annual dose`,`Not under 2 years or in pregnancy`],[`TLD (HIV)`,`One tablet once daily`,`Lifelong`,`Start same day if ready`]]},{heading:`Facility norms (IPHS 2022)`,columns:[`Facility`,`Plain areas`,`Hilly or tribal`,`Key staff`],rows:[[`ASHA`,`1,000`,`Habitation-based`,`Incentive-based link worker`],[`Sub-centre AAM`,`5,000`,`3,000`,`CHO, ANM, male health worker`],[`PHC AAM`,`30,000`,`20,000`,`Medical officer, nurses, pharmacist, lab technician`],[`CHC (FRU)`,`1,20,000`,`80,000`,`Four specialists, 30 beds`]]},{heading:`Key programmes with targets`,columns:[`Programme`,`Target`,`Key indicator`],rows:[[`NTEP`,`End TB (SDG 2030)`,`Treatment success 90% or more`],[`NCVBDC malaria`,`Elimination by 2030`,`Annual parasite incidence, ABER`],[`NLEP`,`Zero transmission by 2027`,`Grade 2 disability per million`],[`NACP-V`,`95-95-95`,`Viral suppression on ART`],[`NP-NCD`,`25% fall in premature NCD deaths`,`Proportion with BP controlled`]]}],remember:[`Facility ladder: **1,000 - 5,000 - 30,000 - 1,20,000** (3,000, 20,000, 80,000 in hilly areas).`,`Ayushman Bharat: **AAM (12 packages) plus PM-JAY (Rs 5 lakh)**; PM-ABHIM is infrastructure.`,`Ni-kshay Poshan is now **Rs 1,000 a month**; BPaLM is 6 months.`,`Leprosy MDT: three drugs for both; **PB 6, MB 12 months**.`,`Five duties: **detect, treat, refer, record, notify**.`,`End every programme answer with **indicators and the portal**.`]},updated:[`WHO Global TB Report 2025: India TB incidence 187 per lakh in 2024 (237 in 2015, a 21% fall); mortality 28 to 21 per lakh; about 26.2 lakh diagnosed in 2024 - replaces 195 per lakh (2023)`,`SRS 2021-23 / SRS 2023: MMR 88 per lakh live births, IMR 25, U5MR 29 - replaces MMR 93 and IMR 27`,`NFHS-6 (2023-24, fact sheet May 2026): institutional delivery 90.6% - replaces 88.6% (NFHS-5)`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - National health programmes`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`A **national health programme** is a centrally designed, funded strategy with goal, target, **standard protocol, free drugs and diagnostics** and a reporting system; **district is the unit of implementation**; delivered on one horizontal NHM platform.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Communicable** (NTEP, NCVBDC, NLEP, NACP, IDSP, NRCP, UIP); **NCD** (NP-NCD, NMHP, NPCBVI, NPHCE, NPPC, NTCP, NPPCF, NOHP); **RMNCAH+N** (JSY, JSSK, PMSMA, RBSK, RKSK, POSHAN); **systems** (NHM, Ayushman Bharat, PM-ABHIM).`],[`Targets vocabulary`,`**Control** (acceptable level) -> **elimination as a public health problem** (leprosy under 1/10,000) -> **elimination of transmission** (zero local cases) -> **eradication** (global zero - smallpox).`],[`Platform (IPHS 2022)`,`**ASHA 1,000; SC 5,000/3,000; PHC 30,000/20,000; CHC 1,20,000/80,000**; AAM with **12 packages**; CHO at SC-AAM; Free Drugs and Diagnostics; PM-ABHIM = infrastructure arm.`],[`NTEP`,`**Detect-Treat-Prevent-Build**; upfront **NAAT**, universal DST; **2HRZE/4HRE daily** weight bands; **Ni-kshay notification mandatory (2018)**; Ni-kshay Poshan **Rs 1,000/month**; **BPaLM 6 months** for DR-TB; incidence **187/lakh (2024), down 21% since 2015**.`],[`NCVBDC`,`Malaria **NFME 2016-30**: zero indigenous **2027**, elimination **2030**; exited WHO HBHI 2024; kala-azar threshold met 2023 (under 1/10,000 per block); **LF elimination 2027** via DA/IDA MDA.`],[`NLEP and NACP`,`Leprosy NSP 2023-27 - **zero transmission 2027**, **SDR-PEP** for contacts, Nikusth 2.0; MDT 3 drugs PB 6, MB 12 months. NACP-V - **95-95-95**, test and treat, **TLD**.`],[`Key regimens`,`**Pv: CQ 25 mg/kg over 3 days + PQ 0.25 mg/kg x 14 days**; **Pf: ACT-SP** (AL in north-east) + PQ 0.75 mg/kg single; severe - **IV artesunate 2.4 mg/kg 0, 12, 24 h**; kala-azar **L-AmB 10 mg/kg single**; LF MDA **DEC + albendazole 400 mg (+ ivermectin)**.`],[`NCD protocols`,`**IHCI: amlodipine 5 mg -> 10 mg + telmisartan -> add chlorthalidone 12.5 mg**; diabetes **metformin 500 mg -> 1 g BD, add glimepiride, then insulin**; NP-NCD screens all **30+**; Tele-MANAS **14416**.`],[`RMNCAH+N and AB`,`JSY 2005, JSSK 2011, **PMSMA 9th of month**, SUMAN 2019; RBSK 0-18 (4 Ds), RKSK 10-19; **PM-JAY Rs 5 lakh/family/year**, **70+ covered since October 2024**.`],[`Outcome figures to quote`,`**MMR 88 (SRS 2021-23)**, **IMR 25, U5MR 29 (SRS 2023)**, institutional delivery **90.6% (NFHS-6)**; SDG: MMR under 70, NMR 12 or less by 2030.`],[`Family physician role`,`**Detect, treat by protocol, refer, record, notify** - Ni-kshay, Nikusth, IHIP same day; link to benefits (Ni-kshay Poshan, PM-JAY, JSY); close two-way referral; supervise ASHA/ANM/CHO.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Portals: **Ni-kshay, Nikusth 2.0, IHIP, NCD portal, U-WIN, RCH, HMIS**. NTEP **treatment success 90% or more**; NLEP **G2D per million**; malaria **API, ABER 10% or more**; NP-NCD **proportion controlled**.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Un-notified TB; AFP under 15, measles, diphtheria, cholera cluster - IHIP same day; child or G2D leprosy; severe malaria - pre-referral artesunate; jaundice on ATT - stop and refer.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**WHO GTR 2025: India incidence 187/lakh (2024)**; **SRS 2021-23 MMR 88**; **NFHS-6 institutional births 90.6%**.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Grade 2 disability rate = late detection; treatment success = NTEP quality; always end with **indicators and the reporting portal**.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Presumptive TB in the OPD - NTEP pathway`,steps:[{label:`Cough 2 weeks+, fever, weight loss, night sweats`,detail:`Or abnormal CXR, contact, PLHIV`},{label:`Upfront NAAT (CBNAAT/TrueNat)`,detail:`Universal DST for rifampicin`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Rifampicin sensitive`,detail:`2HRZE/4HRE daily by weight band + pyridoxine 10 mg`,tone:`good`},{label:`Rifampicin resistant`,detail:`BPaLM 6 months (14 years and over) at DR-TB centre`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Notify on Ni-kshay the same day`,detail:`Ni-kshay Poshan Rs 1,000/month; TPT for contacts`}]},{kind:`branch`,heading:`Malaria treatment by species - NCVBDC`,root:`RDT or smear positive malaria`,arms:[{label:`P. vivax`,steps:[`Chloroquine 25 mg/kg over 3 days (10, 10, 5)`,`Primaquine 0.25 mg/kg daily x 14 days`,`No PQ in pregnancy, infant under 6 months, G6PD deficiency`]},{label:`P. falciparum`,tone:`warn`,steps:[`ACT-SP: artesunate 4 mg/kg x 3 days + SP day 1`,`North-east: artemether-lumefantrine BD x 3 days`,`Primaquine 0.75 mg/kg single dose day 2`]},{label:`Severe malaria`,tone:`warn`,steps:[`IV artesunate 2.4 mg/kg at 0, 12, 24 h then daily`,`PHC: pre-referral rectal or IM artesunate`,`Complete full oral ACT course`]}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Programme - target - indicator to quote`,columns:[`Programme`,`Target`,`Indicator`],rows:[[`NTEP`,`End TB (SDG 2030)`,`Incidence 187/lakh (2024); success 90%+`],[`NCVBDC malaria`,`Zero indigenous 2027, elimination 2030`,`API, ABER 10%+`],[`NLEP`,`Zero transmission 2027`,`G2D per million, child proportion`],[`NACP-V`,`95-95-95`,`Viral suppression on ART`],[`NP-NCD`,`25% fall premature NCD death`,`Proportion controlled`]]}]},"preventive-immunisation":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Active immunity**: the vaccine makes the person's own antibody and memory; slow onset, long lasting.`,`**Passive immunity**: ready-made antibody such as immunoglobulin; immediate but short lived.`,`The **UIP** gives free vaccines against **12 diseases**; EPI 1978 became UIP in 1985.`,`Herd immunity threshold is **1 - 1/R0**; measles needs 92-95% coverage.`,`**Tetanus has no herd immunity** because its spores live in the environment.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Live attenuated**: BCG, OPV, MR, rotavirus, live JE and yellow fever.`,`**Inactivated**: IPV and inactivated JE; **toxoids**: tetanus and diphtheria.`,`**Subunit or recombinant**: hepatitis B and HPV; **conjugate**: Hib and PCV.`,`Live vaccines are avoided in **pregnancy and severe immunosuppression**.`,`Conjugate vaccines recruit T cells, so they **work under 2 years** and give memory.`]},{title:`How vaccines work`,points:[`The first dose gives a slow **primary response**, mainly IgM.`,`Later doses trigger a fast, high-titre **secondary (anamnestic) response** from memory cells.`,`Keep a **minimum 4-week interval** between primary doses.`,`An interrupted schedule is **resumed, never restarted**.`,`Measles starts at **9 months** because maternal antibody would neutralise it earlier.`,`OPV gives **gut IgA** and blocks transmission; IPV gives serum immunity against paralysis.`,`Live vaccines not given the same day need a **4-week gap**.`]},{title:`Schedule and latest changes`,points:[`Visits: **birth, 6, 10, 14 weeks, 9-12 months, 16-24 months, 5-6 years, 10 and 16 years**.`,`A **third fIPV dose at 9-12 months** was added from January 2023.`,`PCV follows a **2+1 schedule**: 6 weeks, 14 weeks and 9-12 months.`,`**Single-dose quadrivalent HPV vaccine** for girls aged 14 launched nationally on 28 February 2026.`,`**U-WIN** records every dose; **eVIN** tracks stock and cold chain temperature.`,`Full immunisation rose from **62% to 76.4%** (NFHS-4 to NFHS-5).`,`India was certified **polio-free in March 2014**; measles-rubella elimination target is 2026.`]},{title:`Cold chain`,points:[`ILR keeps vaccines at **+2 to +8 degrees C**; deep freezer at -15 to -25 makes ice packs.`,`**OPV is the most heat-sensitive**; BCG and MR are also light-sensitive.`,`**Adsorbed vaccines** (HepB, Penta, DPT, Td, PCV, IPV) are destroyed by freezing.`,`**VVM**: use at stages 1-2, discard at stages 3-4; it shows heat, not freezing.`,`The **shake test** detects freezing by comparing sedimentation with a frozen control.`,`**Open vial policy**: 28 days for OPV, HepB, Penta, Td, DPT, PCV and fIPV.`,`Reconstituted **BCG, MR and JE** are discarded after **4 hours** or at session end.`]},{title:`AEFI and contraindications`,points:[`**AEFI** is any untoward event after immunisation, not necessarily caused by the vaccine.`,`WHO causes: product, quality defect, **immunisation error**, anxiety and coincidental.`,`Report a serious AEFI to the District Immunisation Officer **within 24 hours**.`,`PCIF within **10 days**; FCIF within **70 days**.`,`A cluster at one session is an **immunisation error until proved otherwise**.`,`True contraindication: **anaphylaxis to a previous dose**; encephalopathy within 7 days of pertussis vaccine.`,`Minor illness, malnutrition and prematurity are **false contraindications**.`]},{title:`Adult and post-exposure vaccines`,points:[`**Influenza** yearly for pregnant women, 65 and over, chronic disease and health workers.`,`Pneumococcal for all **50 and over**: PCV20 or PCV21 once, or PCV15 then PPSV23.`,`Hepatitis B at **0, 1 and 6 months**; anti-HBs 10 mIU/mL or more is protective.`,`Tetanus-prone wound: Td if last dose over 5 years; add **HTIG 250 IU** if course incomplete.`,`Rabies category III: wash, vaccinate and give **RIG** into the wound.`,`**Yellow fever**: one dose is valid for life; meningococcal ACWY required for Hajj.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`**Check the card at every visit**; missed opportunities are the commonest cause of drop-out.`,`Drop-out rate is **(BCG - MR-1) / BCG x 100**; under 10% is acceptable.`,`Notify AFP under 15 years and fever with rash on **IHIP** immediately.`,`Observe every child for **30 minutes** after vaccination.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Vaccines and immunobiologicals`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`BCG`,`0.05 mL ID left upper arm (0.1 mL after 1 month)`,`Once, birth up to 1 year`,`Ulcer and scar normal; avoid in symptomatic HIV`],[`OPV`,`2 drops oral`,`Birth, 6, 10, 14 weeks, booster`,`Most heat-sensitive; rare VAPP`],[`Hepatitis B birth dose`,`0.5 mL IM thigh`,`Within 24 hours of birth`,`Never freeze`],[`Pentavalent`,`0.5 mL IM left thigh`,`6, 10, 14 weeks`,`Fever, pain; never freeze`],[`fIPV`,`0.1 mL ID right upper arm`,`6 weeks, 14 weeks, 9-12 months`,`Local nodule`],[`PCV`,`0.5 mL IM right thigh`,`6 weeks, 14 weeks, 9-12 months`,`Never freeze`],[`Measles-rubella`,`0.5 mL SC right upper arm`,`9-12 and 16-24 months`,`Fever and rash at 5-12 days; discard at 4 h`],[`HPV (quadrivalent)`,`0.5 mL IM deltoid`,`Single dose, girls aged 14`,`Does not replace screening from 30`],[`Rabies vaccine plus RIG`,`ID 0.1 mL at 2 sites; HRIG 20 IU/kg`,`Days 0, 3, 7, 28`,`Wash wound 15 minutes first`],[`Adrenaline (anaphylaxis)`,`1:1000, 0.01 mL/kg IM thigh`,`Repeat every 5-15 minutes`,`Max 0.5 mL adult, 0.3 mL child`]]},{heading:`National Immunisation Schedule by visit`,columns:[`Age`,`Vaccines`],rows:[[`Birth`,`BCG, OPV-0, hepatitis B birth dose`],[`6 weeks`,`Penta-1, OPV-1, RVV-1, fIPV-1, PCV-1`],[`10 weeks`,`Penta-2, OPV-2, RVV-2`],[`14 weeks`,`Penta-3, OPV-3, RVV-3, fIPV-2, PCV-2`],[`9-12 months`,`MR-1, JE-1, PCV booster, fIPV-3, vitamin A`],[`16-24 months`,`MR-2, JE-2, DPT booster-1, OPV booster`],[`5-6 years`,`DPT booster-2`],[`10 and 16 years`,`Td`],[`14 years (girls)`,`HPV, single dose`],[`Pregnancy`,`Td-1 and Td-2 four weeks apart, or booster`]]},{heading:`Rabies exposure categories (WHO)`,columns:[`Category`,`Exposure`,`Action`],rows:[[`I`,`Touching, licks on intact skin`,`Wash only`],[`II`,`Nibbling, minor scratch without bleeding`,`Wash and vaccinate`],[`III`,`Bleeding bite, licks on broken skin, bat contact`,`Wash, vaccinate and give RIG`]]}],remember:[`**Left arm BCG, right arm fIPV and MR**; left thigh Penta, right thigh PCV.`,`**VVM shows heat, shake test shows freezing.**`,`Open vial 28 days, but **BCG, MR, JE discarded at 4 hours**.`,`Never restart a schedule; **resume** where it stopped.`,`Herd immunity = **1 - 1/R0**; tetanus has none.`,`Category III bite without RIG is a **fatal omission**.`]},updated:[`MoHFW 2026: national single-dose quadrivalent HPV vaccination (Gardasil-4, 0.5 mL IM) for girls aged 14 from 28 February 2026, registered on U-WIN - added to schedule table and pearls`,`ACIP 2025: pneumococcal for all adults 50 and over (and high-risk 19-49) - PCV20 or PCV21 once, or PCV15 then PPSV23; PCV13 no longer used in adults`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Immunisation (UIP, cold chain, AEFI)`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Active** (own antibodies and memory - slow, long) vs **passive** (ready-made Ig/antivenom - immediate, short). **Herd immunity threshold = 1 - 1/R0** - measles 92-95%; **tetanus has none**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Live** (BCG, OPV, MR, rotavirus, JE SA 14-14-2, yellow fever); **inactivated** (IPV); **toxoid** (T, D); **subunit/recombinant** (HepB, HPV); **conjugate** (Hib, PCV - work under 2 years).`],[`UIP facts`,`EPI 1978 -> UIP 1985; **12 diseases**; additions: IPV 2015, rota 2016/19, MR 2017, **PCV 2021**, Td 2018-19, **fIPV-3 at 9 months (2023)**, **HPV girls 14 (Feb 2026)**; polio-free **March 2014**; MR elimination target 2026.`],[`Schedule`,`**Birth** BCG, OPV-0, HepB; **6-10-14 weeks** Penta, OPV, RVV (+ fIPV and PCV at 6 and 14); **9-12 m** MR-1, JE-1, PCV booster, fIPV-3, vit A; **16-24 m** MR-2, JE-2, DPT-B1, OPV-B; **5-6 y** DPT-B2; **10, 16 y** Td; **14 y girls** HPV.`],[`Dose, route, site`,`**BCG 0.05 mL ID left arm** (0.1 mL after 1 month); **fIPV 0.1 mL ID right arm**; **MR 0.5 mL SC right arm**; **Penta left thigh, PCV right thigh 0.5 mL IM**; HepB birth 0.5 mL IM within 24 h; OPV 2 drops; **HPV 0.5 mL IM single dose**.`],[`Concepts`,`Minimum interval **4 weeks**; **resume, never restart**; start 6 weeks (maternal IgG), MR at 9 months; live vaccines same day or **4 weeks apart**, defer **3 months after Ig/blood**; OPV gut IgA, IPV serum IgG.`],[`Cold chain`,`ILR **+2 to +8 C**, deep freezer **-15 to -25 C**; **freeze-sensitive: HepB, Penta, DPT, Td, PCV, IPV**; OPV most heat-sensitive; **VVM 1-2 use, 3-4 discard**; **shake test** for freezing; carrier with 4 conditioned ice packs.`],[`Open vial and 4-hour rule`,`**Open vial 28 days**: OPV, HepB, Penta, Td, DPT, PCV, fIPV, liquid rota; **BCG, MR, JE discarded 4 h after reconstitution** or session end.`],[`AEFI`,`WHO causes: **product, quality defect, immunisation error, anxiety, coincidental**; serious AEFI: CRF to DIO **within 24 h**, **PCIF 10 days**, **FCIF 70 days**; **cluster = immunisation error until proved otherwise**.`],[`Anaphylaxis`,`Lay flat, legs up, **adrenaline 1:1000 0.01 mL/kg IM thigh (max 0.5 mL adult, 0.3 mL child), repeat 5-15 min**; oxygen, fluids, refer; observe all for **30 min**.`],[`Contraindications`,`True: **anaphylaxis to prior dose**, live vaccines in severe immunodeficiency/pregnancy, **encephalopathy within 7 days of pertussis** (give DT/Td). False: mild illness, malnutrition, prematurity, antibiotics, breastfeeding.`],[`Adult and PEP`,`Influenza yearly (pregnancy, 65+, chronic disease); **pneumococcal 50+: PCV20 or PCV21 once, or PCV15 then PPSV23** (ACIP 2025); HepB 0, 1, 6 with anti-HBs 10 mIU/mL; RZV 50+; tetanus-prone wound Td if over 5 years + HTIG 250 IU if incomplete.`],[`Rabies`,`Cat I wash; II wash + vaccine; **III wash + vaccine + RIG** (HRIG 20 IU/kg, ERIG 40 IU/kg or mAb into wound); **ID 0.1 mL x 2 sites days 0, 3, 7, 28** or IM 0, 3, 7, 14, 28; wash 15 min.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Penta-3, MR-1, **FIC**, drop-out **(BCG - MR1)/BCG under 10%**, wastage, AEFI reporting rate, VPD incidence; **U-WIN** due lists, **eVIN** stock and temperature.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Anaphylaxis; AEFI cluster; death/hospitalisation after vaccine; VVM 3-4 or frozen adsorbed vaccine; AFP under 15 or fever with rash - notify IHIP; Cat III bite without RIG.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**HPV single dose for girls 14 (28 February 2026, Gardasil-4)**; **ACIP 2025 adult pneumococcal: PCV20/PCV21 alone, or PCV15 + PPSV23, from 50 years**.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Left arm BCG, right arm fIPV and MR; VVM = heat, shake test = freezing; yellow fever one dose valid for life; Hajj needs MenACWY.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Is this vial usable? Session-site check`,steps:[{label:`Check label and expiry date`,detail:`Expired - discard`},{label:`Read the VVM`,detail:`Stage 1-2 use; stage 3-4 (square as dark as circle) discard`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Freeze-sensitive and suspected frozen?`,detail:`Shake test against frozen control - fast flaky sediment = discard`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Reconstituted BCG, MR or JE?`,detail:`Discard 4 h after reconstitution or at session end`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Opened liquid multidose vial`,detail:`Usable 28 days if VVM ok, kept +2 to +8 C, septum dry`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`flow`,heading:`Serious AEFI - response and reporting`,steps:[{label:`Recognise and treat`,detail:`Anaphylaxis: adrenaline 0.01 mL/kg 1:1000 IM thigh, repeat 5-15 min`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Seal vials, suspend the lot`,detail:`Cluster = immunisation error until proved otherwise`},{label:`Case reporting form within 24 h`,detail:`To MO and District Immunisation Officer`},{label:`Investigate`,detail:`Preliminary CIF within 10 days, final CIF within 70 days`},{label:`Causality by district/state AEFI committee`,detail:`Product, quality, error, anxiety or coincidental`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Rabies exposure category and action`,columns:[`Category`,`Exposure`,`Action`],rows:[[`I`,`Touch, feeding, lick on intact skin`,`Wash only - no PEP`],[`II`,`Nibbling, minor scratch without bleeding`,`Wash 15 min + vaccine (ID days 0, 3, 7, 28)`],[`III`,`Bleeding bite/scratch, lick on broken skin or mucosa, bat contact`,`Wash + vaccine + RIG infiltrated into wound`],[`Previously vaccinated`,`Any category II or III`,`Two boosters days 0 and 3; no RIG`]]}]},"preventive-biostatistics":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Biostatistics** applies statistical methods to biological and medical data.`,`**Descriptive statistics** summarise data; **inferential statistics** generalise from sample to population.`,`The **type of data** decides the summary, the graph and the statistical test.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Qualitative**: nominal (blood group) or ordinal (pain grade, NYHA class).`,`**Quantitative**: discrete (number of children) or continuous (BP, Hb).`,`**Parametric tests** assume a normal distribution: t test, ANOVA, Pearson.`,`**Non-parametric tests** use ranks for skewed or ordinal data: Mann-Whitney, Wilcoxon, Spearman.`]},{title:`Describing data`,points:[`**Mean** for symmetrical data, **median** for skewed data, **mode** for nominal data.`,`In a normal curve, mean, median and mode **coincide**.`,`Positive skew: mean is greater than median, which is greater than mode.`,`Mean plus or minus **1, 2 and 3 SD** covers 68%, 95% and 99.7%.`,`**Standard error = SD divided by square root of n**; it shrinks as n grows.`,`**95% CI = mean plus or minus 1.96 SE**; it shows size and precision.`,`**Coefficient of variation = SD / mean x 100** compares variability across units.`]},{title:`Diagnostic test indices`,points:[`Set the 2x2 table with **disease across the top, test down the side**.`,`**Sensitivity = a/(a+c)**; **specificity = d/(b+d)**; read down the columns.`,`**PPV = a/(a+b)**; **NPV = d/(c+d)**; read across the rows.`,`As prevalence falls, **PPV falls** and NPV rises.`,`At 1% prevalence, a 90% sensitive and specific test has a **PPV of about 8%**.`,`**Likelihood ratios** do not change with prevalence; LR+ over 10 is decisive.`,`**Post-test odds = pre-test odds x LR** (Fagan nomogram).`]},{title:`Risk and effect measures`,points:[`**Relative risk = Ie / Iu**, used in cohort studies and trials.`,`**Odds ratio = ad/bc**, used in case-control studies.`,`OR approximates RR only when the outcome is **rare, under about 10%**.`,`**NNT = 1 / ARR**, rounded up, always quoted with the follow-up period.`,`Events falling from 10% to 6% give ARR 4%, RRR 40%, **NNT 25**.`,`**Population attributable risk** depends on how common the exposure is.`]},{title:`Hypothesis testing`,points:[`The **p value** is the chance of the result if the null were true.`,`p is **not** the probability that the null hypothesis is true.`,`**Type I error (alpha)** is a false positive claim, set at 0.05.`,`**Type II error (beta)** misses a real difference; **power = 1 - beta**, usually 80-90%.`,`A 95% CI that **includes 1 for a ratio**, or 0 for a difference, is not significant.`,`Prevalence survey sample size: **n = 4pq / L squared**, then add non-response and design effect.`]},{title:`Sampling`,points:[`**Simple random, systematic, stratified, cluster and multistage** are probability methods.`,`The WHO **30 x 7 cluster survey** estimates immunisation coverage with 210 children.`,`Convenience, purposive, quota and snowball samples **cannot be generalised**.`,`Sampling error shrinks with size; **bias does not**.`]},{title:`Pitfalls in practice`,points:[`A test validated in a specialist centre **loses PPV in primary care**.`,`Demand **absolute figures and NNT**, not relative risk reduction alone.`,`Explain risk to patients in **natural frequencies**, such as 4 in 100.`,`Confusing **SD with SE** is the commonest thesis error.`,`An underpowered negative study **proves nothing**.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Key formulas`,columns:[`Measure`,`Formula`,`Used in`,`Meaning`],rows:[[`Sensitivity`,`a/(a+c)`,`Diagnostic test`,`True positives found among the diseased`],[`Specificity`,`d/(b+d)`,`Diagnostic test`,`True negatives among the healthy`],[`Relative risk`,`Ie / Iu`,`Cohort, RCT`,`1 means no effect`],[`Odds ratio`,`ad/bc`,`Case-control`,`Approximates RR if outcome rare`],[`NNT`,`1 / ARR`,`RCT`,`Treated to prevent one event`],[`LR+`,`Sn / (1 - Sp)`,`Diagnostic test`,`Over 10 is decisive`],[`Youden index`,`Sn + Sp - 1`,`Cut-off choice`,`Best balance on ROC`]]},{heading:`Choosing a statistical test`,columns:[`Outcome data`,`Two independent groups`,`Two paired groups`,`Three or more groups`],rows:[[`Continuous, normal`,`Unpaired t test`,`Paired t test`,`One-way ANOVA`],[`Skewed or ordinal`,`Mann-Whitney U`,`Wilcoxon signed-rank`,`Kruskal-Wallis`],[`Proportions`,`Chi-square (Fisher if cell under 5)`,`McNemar`,`Chi-square r x c`],[`Time to event`,`Log-rank test`,`Not applicable`,`Cox regression`]]},{heading:`Which graph for which data`,columns:[`Data`,`Graph`],rows:[[`Categorical`,`Bar chart, pie chart`],[`Continuous`,`Histogram, frequency polygon`],[`Trend over time`,`Line graph`],[`Two continuous variables`,`Scatter diagram`],[`Median and IQR`,`Box plot`]]}],remember:[`**Data type first**: it decides summary, graph and test.`,`**SnNout, SpPin**; likelihood ratios travel between populations.`,`NNT = 1 / ARR, rounded up, **with the time period**.`,`1.96 SD covers individuals; **1.96 SE gives the CI of the mean**.`,`CI crossing **1 (ratios) or 0 (differences)** is non-significant.`,`**Fisher's exact** when an expected cell is under 5; McNemar for paired proportions.`]},master:{heading:`Master revision table - Biostatistics for the paper`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Biostatistics** applies statistical methods to biological data - **descriptive** (summarise) and **inferential** (sample -> population).`],[`Classification of data`,`**Qualitative**: nominal (blood group), ordinal (NYHA); **quantitative**: discrete (children), continuous (BP, Hb). Data type decides **summary, graph and test**.`],[`Central tendency and spread`,`Mean (symmetrical), **median (skewed)**, mode (nominal). Positive skew: **mean > median > mode**. Mean +/- SD; median + IQR; **CV = SD/mean x 100**.`],[`Normal curve`,`**+/-1 SD 68%, +/-1.96 SD 95%, +/-3 SD 99.7%**; **SE = SD/root n**; **95% CI = mean +/- 1.96 SE**; **Z = (x - mean)/SD** (growth standards).`],[`2x2 table`,`Disease across, test down: **Sn = a/(a+c), Sp = d/(b+d)** (columns, test property); **PPV = a/(a+b), NPV = d/(c+d)** (rows, prevalence-dependent).`],[`Likelihood ratios`,`**LR+ = Sn/(1 - Sp); LR- = (1 - Sn)/Sp**; LR+ over 10 or LR- under 0.1 decisive; **post-test odds = pre-test odds x LR**; ROC AUC 0.5 useless, over 0.8 good.`],[`Risk measures`,`**RR = Ie/Iu** (cohort, RCT); **AR = Ie - Iu**; **OR = ad/bc** (case-control, approximates RR if outcome under 10%); **PAR** for planners.`],[`Trial effect measures`,`**ARR = CER - EER; RRR = ARR/CER; NNT = 1/ARR** rounded up with time; NNH = 1/ARI. 10% -> 6%: ARR 4%, RRR 40%, **NNT 25**.`],[`Hypothesis testing`,`**p** = probability of data at least this extreme **if null true**; **alpha 0.05** (type I, false positive); **beta 0.1-0.2** (type II); **power = 1 - beta (80-90%)**; CI including 1 (ratio) or 0 (difference) = not significant.`],[`Sample size`,`Prevalence survey **n = 4pq/L squared**, + 10-15% non-response, x **design effect (2)** for clusters; WHO **30 x 7 cluster survey** for immunisation coverage.`],[`Choosing a test`,`2 means **unpaired t** (Mann-Whitney); paired **paired t** (Wilcoxon); 3+ **ANOVA** (Kruskal-Wallis); proportions **chi-square** (**Fisher if expected cell under 5**, McNemar paired); correlation Pearson/Spearman.`],[`Regression and survival`,`Linear (continuous), **logistic (adjusted OR)**, **Cox (hazard ratio)**; **Kaplan-Meier** curve compared by **log-rank**.`],[`Sampling`,`Probability: **simple random, systematic, stratified, cluster, multistage (NFHS)**; non-probability cannot be generalised; **bias not reduced by larger sample**.`],[`Family physician use`,`Ask **LRs and local prevalence** before using a test; demand **ARR, NNT, CI, ITT**; explain risk in **natural frequencies**; teach-back.`],[`Red flags in a paper`,`PPV quoted without prevalence; RRR only; underpowered negative trial; OR read as RR with common outcome; post-hoc subgroups; SD and SE confused.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`No change needed - **CONSORT 2025 / SPIRIT 2025** already cited.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Sn and Sp belong to the test, PV to the population, LRs travel; 1.96 SD = 95% of individuals, 1.96 SE = 95% CI of the mean.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Choosing the statistical test`,steps:[{label:`What type is the outcome?`,detail:`Categorical, continuous, or time-to-event`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Categorical (proportions)`,detail:`Chi-square; Fisher exact if expected cell under 5; McNemar if paired`},{label:`Continuous - normal?`,detail:`Yes: t test (unpaired/paired) or ANOVA for 3+ groups`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Continuous - skewed or ordinal`,detail:`Mann-Whitney, Wilcoxon signed-rank, Kruskal-Wallis`},{label:`Time to event`,detail:`Kaplan-Meier + log-rank; Cox regression for hazard ratio`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`flow`,heading:`From a trial result to NNT`,steps:[{label:`Control event rate (CER)`,detail:`e.g. 10% strokes over 5 years`},{label:`Experimental event rate (EER)`,detail:`e.g. 6%`},{label:`ARR = CER - EER`,detail:`4% - always quote beside RRR (40%)`},{label:`NNT = 1/ARR, round up`,detail:`25 treated for 5 years to prevent one stroke`,tone:`good`},{label:`Same RRR on 1% baseline`,detail:`NNT 250 - relative figures flatter a drug`,tone:`warn`}]}]},"preventive-study-designs":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Epidemiology** studies the distribution and determinants of health states in populations.`,`Designs are classified by **whether the investigator assigns the exposure**.`,`Descriptive studies **generate hypotheses**; analytical studies **test them**.`,`Hierarchy runs from meta-analysis of RCTs down to expert opinion; **GRADE** rates quality, not just design.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Descriptive**: case report, case series, cross-sectional survey and ecological study.`,`**Analytical**: case-control (outcome to exposure) and cohort (exposure to outcome).`,`**Experimental**: RCT, field trial (healthy individuals) and community trial (whole communities).`,`Cohorts may be **prospective, retrospective or ambidirectional**.`]},{title:`Measures of disease frequency`,points:[`**Incidence**: new cases in a period over population at risk; used for aetiology.`,`**Prevalence**: all existing cases over population; used for planning services.`,`**P = I x D**: ART and insulin raised prevalence without raising incidence.`,`**Incidence density** uses person-time as the denominator.`,`**Secondary attack rate** excludes the primary case and measures infectiousness.`,`**Case fatality rate** measures killing power; proportional mortality only ranks causes.`]},{title:`Descriptive and cross-sectional studies`,points:[`Describe disease by **time, place and person**.`,`Time trends: short-term, **cyclic**, **seasonal** and **secular**.`,`**Migrant studies** separate genetic from environmental causes.`,`A **cross-sectional study** gives prevalence but **cannot show temporality**.`,`**Ecological fallacy**: assuming a group association holds for each person.`]},{title:`Case-control studies`,points:[`Start with cases and controls; look **back** for exposure.`,`Best for **rare diseases with long latency** and many exposures.`,`Measure is the **odds ratio (ad/bc)**; incidence and RR cannot be calculated.`,`Signature bias is **recall bias**; reduce it with blinded interviewers.`,`**Berkson bias** comes from hospital controls; Neyman bias from prevalent survivors.`,`Classic example: **Doll and Hill 1950**, smoking and lung cancer.`]},{title:`Cohort studies`,points:[`Start with exposed and unexposed people **free of disease**; follow forward.`,`Gives **incidence, relative risk and attributable risk** directly.`,`Best for **rare exposures** and several outcomes; temporality is secure.`,`Signature bias is **loss to follow-up**; over 20% loss makes results doubtful.`,`**Healthy worker effect** understates occupational hazards.`,`Classic examples: **Framingham 1948** and the British Doctors Study.`]},{title:`Randomised controlled trials`,points:[`Randomisation controls **known and unknown confounders**.`,`**Allocation concealment** prevents bias before entry; **blinding** prevents bias after entry.`,`**Intention-to-treat** keeps everyone in their randomised arm and preserves randomisation.`,`Field trial example: **Salk polio vaccine 1954**; community trial: Newburgh-Kingston fluoridation.`,`Phases: I safety, II efficacy, III large comparison before licence, IV post-marketing.`,`India: **CTRI registration mandatory since 2009**; serious adverse events reported within 24 hours.`]},{title:`Bias, confounding and causation`,points:[`**Bias** is systematic error and is **not reduced by a larger sample**.`,`A **confounder** links to exposure and outcome but is not on the causal pathway.`,`Control confounding by randomisation, restriction, matching, stratification or regression.`,`**Effect modification** is reported stratum by stratum, never adjusted away.`,`**Bradford Hill**: nine viewpoints; **temporality is the only essential one**.`,`Reporting: **CONSORT** (RCT), STROBE (observational), STARD (diagnostic), PRISMA (reviews).`]}],tables:[{heading:`Case-control, cohort and RCT compared`,columns:[`Feature`,`Case-control`,`Cohort`,`RCT`],rows:[[`Starting point`,`Outcome`,`Exposure`,`Allocated intervention`],[`Measure`,`Odds ratio`,`Incidence, RR, AR`,`RR, ARR, NNT`],[`Best for`,`Rare disease, long latency`,`Rare exposure, many outcomes`,`Efficacy of one intervention`],[`Signature bias`,`Recall, Berkson`,`Loss to follow-up`,`Bias if unblinded`],[`Cost and time`,`Low, quick`,`High, long`,`Highest`],[`Classic example`,`Doll and Hill 1950`,`Framingham 1948`,`Salk trial 1954`]]},{heading:`Measures of disease frequency`,columns:[`Measure`,`Numerator`,`Denominator`,`Use`],rows:[[`Incidence`,`New cases in a period`,`Population at risk`,`Aetiology`],[`Point prevalence`,`All cases at one time`,`Population at that time`,`Planning`],[`Incidence density`,`New cases`,`Person-time at risk`,`Unequal follow-up`],[`Secondary attack rate`,`Cases among contacts`,`Susceptible contacts, primary excluded`,`Infectiousness`],[`Case fatality rate`,`Deaths from the disease`,`Cases of the disease`,`Virulence`]]},{heading:`Which design for which question`,columns:[`Question`,`Design`],rows:[[`How common is it?`,`Cross-sectional`],[`Cause of a rare disease?`,`Case-control`],[`Effects of a rare exposure?`,`Cohort`],[`Does a treatment work?`,`RCT`],[`Does a programme work?`,`Cluster or community trial`]]}],remember:[`Incidence for aetiology, **prevalence for planning**, P = I x D.`,`Case-control gives **OR**; cohort gives **RR**; only an RCT allocates exposure.`,`Concealment before entry, **blinding after entry**.`,`Bigger samples do not cure **bias**.`,`**Temporality** is the only essential Bradford Hill viewpoint.`,`Exclude the primary case from **both parts** of the secondary attack rate.`]},master:{heading:`Master revision table - Study designs and disease frequency`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Epidemiology** = distribution and determinants of health-related states in populations and application to control; designs classified by **whether the investigator assigns exposure**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Observational descriptive** (case report, series, **cross-sectional**, **ecological**) -> hypothesis; **analytical** (**case-control, cohort**) -> test; **experimental** (**RCT, field trial, community trial**) -> prove.`],[`Frequency measures`,`**Incidence** = new cases/population at risk (aetiology); **prevalence** = all cases (planning); **P = I x D**; **incidence density** per person-time; **SAR excludes the primary case**; CFR = killing power.`],[`Descriptive`,`Time (short-term, cyclic, **seasonal, secular**), place (spot map - Snow 1854, migrant studies), person; cross-sectional gives prevalence, **no temporality** (NFHS, ICMR-INDIAB); **ecological fallacy**.`],[`Case-control`,`Outcome -> look back for exposure; **OR = ad/bc**; best for **rare disease, long latency**; biases **recall, Berkson, Neyman**; matching + McNemar; Doll and Hill 1950.`],[`Cohort`,`Exposure -> follow forward; **incidence, RR, AR, PAR**; prospective (Framingham 1948), retrospective, ambidirectional; best for **rare exposure, many outcomes**; bias **loss to follow-up (over 20%)**, **healthy worker effect**.`],[`RCT`,`Only design controlling **known and unknown confounders**; **allocation concealment** (before entry) vs **blinding** (after entry); **ITT = effectiveness**, per-protocol = efficacy; crossover, factorial, cluster, non-inferiority.`],[`Field and community trials`,`Field - healthy individuals (**Salk 1954**, 1.8 million children); community - whole communities (**Newburgh-Kingston fluoridation**).`],[`Trial phases`,`**I** 20-80 volunteers safety; **II** 100-300 efficacy; **III** 1,000-3,000 RCT before licence; **IV** post-marketing; **CTRI registration mandatory since 2009**; SAE report **24 h** (NDCT Rules 2019).`],[`Bias and confounding`,`**Bias** = systematic error, **not cured by sample size** (selection, information); **confounder** linked to exposure, independent risk factor, **not on causal path**; control by randomisation, restriction, matching, stratification (M-H), regression.`],[`Effect modification`,`Real difference across strata (asbestos x smoking) - **report by stratum, never adjust away**.`],[`Causation`,`**Bradford Hill 1965**: strength, consistency, specificity, **temporality (only essential)**, gradient, plausibility, coherence, experiment, analogy.`],[`Reporting standards`,`**CONSORT 2025** (RCT), **SPIRIT 2025** (protocols), **STROBE** (observational), **STARD** (diagnostic), **PRISMA 2020** (systematic reviews); GRADE rates quality.`],[`Choosing a design`,`Burden - **cross-sectional**; rare disease cause - **case-control**; rare exposure - **cohort**; does treatment work - **RCT**; does a programme work - **cluster/community trial**.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`No ethics/CTRI; SAE not reported in 24 h; over 20% loss to follow-up; causal claim from cross-sectional or ecological data; per-protocol as main result.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`No change needed - CONSORT 2025 and SPIRIT 2025 already in place.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Case-control starts with disease and gives OR; cohort starts with exposure and gives RR; only an RCT allocates exposure.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Choosing the design for the question`,steps:[{label:`What is the question?`,detail:`Burden, cause, or effect of an intervention`,tone:`decision`},{label:`How much disease?`,detail:`Cross-sectional survey - prevalence`},{label:`Cause of a rare disease?`,detail:`Case-control - odds ratio`},{label:`Effect of a rare exposure / many outcomes?`,detail:`Cohort - incidence, RR, AR`},{label:`Does the treatment or programme work?`,detail:`RCT (individual) or cluster/community trial`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`branch`,heading:`Errors in a study - and the cure`,root:`Why might the result be wrong?`,arms:[{label:`Chance (random error)`,steps:[`Larger sample, adequate power`,`Report 95% CI and p value`]},{label:`Bias (systematic error)`,tone:`warn`,steps:[`Selection - Berkson, Neyman, volunteer, healthy worker`,`Information - recall, interviewer, misclassification`,`Not cured by a larger sample - fix the design`]},{label:`Confounding`,tone:`decision`,steps:[`Design: randomisation, restriction, matching`,`Analysis: stratification (Mantel-Haenszel), regression`]}]}]},"preventive-outbreak-investigation":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`An **epidemic** is disease **clearly in excess of normal expectancy** for that place and season.`,`An **outbreak** is a localised epidemic; a **pandemic** crosses international boundaries.`,`**Endemic** is the usual constant presence that sets the baseline.`,`Hyperendemic means intense transmission; **sporadic** means scattered unlinked cases.`,`IDSP epidemic threshold is the **mean plus 2 SD** of the previous 3-5 years.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Point source**: sharp rise and fall, all cases within one incubation period.`,`**Continuous common source**: a plateau while exposure lasts, as in Delhi hepatitis E 1955-56.`,`**Propagated**: slow rise with peaks about **one incubation period apart**.`,`**Mixed**: common source followed by person-to-person spread.`,`**Slow (modern) epidemics** are NCDs rising over decades.`,`In a point-source outbreak, the **median case marks the median incubation period**.`]},{title:`Key concepts`,points:[`A **case definition** has three tiers: suspected, probable and confirmed.`,`Keep the case definition **sensitive early**, then tighten it.`,`The food vehicle shows the **largest difference in attack rate**, not the highest rate.`,`Vomiting at **1-6 hours** suggests preformed toxin (S. aureus, B. cereus).`,`Fever and diarrhoea at **12-72 hours** suggest Salmonella.`,`Triggers include **one confirmed cholera case** and any AFP under 15 years.`]},{title:`Steps 1 to 5`,points:[`Step 1: **verify the diagnosis**, taking specimens before treatment.`,`Step 2: **confirm the epidemic** by comparing observed with expected cases.`,`Step 3: **define the population at risk** with a map and denominators.`,`Step 4: **search for all cases** house to house and record them on a **line list**.`,`Step 5: **describe by time, place and person**: epidemic curve, spot map and attack rates.`]},{title:`Steps 6 to 10`,points:[`Step 6: **form a hypothesis** naming agent, source, mode of spread and people at risk.`,`Step 7: **test it** by retrospective cohort (enumerable group) or case-control (scattered cases).`,`Step 8: **study the environment**: water, residual chlorine, kitchens and food handlers.`,`Step 9: further population studies such as serosurvey or carrier search.`,`Step 10: **write the report**: preliminary within 24 hours, then final with recommendations.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Control starts on day one**, in parallel with investigation.`,`Treat cases first; cholera **CFR over 1%** means case management has failed.`,`**Superchlorinate** the source; keep residual chlorine **at least 0.5 mg/L** after 30 minutes.`,`**Measles vaccine within 72 hours** of exposure for children 6 months to 5 years.`,`Give **vitamin A to every measles case**.`,`Chemoprophylaxis is only for **defined close contacts**.`]},{title:`IDSP and the legal framework`,points:[`**IDSP** began in November 2004; reports on **IHIP** since April 2021.`,`**S form** by health workers, **P form** by medical officers, **L form** by laboratories.`,`Reporting week is Monday to Sunday; **nil reports are compulsory**.`,`Trigger events are phoned in **immediately**; the Rapid Response Team arrives within 24 hours.`,`IHR 2005: tell WHO **within 24 hours**; 2024 amendments added a **pandemic emergency** level.`,`Epidemic Diseases Act 1897 was amended in 2020 to protect health workers.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`The family physician is the **first sensor**; phone the district unit the same day.`,`**Declare the outbreak over** after two maximum incubation periods without a new case.`,`That means **10 days for cholera** and **42 days for measles**.`,`Surveillance targets: timeliness and completeness **80% or more**.`,`Non-polio AFP rate of **at least 2 per lakh** children under 15.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Outbreak drugs`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Doxycycline (cholera)`,`300 mg oral`,`Single dose`,`Not in pregnancy or under 8 years`],[`Doxycycline (plague contacts)`,`100 mg oral twice daily`,`7 days`,`Photosensitivity, oesophageal ulcer`],[`Azithromycin (cholera)`,`Child 20 mg/kg, adult 1 g oral`,`Single dose`,`Preferred in children and pregnancy`],[`Ciprofloxacin (meningococcal contacts)`,`500 mg oral`,`Single dose`,`Tendinopathy`],[`Rifampicin (meningococcal contacts)`,`600 mg (child 10 mg/kg) twice daily`,`2 days`,`Orange secretions; reduces pill efficacy`],[`Vitamin A (measles)`,`2 lakh IU oral if 12 months or over`,`Day 1 and day 2`,`Lower doses under 1 year`],[`MR vaccine (contacts)`,`0.5 mL SC`,`Within 72 hours of exposure`,`Dose at 6-8 months does not count`]]},{heading:`Types of epidemic curve`,columns:[`Type`,`Curve shape`,`Example`],rows:[[`Point source`,`Sharp rise and fall`,`Food poisoning at a feast, Bhopal`],[`Continuous source`,`Plateau`,`Delhi hepatitis E 1955-56`],[`Propagated`,`Successive peaks`,`Measles, hepatitis A`],[`Mixed`,`Common source then secondary spread`,`Cholera then household spread`],[`Slow (modern)`,`Rise over years`,`Coronary heart disease, diabetes`]]},{heading:`Food-borne agents by incubation period`,columns:[`Agent`,`Incubation`,`Main feature`],rows:[[`Chemicals`,`Minutes to 1 hour`,`Vomiting, neurological signs`],[`S. aureus, B. cereus toxin`,`1-6 hours`,`Vomiting, no fever`],[`C. perfringens`,`8-16 hours`,`Watery diarrhoea, cramps`],[`Salmonella`,`12-72 hours`,`Fever, diarrhoea`],[`Vibrio cholerae`,`Usually 1-2 days`,`Painless rice-water stools`],[`Hepatitis A and E`,`15-60 days`,`Jaundice`]]}],remember:[`**Verify the diagnosis first, confirm the epidemic second.**`,`Point source: all cases within **one incubation period**.`,`Vehicle = **largest difference** in attack rate between eaters and non-eaters.`,`Nobody waits for a culture to **chlorinate a well**.`,`IDSP: **S by worker, P by doctor, L by lab**; nil reports compulsory.`,`Over after **two maximum incubation periods**: cholera 10 days, measles 42 days.`]},updated:[`WHO 2025: IHR 2024 amendments entered into force 19 September 2025 - 'pandemic emergency' alert level and National IHR Authorities; WHO Pandemic Agreement adopted at WHA78, May 2025`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Outbreak investigation and IDSP`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Epidemic** = disease **clearly in excess of normal expectancy** for place and time; outbreak (localised), pandemic (international), **endemic** (usual baseline); hyperendemic, holoendemic, sporadic, exotic.`],[`Classification by curve`,`**Common source - point** (sharp rise and fall within one incubation period: food poisoning, Bhopal); **continuous** (plateau: Delhi hepatitis E 1955-56); **propagated** (successive peaks one IP apart); **mixed**; **slow (NCD)**.`],[`Concepts`,`**Median case = median incubation period** - count back to date exposure; IDSP threshold **mean + 2 SD** of past 3-5 years; case definition **suspected - probable - confirmed** (sensitive early).`],[`Triggers`,`**One confirmed cholera**, **5+ suspected measles in a block in 4 weeks**, **any AFP under 15**, 2+ AES in a village in a fortnight, malaria over twice the monthly mean or any death, rat fall/one pneumonic plague case.`],[`Steps (order examined)`,`1 **verify diagnosis** 2 **confirm epidemic** 3 define population 4 case search + **line list** 5 **time, place, person** (epi curve, spot map, attack rates) 6 hypothesis 7 test it 8 environment 9 further studies 10 **report**.`],[`Testing the hypothesis`,`**Retrospective cohort** with attack rates when group enumerable (wedding, hostel); **case-control with OR** when scattered; vehicle = **largest difference in attack rate** eaters vs non-eaters.`],[`Food-borne incubation`,`Minutes-1 h chemical; **1-6 h vomiting** S. aureus/B. cereus toxin; **8-16 h** C. perfringens; **12-72 h with fever** Salmonella; cholera hours-5 days; hepatitis A/E weeks.`],[`Control from day one`,`Treat cases (ORS, Ringer's; cholera CFR target under 1%); **superchlorinate**, residual **0.5 mg/L**; isolation only for person-to-person; **MR vaccine within 72 h** + vitamin A; chemoprophylaxis only for defined contacts.`],[`Chemoprophylaxis doses`,`Cholera **doxycycline 300 mg single** (azithromycin 20 mg/kg child, 1 g adult); meningococcal **ciprofloxacin 500 mg single** or rifampicin 600 mg BD x 2 days; plague doxycycline 100 mg BD x 7 days.`],[`IDSP / IHIP`,`Launched **November 2004**; **S** (health worker), **P** (MO), **L** (lab) forms, week **Monday-Sunday**, **nil report compulsory**; **IHIP since April 2021**; RRT on site **within 24 h**; trigger events by phone.`],[`Legal framework`,`**Epidemic Diseases Act 1897 (amended 2020)**; DM Act 2005; **IHR 2005** - notify WHO within 24 h of possible PHEIC; always notifiable: smallpox, wild polio, SARS, new influenza subtype.`],[`Declaring over`,`**Two maximum incubation periods with no new case** - cholera 10 days, measles 42 days, Ebola 42 days.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Timeliness and completeness **80%+**; response within 24-48 h; **non-polio AFP rate 2/lakh under 15** with adequate stools 80%+; **NMNR discard rate 2/lakh**; CFR.`],[`Family physician role`,`**First sensor** - phone district unit same day; specimen before treatment; line list; weekly IHIP with nil report; one consistent message.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Single cholera/plague/wild polio case; 2+ unexplained deaths; encephalitis with bats/pigs/date-palm sap (Nipah, JE); rat fall; descending paralysis (botulism); rising CFR.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**IHR 2024 amendments in force 19 September 2025 - 'pandemic emergency' level + National IHR Authority**; **WHO Pandemic Agreement adopted May 2025**.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Verify diagnosis first, confirm epidemic second; nobody waits for a culture to chlorinate a well.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Ten steps of outbreak investigation - with control in parallel`,steps:[{label:`1 Verify the diagnosis`,detail:`See patients; specimens before treatment (Cary-Blair stool, serum)`},{label:`2 Confirm the epidemic`,detail:`Observed vs expected (mean + 2 SD, past 3-5 years)`,tone:`decision`},{label:`3-4 Define population; case definition; line list`,detail:`House-to-house search with ASHA/ANM`},{label:`5 Time, place, person`,detail:`Epidemic curve, spot map, attack rates`},{label:`6-7 Form and test hypothesis`,detail:`Retrospective cohort (attack rates) or case-control (OR)`},{label:`8-9 Environment and further studies`,detail:`Residual chlorine, kitchens, vectors; serosurvey`},{label:`10 Report and after-action review`,detail:`Preliminary in 24 h; over after 2 max incubation periods`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Epidemic curve shapes`,columns:[`Type`,`Curve`,`Example`],rows:[[`Point source`,`Sharp rise and fall within one IP`,`Wedding food poisoning`],[`Continuous source`,`Plateau while exposure lasts`,`Contaminated well; Delhi hepatitis E`],[`Propagated`,`Slow rise, peaks one IP apart`,`Measles, hepatitis A`],[`Mixed`,`Common source then secondary spread`,`Water-borne cholera then household cases`]]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`IHR alert levels after the 2024 amendments`,steps:[{label:`Event notified to WHO`,detail:`Within 24 h of assessment that it may be a PHEIC`},{label:`Public health emergency of international concern`,detail:`Declared by WHO DG on Emergency Committee advice`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Pandemic emergency (new top level)`,detail:`In force from 19 September 2025`,tone:`warn`}]}]},"preventive-nutrition-programmes":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Malnutrition** covers undernutrition and overweight, obesity and diet-related NCDs.`,`Both together in one population or person is the **double burden of malnutrition**.`,`NFHS-6 under-fives: **29.3% stunted, 19.0% wasted, 31.8% underweight**.`,`Anaemia baseline (NFHS-5): **67.1% of children** and 57.0% of women.`,`The **first 1,000 days** decide stunting, which is largely irreversible after 2 years.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Wasting**: weight-for-height below -2 SD; reflects acute undernutrition.`,`**Stunting**: height-for-age below -2 SD; reflects chronic undernutrition.`,`**Underweight**: weight-for-age below -2 SD; a composite measure.`,`**SAM**: weight-for-height below -3 SD, **MUAC under 11.5 cm**, or bilateral pitting oedema.`,`**MAM**: weight-for-height -3 to -2 SD, or MUAC 11.5-12.4 cm.`,`Asian adults: BMI **23-24.9 overweight**, 25 or more obese; waist 90 cm men, 80 cm women.`]},{title:`Nutritional assessment`,points:[`Weigh every child **monthly at the anganwadi** and plot on the MCP card growth chart.`,`Act on a **flattening curve** before a Z-score line is crossed.`,`Measure **MUAC on the left arm**, midway between acromion and olecranon.`,`Press both feet for **3 seconds** to check for bilateral pitting oedema.`,`An **appetite test** that is passed allows community management.`,`WHO 2024 anaemia cut-off: **10.5 g/dL at 6-23 months** and in the second trimester.`]},{title:`Requirements and diet`,points:[`ICMR-NIN 2020: pregnancy needs **+350 kcal**; lactation **+600 kcal**.`,`Protein requirement is **0.83 g/kg/day**.`,`Cereals and millets should give **no more than 45% of energy**.`,`Eat at least **400 g vegetables and fruit**; keep **salt under 5 g** a day.`,`Cereal plus pulse in about **3:1** completes protein quality.`]},{title:`Prevention and programmes`,points:[`**ICDS** (2 October 1975) now runs as **Saksham Anganwadi and Poshan 2.0**.`,`Anganwadi gives **six services** to children under 6, mothers and adolescent girls.`,`Supplementary nutrition: **500 kcal** children, **800 kcal** SAM, **600 kcal** pregnant and lactating.`,`**PM POSHAN**: 450 kcal (primary) and 700 kcal (upper primary) hot cooked meal.`,`**Anaemia Mukt Bharat** runs the **6x6x6 strategy**.`,`**National Deworming Day**: 10 February and 10 August, ages 1-19 years.`,`**Iodised salt**: 30 ppm at production, 15 ppm at consumer level.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Pregnancy: **one red IFA tablet daily** from the fourth month for at least 180 days.`,`Mild to moderate anaemia: **two IFA tablets daily for 3 months**; recheck Hb at 1 month.`,`Children with anaemia: **3 mg/kg/day elemental iron** for 2-3 months.`,`Take iron between meals with vitamin C, **not with tea, milk or calcium**.`,`Uncomplicated SAM: managed **at the anganwadi** with amoxicillin and weekly follow-up.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Admit SAM with **any complication** or age under 6 months to the **NRC**.`,`Follow the **WHO ten steps**, starting with hypoglycaemia, hypothermia and dehydration.`,`Start **F-75 slowly**, then F-100 or RUTF for catch-up growth.`,`Use **ReSoMal**, not standard ORS, for dehydration in SAM.`,`Give **iron only once the child is gaining weight**.`,`Discharge when weight-for-height is **above -2 SD** or MUAC 12.5 cm or more.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`**Refeeding syndrome** in week one: low phosphate, low potassium and heart failure.`,`Bitot spots or corneal haze: **give vitamin A at once** and refer.`,`Hb under 7 g/dL in pregnancy: refer for **IV iron or transfusion**.`,`Family oedema after new mustard oil: **epidemic dropsy** from Argemone; notify.`,`A child not gaining weight on treatment: search for **TB, HIV and UTI**.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Nutrition supplements and drugs`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`IFA syrup (6-59 months)`,`1 mL (20 mg iron) oral`,`Twice weekly`,`Black stools, constipation`],[`IFA pink or blue tablet`,`Pink weekly 5-9 years; blue weekly 10-19`,`Ongoing`,`Nausea; take at night`],[`IFA red tablet (pregnancy)`,`60 mg iron, 500 microgram folic acid daily`,`180 days in pregnancy and after`,`Not with tea, milk or calcium`],[`Iron (treatment)`,`Two red tablets daily; child 3 mg/kg/day`,`3 months; child 2-3 months`,`IV iron if severe or late pregnancy`],[`Albendazole`,`200 mg at 1-2 years; 400 mg at 2-19 years`,`Twice yearly`,`Not in a sick child or first trimester`],[`Vitamin A (prophylaxis)`,`1 lakh IU at 9 months, then 2 lakh IU`,`6-monthly to 5 years, 9 doses`,`Bulging fontanelle, vomiting`],[`Vitamin A (treatment)`,`2 lakh IU oral (less under 1 year)`,`Days 1, 2 and 14`,`Record dose to avoid toxicity`],[`Amoxicillin (SAM)`,`15 mg/kg oral 8-hourly`,`5-7 days`,`Rash, diarrhoea`],[`F-75 then F-100 or RUTF`,`F-75 about 130 mL/kg/day in 8-12 feeds`,`Stabilisation, then catch-up`,`Refeeding syndrome if advanced fast`]]},{heading:`Anthropometric classification in under-fives`,columns:[`Index`,`Cut-off`,`Term`,`What it reflects`],rows:[[`Weight-for-height`,`Below -2 SD (-3 severe)`,`Wasting`,`Acute undernutrition`],[`Height-for-age`,`Below -2 SD (-3 severe)`,`Stunting`,`Chronic undernutrition`],[`Weight-for-age`,`Below -2 SD (-3 severe)`,`Underweight`,`Acute plus chronic`],[`MUAC`,`Under 11.5 cm; 11.5-12.4 cm`,`SAM; MAM`,`Age-independent screen`],[`Bilateral pitting oedema`,`Present`,`Oedematous SAM`,`Kwashiorkor at any weight`]]},{heading:`Anaemia cut-offs (WHO 2024, g/dL)`,columns:[`Group`,`Anaemia below`,`Severe`],rows:[[`Children 6-23 months`,`10.5`,`Under 7.0`],[`Children 24-59 months`,`11.0`,`Under 7.0`],[`Children 5-11 years`,`11.5`,`Under 8.0`],[`Non-pregnant women`,`12.0`,`Under 8.0`],[`Men`,`13.0`,`Under 8.0`],[`Pregnancy (2nd trimester 10.5)`,`11.0`,`Under 7.0`]]}],remember:[`SAM = **WHZ below -3, MUAC under 11.5, or oedema**: any one.`,`Stunting chronic, **wasting acute**, underweight both.`,`AMB colours: **syrup, pink, blue, red**.`,`**No iron in SAM stabilisation**; start once gaining weight.`,`Vitamin A: **9 doses** from 9 months to 5 years.`,`SNP calories: **500, 800, 600**; PM POSHAN 450 and 700.`]},updated:[`NFHS-6 (2023-24, fact sheet May 2026): under-five stunting 29.3% (from 35.5%), wasting 19.0% (severe 5.2%), underweight 31.8% - anaemia not reported, so NFHS-5 remains the anaemia baseline`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Nutrition assessment and programmes`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Malnutrition** = undernutrition (wasting, stunting, underweight, micronutrient deficiency) + overweight/obesity/diet-related NCD; coexistence = **double burden**.`],[`Classification (WHO 2006)`,`**Wasting WHZ under -2** (acute), **stunting HAZ under -2** (chronic), **underweight WAZ under -2** (composite); under -3 = severe. **SAM: WHZ under -3 or MUAC under 11.5 cm or bilateral pitting oedema**; MAM MUAC 11.5-12.4.`],[`Adult and historical`,`**BMI under 18.5 CED**; Asian Indian **overweight 23-24.9, obese 25+**; waist 90 (men), 80 (women). IAP weight-for-age grades I-IV; Wellcome kwashiorkor vs marasmus.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`**NFHS-6 (2023-24): stunting 29.3%, wasting 19.0% (severe 5.2%), underweight 31.8%**; anaemia (NFHS-5) children 67.1%, women 57.0%, pregnant 52.2%; overweight about a quarter of adults.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Poor diet + infection -> anorexia, malabsorption, higher needs -> weight faltering from 6 months -> **malnutrition-infection cycle**; first **1,000 days** decide stunting.`],[`Assessment`,`Monthly weighing on **MCP card + Poshan Tracker**; **MUAC left mid-arm** (red under 11.5, yellow 11.5-12.4, green 12.5+); **oedema 3 s both feet**; **appetite test**; clinical signs (Bitot, koilonychia, goitre).`],[`Anaemia cut-offs (WHO 2024)`,`Hb **10.5 at 6-23 m; 11.0 at 24-59 m; 11.5 at 5-11 y; 12.0 women; 13.0 men; pregnancy 11.0 (T1, T3), 10.5 (T2)**; severe under 7 (under-5, pregnancy), under 8 others.`],[`Programmes`,`**Saksham Anganwadi + Poshan 2.0** (6 services; SNP 500 kcal child, 800 SAM, 600 PW/LW, 300 days); **POSHAN Abhiyaan 2018**; **PM POSHAN 450/700 kcal**; **AMB 6x6x6**; **NDD 10 Feb, 10 Aug**; NIDDCP **30/15 ppm**; fortified rice universal 2024.`],[`Treatment - prophylaxis`,`AMB: **IFA syrup 1 mL twice weekly (6-59 m); pink weekly (5-9 y); blue weekly (10-19 y); red weekly women**; pregnancy **red daily 180 days + 180 postpartum**, calcium 500 mg BD; albendazole 200 mg (1-2 y), 400 mg (2-19 y).`],[`Treatment - anaemia`,`Mild/moderate: **2 IFA tablets (120 mg) daily x 3 months**, recheck at 1 month; child **3 mg/kg/day x 2-3 months**; severe or late pregnancy - **IV FCM/iron sucrose** or transfusion.`],[`SAM management`,`Admit NRC if complication, failed appetite test or under 6 months; **WHO 10 steps**; **F-75 130 mL/kg/day -> F-100/RUTF 150-220 kcal/kg/day**; **ReSoMal**; amoxicillin 15 mg/kg 8-hourly (uncomplicated); **iron only once gaining**.`],[`Vitamin A`,`Prophylaxis **1 lakh IU at 9 m then 2 lakh IU 6-monthly to 5 y (9 doses)**; treatment days **1, 2, 14** (half under 1 year).`],[`Deficiency signs and toxins`,`Xerophthalmia XN-XS (public health problem XN over 1%, Bitot over 0.5%); goitre over 5% endemic; lathyrism (BOAA); **epidemic dropsy (argemone)**; aflatoxin; fluorosis over 1.5 mg/L.`],[`Discharge and follow-up`,`SAM discharge **WHZ above -2 or MUAC 12.5+**, no oedema 2 weeks; follow-up fortnightly 2 months; NRC targets cure **over 75%**, defaulter under 15%, death under 5%.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Complicated SAM or under 6 months; corneal xerosis; Hb under 7 in pregnancy; family oedema after new mustard oil; no weight gain (TB, HIV, UTI); refeeding syndrome in week 1.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**NFHS-6: stunting 29.3%, wasting 19.0%, underweight 31.8%** (anaemia still from NFHS-5); WHO 2024 Hb cut-offs; ICMR-NIN Dietary Guidelines 2024.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`SAM = any one of WHZ under -3, MUAC under 11.5, oedema; iron withheld in stabilisation; vitamin A 9 doses.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Child 6-59 months - classify and act`,steps:[{label:`Measure MUAC, WHZ, check bilateral oedema`,detail:`MUAC on left arm; press feet 3 s`},{label:`SAM? WHZ under -3, MUAC under 11.5, or oedema`,detail:`Any one criterion`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Complication, failed appetite test, or under 6 months`,detail:`Admit to NRC - WHO 10 steps, F-75 first`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Uncomplicated SAM`,detail:`Community care at anganwadi; amoxicillin; weekly follow-up`},{label:`MAM: MUAC 11.5-12.4 or WHZ -3 to -2`,detail:`Supplementary nutrition, counselling, monthly growth`},{label:`Discharge: WHZ above -2 or MUAC 12.5+`,detail:`No oedema for 2 weeks; fortnightly follow-up 2 months`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Anaemia Mukt Bharat - IFA by life stage`,steps:[{label:`6-59 months`,detail:`IFA syrup 1 mL (20 mg Fe + 100 mcg FA) twice weekly`},{label:`5-9 years`,detail:`Pink tablet 45 mg Fe + 400 mcg FA weekly`},{label:`10-19 years`,detail:`Blue tablet 60 mg Fe + 500 mcg FA weekly (WIFS)`},{label:`Women 20-49`,detail:`Red tablet 60 mg Fe + 500 mcg FA weekly`},{label:`Pregnancy and lactation`,detail:`Red tablet daily 180 days + 180 days postpartum; calcium 500 mg BD`},{label:`Anaemia treatment`,detail:`2 tablets daily x 3 months; severe or late pregnancy - IV iron`,tone:`warn`}]}]},"preventive-water-sanitation-vector":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Safe water** is free of pathogens and harmful chemicals and pleasant to drink.`,`Standard: **no E. coli in any 100 mL sample**.`,`Large-scale purification: **storage, filtration and chlorination**.`,`Target: **free residual chlorine 0.5 mg/L after 1 hour** of contact.`,`At least **0.2 mg/L at the consumer's tap** proves the pipeline is safe.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Water-borne**: cholera, typhoid, hepatitis A and E, rotavirus.`,`**Water-washed**: scabies, trachoma and shigellosis, from too little water.`,`**Water-based**: guinea worm and schistosomiasis.`,`**Water-related vector**: malaria, dengue, filaria and JE.`,`Vector control: **environmental, biological, chemical, personal and legal**.`]},{title:`Water quality standards`,points:[`IS 10500: **fluoride 1.0 mg/L acceptable, 1.5 permissible**.`,`**Arsenic 0.01 mg/L**; **nitrate 45 mg/L**; pH 6.5-8.5.`,`Excess fluoride causes dental then **skeletal fluorosis**; Nalgonda technique removes it.`,`Nitrate in shallow wells causes **infant methaemoglobinaemia** (blue baby).`,`A sanitary well is **at least 15 m from a latrine**, with parapet, platform and hand pump.`]},{title:`Purification`,points:[`**Slow sand filter** works through the **vital layer (Schmutzdecke)** and is cleaned by scraping.`,`**Rapid sand filter** needs **alum coagulation** and is cleaned by back-washing.`,`Chlorine acts as **hypochlorous acid**, best below pH 7.`,`Chlorine **does not kill spores, cysts or ova**, so filtration cannot be skipped.`,`**Break-point chlorination** is the dose after which free residual chlorine appears.`,`Residual chlorine is tested by the **orthotolidine (OT) test**.`]},{title:`Household and well disinfection`,points:[`**Boil** at a rolling boil for 1 minute; store covered and use a long-handled ladle.`,`**NaDCC tablet 0.5 g for 20 litres**, with 30 minutes' contact.`,`Bleaching powder has about **33% available chlorine** when fresh.`,`**Horrock's apparatus**: first distinct blue cup gives the dose.`,`Each cup number means **2.5 g bleaching powder per 455 litres**.`,`Well volume = **pi r squared x water depth x 1,000** litres.`]},{title:`Sanitation and waste`,points:[`A sanitary latrine must not contaminate water and must keep out **flies and animals**.`,`Rural standard: **twin-pit pour-flush latrine** with a 20 mm water seal.`,`**SBM** declared rural India **ODF on 2 October 2019**; Phase II targets ODF Plus.`,`**Jal Jeevan Mission** aims at **55 litres per capita per day** by household tap.`,`**SWM Rules 2026** need four-stream segregation: wet, dry, sanitary and special care.`]},{title:`Biomedical waste in the clinic`,points:[`**BMW Rules 2016** apply to every generator, even a single-doctor clinic.`,`Segregate **at the point of generation**; store no more than **48 hours**.`,`**Never recap needles**; vaccinate all staff against hepatitis B.`,`Needle-stick: wash, report, and start **HIV PEP within 2 hours**, never beyond 72.`]},{title:`Vector control`,points:[`**Aedes aegypti** is a day-biting container breeder; source reduction and a **weekly dry day**.`,`**Temephos 1 ppm** is the only larvicide safe for drinking water.`,`Biological control uses **Gambusia, guppy fish and Bti**.`,`IRS: **DDT 1 g/m2** (2 rounds) or **malathion 2 g/m2** (3 rounds).`,`**LLIN**: one net per two people, effective about 3 years.`,`**Breteau index over 5** or house index over 10 signals dengue risk.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Disinfectants and insecticides`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Bleaching powder (wells)`,`Cup number x 2.5 g per 455 litres`,`1 hour contact, preferably overnight`,`Loses strength in air and light`],[`NaDCC tablet`,`0.5 g per 20 litres`,`30 minutes before drinking`,`Settle turbid water first`],[`Temephos (Abate)`,`1 ppm in containers`,`Weekly in outbreaks`,`Only larvicide for drinking water`],[`DDT 50% WP (IRS)`,`1 g/m2 on walls`,`2 rounds a year`,`Check local resistance`],[`Malathion 25% WP (IRS)`,`2 g/m2 on walls`,`3 rounds a year`,`Do not wash walls`],[`Alphacypermethrin 5% WP (IRS)`,`25 mg/m2`,`2 rounds a year`,`Spray before transmission season`],[`LLIN`,`One net per two people`,`About 3 years`,`Avoid frequent washing`]]},{heading:`Biomedical waste colour coding`,columns:[`Colour`,`Waste`,`Disposal`],rows:[[`Yellow bag`,`Anatomical waste, soiled dressings, expired drugs`,`Incineration or deep burial`],[`Red bag`,`Contaminated recyclable plastics, tubing, gloves`,`Autoclave, shred, recycle`],[`White translucent container`,`Sharps, needles, blades`,`Autoclave, shred`],[`Blue cardboard box`,`Glass vials, ampoules, metal implants`,`Disinfect and recycle`]]},{heading:`Vector indices`,columns:[`Index`,`Formula`,`Threshold`],rows:[[`API`,`Confirmed malaria cases per 1,000 per year`,`Under 1 = elimination phase`],[`ABER`,`Slides examined per 100 population per year`,`10% or more`],[`House index`,`Houses with Aedes larvae per 100 inspected`,`Over 10 = high risk`],[`Breteau index`,`Positive containers per 100 houses`,`Over 5 = transmission risk`]]}],remember:[`Residual chlorine **0.5 mg/L at 1 hour**; 0.2 mg/L at the tap.`,`**Vital layer** = slow sand filter; alum and back-wash = rapid sand filter.`,`Horrock's: each cup = **2.5 g per 455 litres**.`,`Fluoride **1.0 acceptable, 1.5 permissible**.`,`**Yellow burns, red recycles plastic, white is sharps, blue is glass.**`,`API under 1 = elimination; **Breteau over 5** = dengue risk.`]},updated:[`MoEFCC 2026: Solid Waste Management Rules 2026 (in force 1 April 2026) replace the 2016 Rules - four-stream segregation at source: wet, dry, sanitary and special care waste; bulk generators process wet waste on site`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Water, sanitation, waste and vector control`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Safe and wholesome water** = free of pathogens and harmful chemicals, pleasant to drink; **no E. coli/thermotolerant coliform in any 100 mL**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`Bradley: **water-borne** (cholera, typhoid, hepatitis A/E), **water-washed** (scabies, trachoma), **water-based** (guinea worm), **water-related vector**; JMP ladders (SDG 6); IVM: environmental, biological, chemical, personal, legal.`],[`Standards (IS 10500:2012)`,`**Fluoride 1.0 acceptable, 1.5 permissible**; **arsenic 0.01**; **nitrate 45**; iron 0.3; pH 6.5-8.5; TDS 500/2,000; hardness 200/600 mg/L; **residual chlorine 0.2 mg/L at tap**.`],[`Hazards`,`Fluorosis (genu valgum); arsenicosis (raindrop pigmentation, keratosis, cancers); nitrate -> **infant methaemoglobinaemia**; **hepatitis E kills 20-25% of third-trimester women**.`],[`Large-scale purification`,`**Storage** 5-7 days; **slow sand filter - vital layer (Schmutzdecke)**, 99.9% bacteria, scrape; **rapid sand - alum 5-40 mg/L, back-wash**; chlorination: HOCl, **break-point**, **free residual 0.5 mg/L after 1 h** (1 mg/L if viral).`],[`Household and well`,`**Boil 1 min**; **NaDCC 0.5 g per 20 L**, 30 min; **Horrock's - first blue cup x 2.5 g bleaching powder per 455 L**; well volume = **pi r squared x depth x 1,000 L**; OT/OTA test.`],[`Sanitation`,`Sanitary latrine criteria; **twin-pit pour-flush, 20 mm water seal, 15 m from well**; septic tank; sewage - primary, secondary (trickling filter/activated sludge), BOD under 20; **SBM ODF 2019, ODF Plus**; **JJM 55 lpcd, extended to 2028**.`],[`Solid waste`,`**SWM Rules 2026 (from 1 April 2026): wet, dry, sanitary, special care** segregation; bulk generators process wet waste on site; composting (Bangalore anaerobic, Indore aerobic), landfill.`],[`Biomedical waste (BMW 2016, amended 2018-19)`,`**Yellow** anatomical/soiled/expired drugs - incinerate; **red** contaminated recyclable plastics - autoclave, shred; **white** sharps - puncture-proof; **blue** glass/metal implants; **storage max 48 h**; annual report 30 June.`],[`Needle-stick`,`Wash with soap and water, report, **HIV PEP within 2 h (max 72 h) - TLD x 28 days**, HepB prophylaxis per status; never recap.`],[`Vectors`,`**An. culicifacies** rural, **An. stephensi** urban malaria; **Aedes aegypti** day-biting container breeder (dengue, chikungunya, Zika); **Culex tritaeniorhynchus** JE; **C. quinquefasciatus** filaria; **Phlebotomus argentipes** kala-azar.`],[`Vector control doses`,`**Temephos 1 ppm** (only larvicide for drinking water); IRS **DDT 1 g/m2 x 2 rounds, malathion 2 g/m2 x 3, alphacypermethrin 25 mg/m2 x 2**; **LLIN 1 per 2 people, 3 years**; Gambusia/guppy, Bti; weekly dry day.`],[`Indices`,`**API** (under 1 = elimination phase), **ABER 10%+**, SPR; **house index over 10, Breteau over 5** = dengue risk, act in 24 h.`],[`Family physician role`,`Ask water source and toilet in every diarrhoea/jaundice; report clusters on IHIP; colour-coded clinic bins, SPCB authorisation; advise boil/chlorinate, ladle, handwashing, nets.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`E. coli or residual under 0.2 mg/L in a diarrhoea cluster; jaundice cluster (warn pregnant women); cyanosed infant on well water; Breteau over 5; AES in rice district; needle-stick.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**Solid Waste Management Rules 2026 - four-stream segregation, in force 1 April 2026**.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Vital layer = slow sand; alum + back-wash = rapid sand; yellow burns, red recycles plastic, white is sharps, blue is glass.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Water-borne diarrhoea cluster - water response`,steps:[{label:`Cluster reported on IHIP`,detail:`Stool for cholera (Cary-Blair); ORS corners`},{label:`Sanitary survey of the source`,detail:`Pipe leak, well near latrine, flood`},{label:`Test residual chlorine at the tap`,detail:`Under 0.2 mg/L or E. coli present?`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Unsafe`,detail:`Boil-water advisory; superchlorinate; NaDCC tablets house to house`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Daily chlorination and re-test`,detail:`Free residual 0.5 mg/L after 1 h`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Biomedical waste - colour, content, treatment`,columns:[`Colour`,`Content`,`Treatment`],rows:[[`Yellow bag`,`Anatomical, placenta, soiled dressings, expired drugs, chemical`,`Incineration, plasma pyrolysis, deep burial`],[`Red bag`,`Contaminated recyclable plastic - IV sets, catheters, gloves`,`Autoclave/microwave, shred, recycle`],[`White container`,`Sharps - needles, blades`,`Autoclave, shred; puncture-proof`],[`Blue box`,`Glass vials, ampoules, metal implants`,`Disinfect and recycle`]]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Solid waste streams - SWM Rules 2026`,columns:[`Stream`,`Examples`,`Route`],rows:[[`Wet`,`Kitchen and food waste`,`Composting or bio-methanation`],[`Dry`,`Plastic, paper, metal, glass`,`Material recovery facility`],[`Sanitary`,`Diapers, sanitary napkins`,`Separately wrapped, treated`],[`Special care`,`Bulbs, batteries, medicines (domestic hazardous)`,`Authorised agency or collection centre`]]}]},"preventive-disaster-management":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`A **disaster** is beyond the **coping capacity of the affected community** (DM Act 2005).`,`An emergency uses existing resources; a **mass casualty incident** overwhelms one facility.`,`**Risk = hazard x vulnerability / capacity**.`,`We rarely change the hazard, so we **lower vulnerability or raise capacity**.`,`About **58.6% of India** is earthquake-prone and 12% is flood-prone.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Natural: **geophysical, hydrological, meteorological, climatological and biological**.`,`Man-made: **technological** (Bhopal 1984), transport, conflict and CBRN.`,`**L0** is normal time; **L1** district; **L2** state; **L3** national assistance.`,`Levels are graded by **resources needed**, not by deaths.`,`The Act has **no provision to declare a national disaster**.`]},{title:`The disaster cycle`,points:[`Four stages: **mitigation, preparedness, response and recovery**.`,`**Structural mitigation**: seismic codes, embankments, cyclone shelters, mangroves.`,`**Non-structural mitigation**: zoning, hazard mapping, insurance and early warning.`,`**Preparedness** is a written and **rehearsed** plan with stockpiles and drills.`,`**Recovery** means rehabilitation and reconstruction: **build back better**.`,`Most survivors are rescued **in the first hours by neighbours**.`]},{title:`Institutions in India`,points:[`**NDMA** is chaired by the Prime Minister; **SDMA** by the Chief Minister.`,`**DDMA** is chaired by the District Magistrate, with the CMO as a member.`,`**MHA is the nodal ministry**; MoHFW is nodal for biological disasters.`,`**DM Amendment Act 2025** gives statutory status to the NCMC and allows urban authorities.`,`**NDRF** (the Force) has 16 battalions; Aapda Mitra trains community volunteers.`,`**Sendai Framework 2015-2030**: four priorities and seven targets (A-G).`]},{title:`Preparedness at the CHC`,points:[`Keep a **village-wise vulnerability line list**: pregnancies, infants, elderly, dialysis, ART, insulin.`,`Write a facility plan with an **incident commander** and drill it twice a year.`,`Stockpile ORS, chlorine tablets, antibiotics, **anti-snake venom**, MR vaccine and vitamin A.`,`Raise the drug store above the flood line and **protect the cold chain**.`,`Arrange **redundant communication**: mobile, landline, radio and runner.`]},{title:`Response - triage and casualty care`,points:[`Triage aims at **the greatest good for the greatest number**.`,`**Red** immediate, **yellow** delayed, **green** walking wounded, **black** expectant or dead.`,`**START (30-2-Can do)**: RR over 30, refill over 2 seconds, or cannot obey = red.`,`Only two interventions during triage: **open the airway and stop bleeding**.`,`**JumpSTART** for children gives 5 rescue breaths and uses RR 15-45.`,`Crush injury: **start IV fluids before extrication**.`]},{title:`Camp health`,points:[`Priorities: water, sanitation, shelter, food and **measles vaccine with vitamin A**.`,`Measles vaccine covers children **6 months to 15 years**.`,`Start **daily syndromic surveillance on IHIP** from day one.`,`Flood leptospirosis prophylaxis: **doxycycline 200 mg weekly** for heavily exposed people only.`,`**Dead bodies do not cause epidemics**; identify each body.`,`Offer **psychological first aid**; single-session debriefing is not recommended.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`**Crude mortality over 1 per 10,000 per day** means the operation is failing.`,`One case of fever with rash: isolate and start **camp-wide measles vaccination**.`,`Three or more watery diarrhoea cases in 24 hours: **treat as cholera**.`,`Interrupted ART, ATT, insulin or antiepileptics cause a **wave of late deaths**.`,`Write an **after-action report** to the DDMA.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs and vaccines in disasters`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Doxycycline (leptospirosis prophylaxis)`,`200 mg oral`,`Once weekly during exposure`,`Heavily exposed groups only`],[`Doxycycline or azithromycin (cholera)`,`Single oral dose`,`Household contacts only`,`No mass prophylaxis`],[`Measles-rubella vaccine`,`0.5 mL SC`,`All aged 6 months-15 years`,`Give with vitamin A`],[`Vitamin A`,`Oral, by age`,`With measles vaccine`,`Avoid double dosing`],[`Td`,`0.5 mL IM`,`For wounds as needed`,`Add immunoglobulin if course incomplete`],[`Polyvalent anti-snake venom`,`IV`,`As per protocol`,`Stockpile before floods`]]},{heading:`Triage categories`,columns:[`Colour`,`Meaning`,`Example`,`Action`],rows:[[`Red`,`Immediate, salvageable`,`Shock, tension pneumothorax`,`Treat and evacuate first`],[`Yellow`,`Delayed, can wait 2-4 hours`,`Long-bone fracture`,`Stabilise and re-triage`],[`Green`,`Walking wounded`,`Abrasions, sprains`,`First aid; use as helpers`],[`Black`,`Expectant or dead`,`Unsurvivable injury`,`Comfort care; re-triage later`]]},{heading:`Sphere minimum standards for camps`,columns:[`Need`,`Minimum standard`,`Warning sign`],rows:[[`Water`,`15 litres per person per day`,`Residual chlorine under 0.5 mg/L`],[`Sanitation`,`20 people per latrine, 30 m from water`,`Diarrhoea cluster`],[`Shelter`,`3.5 square metres per person`,`Measles or ARI cases`],[`Food`,`2,100 kcal per person per day`,`MUAC under 12.5 cm`],[`Health`,`1 health unit per 10,000`,`CMR over 1 per 10,000 per day`]]}],remember:[`**Risk = hazard x vulnerability / capacity**.`,`Definition mark: **beyond the coping capacity** of the community.`,`**START = 30-2-Can do.**`,`**Dead bodies do not cause epidemics**; crowded survivors do.`,`Camp vaccine: **measles plus vitamin A, 6 months-15 years**.`,`**NDMA (PM), SDMA (CM), DDMA (DM).**`]},master:{heading:`Master revision table - Disaster management`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Disaster (DM Act 2005 s2(d)) = catastrophe from natural or man-made cause causing substantial loss, **beyond the coping capacity of the affected community**; **risk = hazard x vulnerability / capacity**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`Natural - **geophysical, hydrological, meteorological, climatological, biological**; man-made - **technological** (Bhopal), transport, conflict, **CBRN**. Levels **L0-L3** by resources needed, not deaths; no 'national disaster' declaration.`],[`Exposure in India`,`**58.6% land** earthquake-prone, **12% flood-prone**, 5,700 km coast cyclone/tsunami-prone, **68% cultivable land drought-prone**; heat waves a major killer.`],[`Disaster cycle`,`**Mitigation (structural, non-structural) -> preparedness -> response -> recovery (build back better)** - drawn as a wheel; most excess late deaths from crowding, unsafe water, interrupted chronic care.`],[`Institutions`,`**NDMA (PM), SDMA (CM), DDMA (DM; CMO member)**; NEC (Home Secretary); NIDM; MHA nodal, **MoHFW nodal for biological disasters**; **NDRF 16 battalions**, SDRF, Aapda Mitra.`],[`DM (Amendment) Act 2025`,`In force **9 April 2025**: statutory **NCMC** and High-Level Committee; NDMA/SDMA prepare plans; **national and state disaster databases**; **Urban DMAs** for capitals and municipal cities.`],[`Frameworks`,`**NDMP 2016 (revised 2019)**; **Sendai 2015-30: 4 priorities, 7 targets (A-G)**; PM's Ten Point Agenda 2016; **CDRI 2019**; 15th FC Disaster Risk Management Funds.`],[`Preparedness (CHC)`,`Hazard and **vulnerability line list** (pregnant, infants, dialysis, ART, ATT, insulin); facility plan with **incident commander**, triage area, casualty expansion; **drill twice a year**; forward stockpile (ORS, chlorine, ASV, MR + vitamin A).`],[`Triage`,`**Red** immediate, **yellow** delayed 2-4 h, **green** walking, **black** expectant/dead; **START 30-2-Can do** (RR over 30, CRT over 2 s, cannot obey = red); only **open airway and stop bleeding**; **JumpSTART** 5 rescue breaths, RR 15-45.`],[`Casualty care`,`ABCDE, tetanus prophylaxis, delayed primary closure; **crush injury - IV fluids before extrication** (hyperkalaemia, AKI); distribute casualties across hospitals.`],[`Camp health`,`**Sphere: 15 L water/person/day, 20 per latrine, 3.5 m2 shelter, 2,100 kcal**; **measles vaccine + vitamin A 6 months-15 years**; daily IHIP surveillance; restore ANC, TB, ART, insulin.`],[`Prophylaxis and myths`,`**Doxycycline 200 mg weekly** for heavily flood-exposed (leptospirosis); cholera contacts only; **dead bodies do not cause epidemics** - identify, no mass burial; **psychological first aid**, not single-session debriefing.`],[`Heat waves`,`Heat action plans, cool rooms, ORS; **rapid cooling** for heat stroke; work hours shifted.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`**CMR over 1/10,000/day** (U5 over 2) = out of control; Sphere coverage; measles coverage over 95%; diarrhoea CFR under 1%; Sendai indicators.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`3+ watery diarrhoea in a camp sector in 24 h or one death; fever with rash in camp; residual under 0.5 mg/L; CMR over 1/10,000/day; crush injury; interrupted ART/ATT/insulin.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`No further change - **DM (Amendment) Act 2025** already incorporated.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Definition mark = 'beyond the coping capacity'; camp priority = measles vaccine with vitamin A.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`START triage - 30-2-Can do`,steps:[{label:`Can walk?`,detail:`Yes - GREEN (minor)`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Breathing after opening airway?`,detail:`No - BLACK (expectant/dead)`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Respiratory rate over 30?`,detail:`Yes - RED (immediate)`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Capillary refill over 2 s or no radial pulse?`,detail:`Yes - RED; control bleeding`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Cannot obey simple commands?`,detail:`Yes - RED; otherwise YELLOW (delayed)`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`cycle`,heading:`Disaster management cycle`,steps:[{label:`Mitigation`,detail:`Seismic codes, embankments, mangroves, zoning`},{label:`Preparedness`,detail:`Rehearsed plan, early warning, stockpiles, drills`},{label:`Response`,detail:`Rescue, triage, camps, surveillance, PFA`},{label:`Recovery`,detail:`Rehabilitation and reconstruction - build back better`}]}]},"preventive-health-planning-management":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Health planning (WHO)**: defining problems, identifying unmet needs, surveying resources, setting priority goals.`,`It ends with **projecting administrative action**; quote the definition verbatim.`,`**Management** gets things done **through and with people**.`,`Planning is **a function of management**, not a synonym for it.`,`**POSDCORB**: Planning, Organising, Staffing, Directing, Coordinating, Reporting, Budgeting.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Strategic** planning covers policy over 5-20 years.`,`**Tactical** planning covers programmes over 1-5 years.`,`**Operational** planning covers days to a year, like the PHC roster.`,`A **goal** is broad; an **objective** is measurable; a **target** has a number and a date.`,`Objectives must be **SMART**: specific, measurable, achievable, relevant, time-bound.`,`Theorists: **Fayol** (14 principles), Taylor, Mayo (Hawthorne effect), **Drucker** (MBO).`]},{title:`The planning cycle`,points:[`Step 1: **situation analysis**, ending in a problem statement with baselines.`,`Step 2: **objectives** written as outcomes, not activities.`,`Step 3: **assess resources** before fixing targets.`,`Step 4: **fix priorities** using Hanlon's BPR or the PEARL test.`,`Steps 5 and 6: **write the plan**, then implement with intersectoral and community action.`,`Step 7: **monitoring** tracks inputs and process continuously.`,`Step 8: **evaluation** judges outcome and impact and feeds the next cycle.`]},{title:`Economic and management techniques`,points:[`**Cost-benefit analysis** values both cost and benefit in money.`,`**Cost-effectiveness analysis** finds the cheapest way to reach the same objective.`,`**Cost-utility analysis** uses QALYs or DALYs to compare unlike programmes.`,`WHO-CHOICE: under **1 x GDP per capita per DALY averted** is highly cost-effective.`,`**WISN** sets staffing by workload; **queuing models** reduce OPD waiting time.`,`India appraises health technologies through **HTAIn (2017)**.`]},{title:`Network analysis`,points:[`The **critical path is the longest path** and gives the shortest project time.`,`Activities on the critical path have **zero float**.`,`**PERT** (1958) is probabilistic and event-oriented, with three time estimates.`,`PERT expected time: **te = (to + 4tm + tp) / 6**.`,`**CPM** (1957) is deterministic and activity-oriented; only CPM allows **crashing**.`,`Crash the critical activity with the **lowest cost slope** first.`]},{title:`SWOT and TOWS`,points:[`**Strengths and weaknesses** are internal and present.`,`**Opportunities and threats** are external and future.`,`Drug shortage in my PHC is a **weakness**; state supply delay is a **threat**.`,`Always state an objective first; otherwise SWOT lists mean nothing.`,`**TOWS (Weihrich 1982)** turns the matrix into SO, WO, ST and WT strategies.`,`Follow SWOT with **fishbone, five whys and Pareto** analysis.`]},{title:`Planning in India`,points:[`**Bhore Committee (1946)**: integrated care, a PHC per 40,000, the social physician.`,`**Kartar Singh (1973)**: multipurpose workers; **Shrivastav (1975)**: community health workers.`,`**NITI Aayog** replaced the Planning Commission on 1 January 2015.`,`**NHP 2017**: government health spending 2.5% of GDP by 2025.`,`NHA 2022-23: government spending about **1.4-1.5% of GDP**; out-of-pocket **43.4%**.`,`NHM district plans feed the **State PIP**; funding 60:40 (90:10 in north-east).`]},{title:`Quality and monitoring`,points:[`**Donabedian**: structure, process and outcome.`,`**NQAS** assesses **eight areas of concern** with a **70% pass mark**.`,`**Kayakalp** rewards cleanliness; **LaQshya** improves labour rooms; **MusQan** is child-friendly care.`,`Improvement runs on **PDSA cycles** with supportive supervision.`,`WHO evaluation: relevance, adequacy, progress, efficiency, effectiveness and impact.`]}],tables:[{heading:`The eight-step planning cycle`,columns:[`Step`,`Question`,`Product`],rows:[[`1. Situation analysis`,`Where are we?`,`Problem statement with baselines`],[`2. Objectives`,`Where do we want to be?`,`SMART objectives with targets`],[`3. Resources`,`What do we have?`,`Resource and gap list`],[`4. Priorities`,`What first?`,`Ranked problem list`],[`5. Plan write-up`,`Who, what, when, cost?`,`Plan with indicators`],[`6. Implementation`,`How on the ground?`,`Schedules, indents, minutes`],[`7. Monitoring`,`On track now?`,`Monthly progress and correction`],[`8. Evaluation`,`Did it work?`,`Report feeding next cycle`]]},{heading:`PERT and CPM compared`,columns:[`Feature`,`PERT`,`CPM`],rows:[[`Origin`,`US Navy Polaris, 1958`,`DuPont, 1957`],[`Time estimates`,`Probabilistic, three estimates`,`Deterministic, one estimate`],[`Orientation`,`Event-oriented`,`Activity-oriented`],[`Controls`,`Time only`,`Time and cost; crashing`],[`Best for`,`Novel one-off projects`,`Repetitive projects`]]},{heading:`TOWS strategies`,columns:[`Cell`,`Meaning`,`PHC example`],rows:[[`SO`,`Strengths seize opportunities`,`Full staff plus NHM budget: start NQAS`],[`WO`,`Opportunities fix weaknesses`,`Consultant writes SOPs, trains staff`],[`ST`,`Strengths blunt threats`,`Advertise 24x7 labour room`],[`WT`,`Minimise both`,`Two-month buffer stock`]]}],remember:[`Quote the **WHO definition** verbatim; expand **POSDCORB** letter by letter.`,`Draw the planning cycle as a **circle**.`,`Critical path = **longest path = shortest project time**.`,`te = **(to + 4tm + tp) / 6**; only CPM allows crashing.`,`**Weakness is internal, threat is external**; finish SWOT with TOWS.`,`Monitoring = **inputs and process**; evaluation = **outcome and impact**.`]},updated:[`NHA 2022-23 (released May 2026): government health expenditure about 1.4-1.5% of GDP; out-of-pocket 43.4% of THE (up from the 39.4% low of 2021-22) - replaces the 2021-22 figures`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Health planning and management`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Health planning (WHO)** = orderly process of **defining community problems, identifying unmet needs, surveying resources, setting realistic priority goals and projecting administrative action**; management = getting things done **through and with people**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`Levels: **strategic** (5-20 y, NHP), **tactical** (1-5 y, state/district plans), **operational** (PHC session plan). **Goal - objective (SMART) - target (number + date)**.`],[`Management theory`,`**POSDCORB** (Gulick-Urwick 1937); Fayol 14 principles; Taylor; Hawthorne effect; **MBO (Drucker 1954)**.`],[`Planning cycle (8 steps)`,`**Situation analysis -> objectives -> resource assessment -> priorities -> write-up -> implementation -> monitoring -> evaluation** -> back to situation analysis (draw a circle).`],[`Priority setting`,`**Hanlon BPR = [(A + 2B) x C] / 3**; **PEARL** (propriety, economics, acceptability, resources, legality); magnitude, seriousness, feasibility.`],[`Economic evaluation`,`**CBA** (money both sides), **CEA** (cost per natural unit, ICER), **CUA** (QALY/DALY; under 1 x GDP per capita per DALY highly cost-effective); **HTAIn 2017**.`],[`Other techniques`,`System analysis, PPBS, **work study and WISN**, decision tree (rolled back right to left), operational research (queuing, inventory).`],[`Network analysis`,`**Critical path = longest path = shortest project time, zero float**; **PERT (1958) te = (to + 4tm + tp)/6, SD = (tp - to)/6**, probabilistic; **CPM (1957)** deterministic, **crashing by lowest cost slope**.`],[`SWOT-TOWS`,`S, W internal/present; O, T external/future; against a **stated objective**; **my stock-out = weakness, state supply delay = threat**; TOWS: SO, WO, ST, WT strategies; then fishbone, 5 whys, Pareto.`],[`Committees`,`**Bhore 1946** (3-tier, PHC per 40,000, social physician); Mudaliar 1962; Chadha 1963; Mukherjee; Jungalwalla 1967; **Kartar Singh 1973** (MPW pair; PHC per 50,000 with 16 SCs of 3,000-3,500); Shrivastav 1975; NITI Aayog replaced Planning Commission 1 Jan 2015.`],[`Current architecture`,`**NHP 2017**: 2.5% GDP by 2025, two-thirds to primary care; NHM PIP (village -> DHAP -> state PIP -> RoP), **60:40 (90:10 NE/hill)**; **IPHS 2022**; ABDM data layer.`],[`Financing figures`,`**NHA 2022-23: GHE about 1.4-1.5% of GDP; OOPE 43.4% of THE** (64.2% in 2013-14, 39.4% low in 2021-22).`],[`Quality`,`**Donabedian - structure, process, outcome**; **NQAS (revised 2024) 8 areas, 70% pass, 3-year certification**; Kayakalp, LaQshya, MusQan, Mera Aspataal; PDSA cycles.`],[`Monitoring vs evaluation`,`**Monitoring** = continuous, inputs and process, correct now; **evaluation** = periodic, outcome and impact (relevance, adequacy, progress, efficiency, effectiveness, impact).`],[`Family physician role`,`Annual situation analysis, **2-3 SMART objectives**, indent from consumption, roster, Jan Arogya Samiti untied funds, monthly HMIS review.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Objectives with no number/date; targets fixed before resources counted; HMIS coverage over 100%; no community voice; no monitoring rhythm or evaluation loop.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**NHA 2022-23 (May 2026): OOPE 43.4%, GHE about 1.4-1.5% of GDP**.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Planning is a function of management, not a synonym; CPM alone allows crashing.`]]},flows:[{kind:`cycle`,heading:`The planning cycle`,steps:[{label:`1 Situation analysis`,detail:`SRS, NFHS, HMIS, IDSP, IPHS gap, SWOT`},{label:`2 Objectives`,detail:`SMART - outcome, not activity`},{label:`3 Resource assessment`,detail:`Staff, budget, untied funds, drugs`},{label:`4 Priorities`,detail:`Hanlon BPR, PEARL`},{label:`5 Write-up`,detail:`Gantt/network, budget, named responsibility`},{label:`6 Implementation`,detail:`Training, procurement, intersectoral action`},{label:`7 Monitoring`,detail:`Inputs and process - correct now`},{label:`8 Evaluation`,detail:`Outcome and impact - feeds step 1`}]},{kind:`flow`,heading:`Working a network - PERT/CPM`,steps:[{label:`List activities, durations, dependencies`,detail:`PERT: te = (to + 4tm + tp)/6`},{label:`Draw network (Fulkerson numbering)`,detail:`Dummies for dependence; no loops`},{label:`Forward pass - earliest times`,detail:`Take the largest at a merge`},{label:`Backward pass - latest times`,detail:`Take the smallest at a burst`},{label:`Critical path = zero float, longest path`,detail:`Delay here delays the project`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Crash critical activity with lowest cost slope`,detail:`Slope = (crash - normal cost)/(normal - crash time)`,tone:`good`}]}]},"preventive-iec-social-marketing":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Communication** has five elements: **sender, message, channel, receiver and feedback**.`,`**Noise** acts at every stage; **feedback** turns an announcement into communication.`,`**Social marketing** applies commercial marketing to achieve **voluntary behaviour change** (Kotler and Zaltman 1971).`,`**Social mobilisation** brings together **all feasible intersectoral partners** for demand and ownership.`,`**Advocacy** uses evidence and pressure to change policy, law and resources.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Five approaches: **health education, IEC and BCC, social marketing, social mobilisation, advocacy**.`,`They are **layers of one strategy**, not alternatives.`,`**One-way (didactic)**: lecture, poster, TV spot.`,`**Two-way (Socratic)**: counselling, discussion, home visit.`,`Barriers: physiological, psychological, environmental, linguistic and **cultural** (most important in India).`]},{title:`How behaviour changes`,points:[`**Knowledge is necessary but not sufficient** for behaviour change.`,`Mass media move people to awareness; **interpersonal contact** moves them to adoption.`,`Stages of adoption: **awareness, interest, evaluation, trial, adoption**.`,`**Health Belief Model**: susceptibility, severity, benefits, barriers, cues to action, self-efficacy.`,`**Stages of change**: precontemplation to maintenance; expect relapse.`,`**Diffusion of Innovation (Rogers)**: recruit **early adopters** first.`]},{title:`IEC in practice`,points:[`A poster carries **one idea**, few words and a large picture.`,`**Folk media** such as nukkad natak and puppetry carry sensitive topics cheaply.`,`**Kilkari** gives free weekly voice calls from pregnancy to the first birthday.`,`Classic campaign: **Do boond zindagi ki** for polio.`,`The **Central Health Education Bureau (1956)** is the apex body.`,`Seven Cs: **credibility, content, context, clarity, continuity, channels, capability**.`]},{title:`Planning a campaign`,points:[`Start with **formative research**: KAP survey, focus groups, observation.`,`Define **one behaviour** and a SMART communication objective.`,`Segment the audience into **primary, secondary and tertiary** groups.`,`Fear appeals work **only with an achievable solution**.`,`**Pre-test on the intended audience**, not on colleagues.`,`Monitor process, then **evaluate outcome** against a baseline.`]},{title:`Social marketing and mobilisation`,points:[`The competition is the **existing convenient behaviour**, not a rival brand.`,`**Price** includes time, travel, stigma and lost wages.`,`**Place and price beat promotion**: Nirodh (1968) sold in paan shops.`,`**JSY plus JSSK** raised institutional delivery to **90.6%** (NFHS-6).`,`**Pulse Polio** used SMNet and imams to overcome refusal.`,`**Ni-kshay Mitra** lets individuals and corporates adopt TB patients.`]},{title:`The family physician as communicator`,points:[`The **single consultation** is the most powerful communication event.`,`Elicit **ICE** (ideas, concerns, expectations) and confirm with **teach-back**.`,`Use the **5 As**: ask, advise, assess, assist, arrange.`,`Use **person-first, non-stigmatising language**.`,`**Never create demand you cannot meet**.`]},{title:`Evaluation`,points:[`**Process**: reach, materials, sessions and staff trained.`,`**Outcome**: knowledge, attitude, practice and service uptake.`,`**Impact**: coverage, incidence and mortality.`,`Use **cost per behaviour changed**, not cost per person reached.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Five communication approaches`,columns:[`Approach`,`Defining feature`,`Indian example`],rows:[[`Health education`,`Knowledge and skill for informed choice`,`ORS demonstration at VHSND`],[`IEC, BCC, SBCC`,`Segmented, pre-tested, multi-channel`,`Do boond zindagi ki`],[`Social marketing`,`Four Ps for voluntary change`,`Nirodh 1968, ORS sachets`],[`Social mobilisation`,`All intersectoral partners`,`SMNet in Pulse Polio`],[`Advocacy`,`Policy, law and resources`,`COTPA 2003`]]},{heading:`The marketing mix in health`,columns:[`Element`,`Meaning`,`Indian example`],rows:[[`Product`,`Good, service or practice, branded`,`Low-osmolarity ORS sachet with zinc`],[`Price`,`Money plus time, travel, stigma`,`JSY cash plus JSSK free delivery`],[`Place`,`Where and when available`,`Condoms in paan shops`],[`Promotion`,`Advertising, folk media, endorsement`,`Pulse Polio celebrity, mosque announcements`]]},{heading:`Choosing a channel`,columns:[`Channel`,`Reach`,`Best use`],rows:[[`Interpersonal`,`Very small`,`Persuasion, skills, stigmatised topics`],[`Group`,`Small to moderate`,`Demonstration, norm setting`],[`Print`,`Moderate`,`Reminders and job aids`],[`Radio and TV`,`Very large`,`Awareness, agenda setting`],[`Folk media`,`Local but dense`,`Low-literacy audiences`],[`Digital`,`Large but skewed`,`Timed reminders, rumour rebuttal`]]}],remember:[`**Feedback** makes it communication.`,`Social marketing = **Kotler and Zaltman 1971**, voluntary behaviour change.`,`**Place and price beat promotion.**`,`Mass media for awareness; **interpersonal for adoption**.`,`**Pre-test always**, on the real audience.`,`Never create demand you **cannot meet**.`]},updated:[`NFHS-6 (2023-24): institutional delivery 90.6% quoted for the JSY/JSSK 'price and place' example (was 88.6%, NFHS-5)`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - IEC, social marketing and social mobilisation`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Communication** = two-way process: **sender, message, channel, receiver, feedback** with noise; **social marketing** = commercial marketing for **voluntary behaviour change** (Kotler and Zaltman 1971).`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Five approaches**: health education, **IEC/BCC/SBCC**, **social marketing**, **social mobilisation** (all feasible intersectoral partners), **advocacy** (policy) - layers, not alternatives.`],[`Types and barriers`,`One-way (didactic) vs **two-way (Socratic)**; channels interpersonal, group, mass, folk, digital; barriers **physiological, psychological, environmental, linguistic, cultural** (cultural most important in India).`],[`Behaviour theories`,`Ladder: unaware -> aware -> concerned -> knowledgeable -> motivated -> trial -> practice; adoption: **awareness, interest, evaluation, trial, adoption**; **stages of change**; **HBM** (susceptibility, severity, benefits, barriers, cues, self-efficacy); PRECEDE-PROCEED; **Rogers diffusion**.`],[`Good message`,`**Seven Cs**: credibility, content, context, clarity, continuity/consistency, channels, capability of audience; one idea, local words, one feasible action; **fear only with a solution**.`],[`Planning a campaign`,`**Formative research/KAP** -> one behaviour, SMART objective -> **segment** (primary, secondary, tertiary) -> message -> channel mix -> **pre-test on real audience** -> train -> monitor process -> evaluate outcome vs baseline (LQAS).`],[`Four Ps (+ more)`,`**Product, price (incl. non-monetary), place, promotion** + people, partnership, policy, purse strings; **place and price beat promotion** - Nirodh 1968 in paan shops; **JSY + JSSK: institutional births 39% -> 88.6% -> 90.6% (NFHS-6)**.`],[`Social mobilisation`,`Situation analysis, stakeholder mapping, alliance with roles, one core message, partners' channels, participation, institutionalise in VHSNC; **Pulse Polio SMNet + ulema**; **Ni-kshay Mitra**; Arnstein's ladder.`],[`Indian media`,`Print (poster one idea, 3 m legible), radio/community radio, **folk media** (nukkad natak, burrakatha, jatra), **Kilkari**, Tele-MANAS 14416; CHEB 1956; IEC line in NHM PIP.`],[`Consultation skills`,`**ICE**, small chunks, **teach-back**, **5 As**, motivational interviewing, person-first language; never create demand you cannot meet.`],[`Evaluation`,`**Process** (materials, sessions), **outcome** (recall, KAP, service uptake), **impact** (incidence, mortality); **cost per behaviour changed**.`],[`Observance days`,`World TB Day 24 March; World Health Day 7 April; No Tobacco 31 May; Population Day 11 July; Breastfeeding Week 1-7 Aug; Diabetes 14 Nov; AIDS 1 Dec.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Material not pre-tested; wrong language/literacy; fear without action; demand for absent service; stigmatising words; no feedback channel.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`Data refresh only - **NFHS-6 institutional births 90.6%**.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Knowledge is necessary but not sufficient; mass media for awareness, interpersonal contact for adoption.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Planning a communication campaign`,steps:[{label:`Formative research`,detail:`KAP survey, focus groups - why people behave as they do`},{label:`One behaviour, SMART objective`,detail:`e.g. breastfeeding within 1 h from 40% to 70% in 12 months`},{label:`Segment the audience`,detail:`Primary, secondary (mother-in-law, ASHA), tertiary (panchayat)`},{label:`Design message and channel mix`,detail:`Seven Cs; several trusted channels`},{label:`Pre-test on the intended audience`,detail:`Attraction, comprehension, acceptability, persuasion`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Implement, monitor, evaluate`,detail:`Process -> outcome -> impact vs baseline`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Stages of adoption and the channel that moves each`,steps:[{label:`Awareness`,detail:`Mass media, posters, radio`},{label:`Interest`,detail:`Mass media, group talks`},{label:`Evaluation`,detail:`Interpersonal - opinion leaders, peers`},{label:`Trial`,detail:`Demonstration, free sample, easy access`},{label:`Adoption`,detail:`Follow-up, reinforcement, supply assured`,tone:`good`}]}]},"preventive-occupational-health":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`Occupational health (ILO and WHO 1950, revised 1995) promotes the highest well-being of all workers.`,`It ends with **the adaptation of work to man and of each man to his job**.`,`**Pneumoconiosis** (ILO 1971): accumulation of dust in the lungs and the tissue reaction to it.`,`About **90% of Indian workers** are in the unorganised sector.`,`Long working hours of **55 or more a week** are the largest occupational killer.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Hazards: **physical, chemical, biological, mechanical, ergonomic and psychosocial**.`,`Chemical hazards are the largest group; they enter by **inhalation, skin and ingestion**.`,`**Fibrogenic dusts**: silica, asbestos and coal.`,`**Inert dusts**: iron (siderosis), tin and barium; function stays normal.`,`**Organic dusts**: cotton (byssinosis), bagasse and mouldy hay.`,`An **occupational disease** is listed; a **work-related disease** is multifactorial.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`Particles of **0.5-5 micrometre** reach the alveoli.`,`Macrophages ingest **free crystalline silica** and their lysosomes rupture.`,`Released cytokines drive fibroblasts to form **whorled collagen nodules** in the upper zones.`,`Dying macrophages release silica again, so **silicosis progresses after exposure stops**.`,`Long asbestos fibres cause **frustrated phagocytosis** and lower-zone fibrosis.`,`Byssinosis is **bronchoconstriction**, not fibrosis: the **Monday chest tightness**.`,`Noise destroys outer hair cells, giving a **4000 Hz notch**.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`Ask **Ramazzini's question**: what is your occupation?`,`Take a **job-by-job history from the first job**; long-latency disease comes from past jobs.`,`Ask whether symptoms are **better on holidays** and worse on return.`,`Ask whether **co-workers have similar illness**; two or more is a cluster.`,`Ask about **home and bystander exposure**, such as washing dusty work clothes.`,`Ask about smoking, which **multiplies asbestos lung cancer risk**.`]},{title:`Examination and investigations`,points:[`Lead: pallor, **Burton's blue line** and wrist drop.`,`Asbestosis: **clubbing and bibasal crackles**; check SpO2 after a 6-minute walk.`,`Chest X-ray by **ILO classification**; profusion 1/0 or more is positive.`,`**Egg-shell hilar calcification** suggests silicosis; pleural plaques mark asbestos.`,`Spirometry is restrictive in most pneumoconioses but **obstructive in byssinosis**.`,`**Serial peak flow** comparing work and rest days diagnoses occupational asthma.`,`Sputum **NAAT** for every silicotic with cough, fever or weight loss.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Remove the worker from exposure first**; it matters more than any drug.`,`Chelate adults with **blood lead 80 microgram/dL or more**, or 50 or more with symptoms.`,`Organophosphate: decontaminate, then **atropine 1.8-3 mg IV**, doubling every 5 minutes.`,`Atropinised means clear chest, HR over 80, SBP over 80, dry axillae.`,`Carbon monoxide: **100% oxygen** by tight non-rebreather mask; pulse oximeter reads falsely normal.`,`Silicotuberculosis: standard **NTEP daily regimen**; offer TB preventive therapy to silicotics.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Lead encephalopathy: give **dimercaprol 4 hours before EDTA**.`,`**Dimercaprol is contraindicated in methylmercury** poisoning.`,`Watch OP patients for **intermediate syndrome at 24-96 hours**: neck flexor weakness.`,`**Hyperbaric oxygen** for CO with unconsciousness, neurological signs or COHb over 25%.`,`Needle-stick: **TLD for 28 days**, started within 72 hours (ideally 2 hours).`]},{title:`Prevention and programmes`,points:[`**Hierarchy of control**: elimination, substitution, engineering, administrative, then PPE.`,`**PPE ranks last** because it fails silently and protects only the wearer.`,`**Pre-placement examination** places the worker; it does not reject him.`,`Noise limit: **90 dB(A) for 8 hours** with a 5 dB exchange rate.`,`IARC Group 1 includes **silica, benzene and asbestos**; Group 4 was abolished in 2019.`,`Sentinel tumours: **mesothelioma, liver angiosarcoma, sinonasal adenocarcinoma**.`]},{title:`Legislation`,points:[`**OSH Code and Social Security Code 2020** came into force on **21 November 2025**.`,`OSH Code factory: **20 or more workers with power, 40 without**.`,`Factories Act **section 89**: every doctor must notify Third Schedule diseases.`,`**ESI** covers wages up to **Rs 21,000 a month**; contributions 0.75% and 3.25%.`,`ESI maternity benefit is **full wages for 26 weeks**.`,`**Employees' Compensation Act 1923** covers injury arising out of and in the course of employment.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Start **silicosis certification** for every confirmed case.`,`Treat **two or more similar cases** from one workplace as an outbreak.`,`Painless haematuria in a dye or rubber worker: **cytology and cystoscopy**.`,`Pleural mass after asbestos exposure: suspect **mesothelioma**; refer for CT and biopsy.`,`Follow silicotics **yearly** with X-ray, spirometry and TB symptom screen.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs and antidotes`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Succimer (DMSA)`,`10 mg/kg oral 8-hourly, then 12-hourly`,`5 days, then 14 days`,`GI upset, raised enzymes, neutropenia`],[`Calcium disodium EDTA`,`1000 mg/m2/day continuous IV`,`5 days`,`Nephrotoxic; never disodium EDTA`],[`Dimercaprol (BAL)`,`75 mg/m2 deep IM 4-hourly`,`5 days`,`Peanut allergy; not in methylmercury`],[`Atropine`,`1.8-3 mg IV, double every 5 minutes`,`Then infusion 10-20% of load per hour`,`Delirium, hyperthermia, ileus`],[`Pralidoxime`,`30 mg/kg IV, then 8 mg/kg/hour`,`Until recovery`,`Never replaces atropine`],[`Oxygen 100%`,`Non-rebreather at 15 L/min`,`6 hours or until symptom-free`,`Hyperbaric if severe`],[`TLD (HIV PEP)`,`One tablet oral once daily`,`28 days`,`Start within 72 hours`],[`Isoniazid (TPT in silicosis)`,`300 mg oral daily with pyridoxine`,`6 months`,`Hepatitis, neuropathy`]]},{heading:`Dust diseases at a glance`,columns:[`Disease`,`Exposure`,`X-ray and function`,`Key complication`],rows:[[`Silicosis`,`Quarry, stone cutting, sandblasting`,`Upper-zone nodules; restrictive`,`Silicotuberculosis`],[`Coal workers' pneumoconiosis`,`Underground coal mining`,`Upper-zone small opacities`,`PMF, Caplan syndrome`],[`Asbestosis`,`Cement sheets, insulation, brakes`,`Lower-zone fibrosis, plaques; restrictive`,`Lung cancer, mesothelioma`],[`Byssinosis`,`Cotton blowing and card rooms`,`Normal X-ray; obstructive`,`Chronic airflow limitation`],[`Bagassosis, farmer's lung`,`Mouldy bagasse or hay`,`Diffuse mottling; restrictive`,`Fibrosis if exposure continues`],[`Siderosis`,`Iron, welding`,`Dense opacities, normal function`,`None; benign`]]},{heading:`Occupational cancers`,columns:[`Carcinogen`,`Tumour`,`Industry`],rows:[[`Benzene`,`Acute myeloid leukaemia`,`Rubber, shoe adhesive, petrochemical`],[`Asbestos`,`Mesothelioma, lung cancer`,`Insulation, shipbreaking`],[`Vinyl chloride`,`Liver angiosarcoma`,`PVC manufacture`],[`Aromatic amines`,`Bladder cancer`,`Aniline dye, rubber, leather`],[`Soot and mineral oil`,`Scrotal and skin cancer`,`Chimney sweeps, coke ovens`],[`Hardwood and leather dust`,`Sinonasal adenocarcinoma`,`Furniture, shoe making`]]}],remember:[`**Adaptation of work to man and of each man to his job.**`,`All pneumoconioses restrictive **except byssinosis** (Monday feeling).`,`**Egg-shell = silica; plaques = asbestos; melanoptysis = coal.**`,`Mesothelioma has **no link to smoking**; asbestos plus smoking is about 50-fold.`,`Hierarchy: **eliminate, substitute, engineer, administer, PPE**.`,`**BAL before EDTA** in lead encephalopathy; no BAL in methylmercury.`]},updated:[`GINA 2026: tag updated for occupational asthma management (ICS-formoterol per current asthma guideline); Labour Codes in force 21 November 2025 already incorporated`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Occupational health`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`ILO/WHO 1950 (revised 1995): promotion of highest physical, mental and social well-being of workers ... **'the adaptation of work to man and of each man to his job'**.`],[`Classification of hazards`,`**Physical, chemical (largest; inhalation, skin, ingestion), biological, mechanical, ergonomic, psychosocial**. Pneumoconiosis (ILO 1971) - **fibrogenic** (silica, asbestos, coal), **inert** (iron, tin, barium), **organic** (byssinosis, bagassosis, farmer's lung).`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`About **90% of 50 crore workers unorganised**; e-Shram 30 crore+; WHO/ILO **1.9 million deaths/year**, largest factor **long hours 55+/week**; occupational disease grossly under-notified.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`Respirable dust **0.5-5 micron** -> alveolar macrophage -> silica lyses lysosome -> **NLRP3, IL-1, TGF-beta** -> whorled nodules (progresses after exposure stops); asbestos -> **frustrated phagocytosis** -> lower-zone fibrosis, plaques, mesothelioma.`],[`Key history`,`**'What is your occupation?'** - job-by-job from first job; dust, fumes, noise, heat; **better on holidays**; co-workers ill (cluster); home/bystander exposure; smoking; registration (ESI, e-Shram).`],[`Key examination`,`Burton's line, wrist drop (lead); clubbing + bibasal crackles (asbestosis); SpO2 after 6-minute walk; dermatitis, arsenical keratosis; Rinne/Weber; Phalen/Tinel; cock-walk gait (manganese).`],[`Investigations`,`**CXR by ILO 2022** (p, q, r rounded; s, t, u irregular; **profusion 1/0+ positive**; egg-shell hilar calcification = silica); spirometry (restrictive vs obstructive in byssinosis); **NAAT in silicotics**; serial PEF; **audiometry 4 kHz notch**; blood lead, ZPP.`],[`Prevention - hierarchy`,`**Elimination > substitution > engineering (LEV, wet methods) > administrative > PPE (last, fails silently)**; pre-placement (place, not reject), periodic, exit, post-employment exams; TLV-TWA, STEL, ceiling.`],[`Treatment - lead`,`Chelate **80 microgram/dL+ or 50+ with symptoms** (child 45+); **succimer 10 mg/kg 8-hourly x 5 days then 12-hourly x 14 days**; **CaNa2EDTA 1 g/m2/day IV x 5 days**; encephalopathy - **BAL 75 mg/m2 IM 4-hourly, 4 h before EDTA**.`],[`Treatment - OP and CO`,`OP: decontaminate; **atropine 1.8-3 mg IV, double every 5 min** to clear chest, HR over 80, SBP over 80, dry axillae; **pralidoxime 30 mg/kg then 8 mg/kg/h**; watch intermediate syndrome 24-96 h. CO: **100% O2**, HBO if LOC, COHb over 25% (15-20% pregnancy).`],[`Silicosis, asthma, needle-stick`,`Silicotuberculosis - **2HRZE/4HRE**; **TPT** (6H or 3HP) for latent TB; occupational asthma - early removal + **ICS-formoterol (GINA 2026)**; needle-stick - **TLD 28 days within 72 h**, HBIG 0.06 mL/kg + vaccine if unvaccinated.`],[`Law - Labour Codes`,`**4 Codes in force 21 November 2025**; **OSH Code** subsumes Factories Act, Mines Act, BOCW etc. - factory **20+ with power, 40+ without**, appointment letter, **free annual health check**, women at night with consent; **SS Code** carries ESI and EC Act.`],[`ESI and compensation`,`ESI up to **Rs 21,000/month**; contributions **0.75% + 3.25%**; benefits: medical from day 1, sickness 70% x 91 days, **maternity 26 weeks**, disablement 90%, dependants; commuting accidents covered; EC Act death 50% x age factor (min 1.2 lakh).`],[`Noise and heat`,`**90 dB(A) 8 h, 5 dB exchange**, none above 115, impulse 140; hearing conservation programme; heat - shade, **250 mL water every 20 min**, acclimatise 7-14 days.`],[`Family physician role`,`Record occupation for every adult; **2+ similar cases = cluster** - visit, notify; notify Third Schedule diseases; start silicosis certification (Rajasthan 2019 policy, NHRC); smoking cessation; early modified duty.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Restrictive breathlessness in stone/quarry worker; weight loss or haemoptysis in silicotic; pleural mass with asbestos exposure; haematuria in dye worker; lead colic/wrist drop; CO with normal oximetry; child in hazardous work.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**OSH Code and SS Code in force 21 November 2025**; **GINA 2026** for occupational asthma.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Every pneumoconiosis is restrictive except byssinosis (obstructive, Monday feeling); mesothelioma has no link to smoking; IARC Group 4 abolished 2019.`]]},flows:[{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Hierarchy of control - silica dust example`,steps:[{label:`Elimination`,detail:`Stop dry sandblasting altogether`,tone:`good`},{label:`Substitution`,detail:`Steel shot or garnet instead of silica sand`},{label:`Engineering controls`,detail:`Wet drilling, water sprays, local exhaust ventilation, enclosure`},{label:`Administrative controls`,detail:`Job rotation, wet sweeping, training, medical surveillance`},{label:`PPE - last resort`,detail:`Fit-tested respirator; fails silently`,tone:`warn`}]},{kind:`flow`,heading:`Breathless stone worker - work-up`,steps:[{label:`Occupational history`,detail:`Silica exposure, years, wet vs dry methods, co-workers`},{label:`CXR by ILO classification`,detail:`Upper-zone rounded opacities 1/0+; egg-shell hilar nodes`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Spirometry and SpO2 after walk`,detail:`Restrictive pattern, exertional desaturation`},{label:`Cough 2 weeks+, fever, weight loss?`,detail:`Sputum NAAT - silicotuberculosis`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Certify, notify, compensate`,detail:`Remove from exposure; TPT if latent TB; smoking cessation`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Pneumoconioses at a glance`,columns:[`Disease`,`Radiology`,`Function`,`Hallmark`],rows:[[`Silicosis`,`Upper-zone nodules, egg-shell hilar calcification`,`Restrictive`,`Silicotuberculosis`],[`Asbestosis`,`Lower-zone irregular opacities, pleural plaques`,`Restrictive, low DLCO`,`Mesothelioma, lung cancer x50 with smoking`],[`Coal workers'`,`Upper-zone small rounded`,`Little loss (simple)`,`PMF, melanoptysis, Caplan`],[`Byssinosis`,`Normal`,`Obstructive`,`Monday chest tightness`],[`Siderosis`,`Dense opacities`,`Normal`,`Benign, regresses`]]}]},"preventive-climate-change-health":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Climate change** (UNFCCC 1992) is a change attributed to human activity, beyond natural variability.`,`Weather is hours to days; **climate** is the average over **30 years or more**.`,`Climate change is a **risk multiplier**; it rarely creates new diseases.`,`**Risk = hazard x exposure x vulnerability**.`,`2024 was the warmest year and the **first calendar year above 1.5 degrees C**.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Direct pathway**: heat, floods, cyclones, drought and wildfire.`,`**Ecosystem-mediated**: vector-, water- and food-borne disease, air pollution, undernutrition.`,`**Socially mediated**: livelihood loss, displacement, conflict and mental illness.`,`**Mitigation** cuts emissions; **adaptation** reduces harm already locked in.`,`**Co-benefits** are immediate health gains from climate action.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`Above about 35 degrees C, the body loses heat **almost only by sweating**.`,`Sweating fails in high humidity, so **wet-bulb temperature** sets the danger.`,`Heat exhaustion is **volume depletion with a normal brain**.`,`In **heat stroke**, core temperature over 40 degrees C denatures proteins.`,`Gut endotoxin and cytokines cause **rhabdomyolysis, AKI, liver failure and DIC**.`,`**Antipyretics do not work** because the hypothalamic set point is normal.`,`Warming **shortens the extrinsic incubation period** in mosquitoes.`]},{title:`History and examination`,points:[`Ask occupation, **hours outdoors**, shade, water and rest breaks.`,`Any **change in sensorium** in the heat is heat stroke until proved otherwise.`,`Review drugs: **diuretics, SGLT2 inhibitors, lithium, anticholinergics, antipsychotics**.`,`Exclude **malaria, dengue, leptospirosis and meningitis**, which can mimic heat stroke.`,`Measure **core temperature rectally**; never delay cooling to find a thermometer.`,`Ask about **biomass cooking fuel**, a common missed cause of COPD in women.`]},{title:`Investigations`,points:[`Start cooling **before** any test.`,`Send glucose, electrolytes, creatinine, **CK**, LFT, CBC and INR.`,`Dipstick blood with **no red cells** on microscopy means myoglobinuria.`,`Transaminases peak at **24-48 hours**; repeat labs then.`,`**ECG** for hyperkalaemia; malaria RDT and dengue NS1 in endemic season.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Cool first, transfer second**.`,`**Cold-water immersion** is fastest, especially in exertional heat stroke.`,`Otherwise use **ice packs to neck, axillae and groins** with spray and fanning.`,`Aim below **39 degrees C within 30 minutes**; stop cooling at 38.5-39.`,`Heat exhaustion: shade, cooling and **ORS 1-2 litres over 2-4 hours**.`,`**No paracetamol, NSAIDs or dantrolene** in heat stroke.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`Shivering or seizures: **lorazepam 2-4 mg IV**.`,`Rhabdomyolysis: IV fluids to keep **urine 200-300 mL an hour** in adults.`,`Hyperkalaemia with ECG changes: **calcium gluconate 10% 10 mL IV**.`,`Catheterise, then **refer to a unit with ICU and dialysis**.`,`Pollution-triggered asthma: **salbutamol** via spacer, then prednisolone 40-50 mg for 5-7 days.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`**Elderly people** have blunted thirst and often live alone.`,`**Infants** have immature thermoregulation and depend on carers.`,`**Pregnant women**: heat is linked to preterm birth and stillbirth.`,`**Outdoor workers** lose wages if they stop work.`,`**Slum dwellers** live in tin-roofed rooms in the urban heat island.`,`Women cooking on biomass suffer **household air pollution**.`]},{title:`Prevention and programmes`,points:[`**NPCCHH** was launched in **2019** under NHM, run by NCDC.`,`Heat illness surveillance on **IHIP daily from 1 March to 31 July**.`,`IMD heat wave: **40 degrees C plains, 37 coast, 30 hills**, plus departure from normal.`,`**Ahmedabad (2013)** made South Asia's first Heat Action Plan.`,`**NAPCC 2008** has eight missions and **health is not one**.`,`India aims at **net zero by 2070**; NCAP targets 40% less particulate matter.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Keep a **vulnerable-patient register** before summer and review their drugs.`,`Advise **sick-day rules**: hold diuretics, SGLT2 inhibitors, metformin, ACE inhibitors when dehydrated.`,`At **AQI poor (201-300) or worse**, at-risk people avoid outdoor exertion.`,`Review heat-stroke survivors at **1-2 weeks** with creatinine and LFT.`,`Prefer **dry-powder inhalers** where suitable; health care emits 4-5% of global emissions.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs and fluids in heat and pollution illness`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Low-osmolarity ORS`,`Adults 1-2 L oral in sips`,`Over 2-4 hours`,`Vomiting if drunk fast`],[`Saline or Ringer's lactate`,`500 mL-1 L IV`,`Over 30-60 minutes, reassess`,`Smaller boluses in elderly, heart failure`],[`Lorazepam`,`2-4 mg IV (child 0.1 mg/kg)`,`Repeat once after 10 minutes`,`Respiratory depression`],[`Dextrose 25%`,`100 mL IV (child 10%, 2-5 mL/kg)`,`Once, recheck in 15 minutes`,`Thrombophlebitis`],[`Calcium gluconate 10%`,`10 mL IV`,`Over 5-10 minutes`,`Bradycardia; ECG monitoring`],[`Salbutamol pMDI`,`4-10 puffs via spacer`,`Every 20 minutes for 1 hour`,`Tremor, tachycardia`],[`Prednisolone`,`40-50 mg oral (child 1-2 mg/kg)`,`5-7 days (child 3-5)`,`Hyperglycaemia, gastritis`]]},{heading:`Heat-related illness by severity`,columns:[`Type`,`Features`,`Management`],rows:[[`Heat rash and oedema`,`Itchy papules, ankle swelling`,`Cool clothes, elevate legs`],[`Heat cramps`,`Painful spasms after sweating`,`Shade and ORS`],[`Heat syncope`,`Brief faint, quick recovery`,`Lie flat, raise legs, fluids`],[`Heat exhaustion`,`37-40 C, sensorium normal`,`Cooling, ORS or IV fluids`],[`Heat stroke`,`Over 40 C with altered sensorium`,`Cool immediately, ABC, refer`]]},{heading:`Three pathways from climate to health`,columns:[`Pathway`,`Health example`,`Most affected`],rows:[[`Direct: heat`,`Heat stroke, AKI, cardiac deaths`,`Elderly, outdoor workers`],[`Direct: extreme events`,`Drowning, trauma, interrupted care`,`Coastal and hill communities`],[`Ecosystem: vectors`,`Dengue, malaria, JE`,`Newly exposed areas`],[`Ecosystem: water and food`,`Cholera, hepatitis, leptospirosis`,`Households without safe water`],[`Socially mediated`,`Undernutrition, depression, suicide`,`The poor, women, migrants`]]}],remember:[`Climate change is a **risk multiplier**, not a new disease.`,`Three pathways: **direct, ecosystem-mediated, socially mediated**.`,`Exhaustion versus stroke = **the sensorium**.`,`**Cool first, transfer second**; stop at 38.5-39 C.`,`**No antipyretics** in heat stroke.`,`NAPCC has eight missions; **health is not one**.`]},updated:[`GINA 2026: pollution-triggered mild asthma attack - ICS-formoterol an accepted alternative to SABA; oxygen only if SpO2 is under 92%, titrated to 92-95% (children 5 years and under: keep 92% or more)`,`WHO 2025: Belem Health Action Plan launched on Health Day at COP30 (Belem, 13 November 2025) added to international milestones`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Climate change and health`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Climate change (UNFCCC 1992) = change **attributed to human activity** altering atmospheric composition, **in addition to natural variability**; climate = 30-year average; a **risk multiplier**, not a new disease.`],[`Classification - pathways`,`**Direct** (heat, floods, cyclones, drought); **ecosystem-mediated** (vectors, water/food, air, allergens, undernutrition); **socially mediated** (livelihood loss, migration, conflict, mental illness).`],[`Risk framework`,`**Risk = hazard x exposure x vulnerability**; vulnerability = sensitivity + limited adaptive capacity; **mitigation** (cut emissions, global) vs **adaptation** (local) vs **co-benefits**.`],[`Science`,`CO2 280 -> over 420 ppm; methane 28x, N2O 265x; black carbon/ozone short-lived pollutants; **2024 warmest year, first above 1.5 C**; India +0.7 C (1901-2018); WHO projects 2.5 lakh extra deaths/year 2030-50.`],[`Pathophysiology of heat`,`Above 35 C ambient only sweat evaporation cools - fails in humidity (**wet-bulb**); exhaustion = volume depletion, **brain intact**; stroke = core over 40 C -> endotoxaemia, cytokine storm -> encephalopathy, rhabdomyolysis, AKI, DIC; **antipyretics useless**.`],[`Vulnerable groups`,`Elderly alone, infants, pregnant (preterm, LBW, stillbirth), chronic disease, **drugs** (diuretics, ACEi/ARB, SGLT2i, beta-blockers, anticholinergics, antipsychotics, lithium), outdoor workers, slum dwellers, women on biomass.`],[`Key history and exam`,`Occupation, hours outdoors, fluids, urine colour; **any change in sensorium = heat stroke**; drug review; **rectal temperature**; GCS, BP lying/standing, glucose, muscle tenderness, bleeding.`],[`Investigations`,`Cool first; then glucose, Na, K, creatinine, **CK (over 5x normal = rhabdomyolysis)**, LFT (peak 24-48 h), CBC, PT/INR, urine dipstick (blood without RBC); ECG; malaria RDT, dengue NS1.`],[`Treatment - first line`,`Exhaustion: shade, **ORS 1-2 L over 2-4 h**, IV NS/RL 500 mL-1 L if vomiting. **Heat stroke: cold-water immersion** (or ice packs + spray + fan) to **under 39 C within 30 min, stop at 38.5-39 C**; no paracetamol, NSAID or dantrolene.`],[`Treatment - escalation`,`Shivering/seizures **lorazepam 2-4 mg IV**; hypoglycaemia **25% dextrose 100 mL**; rhabdomyolysis - urine 200-300 mL/h; hyperkalaemia **calcium gluconate 10 mL** then insulin-dextrose; refer to ICU/dialysis; **continue cooling in ambulance**.`],[`Pollution exacerbation`,`**Salbutamol 4-10 puffs via spacer q20 min x 1 h** (mild asthma: **ICS-formoterol alternative to SABA - GINA 2026**), **prednisolone 40-50 mg x 5-7 days**; **oxygen only if SpO2 is under 92%, titrated to 92-95% (children 5 years and under: keep 92% or more)**; DPI preferred for low-carbon prescribing.`],[`Indian response`,`**NPCCHH 2019** (NHM, NCDC): state/district health action plans, heat illness surveillance on IHIP **1 March-31 July**, green facilities; **IMD heat wave 40 C plains/37 coast/30 hills + departure 4.5-6.4 C (or 45 C)**; **Ahmedabad HAP 2013**; NAPCC 2008 8 missions, none health.`],[`Policy and commitments`,`**NDC 2022: 45% intensity cut by 2030, 50% non-fossil power (reached 2025), net zero 2070**; NCAP 40% PM cut by 2025-26; PMUY 10 crore+; Paris 2015, Kigali 2016, **COP28 Health Day 2023**, **COP30 Belem Health Action Plan 2025**.`],[`Family physician role`,`Clinic cooling kit; **vulnerable-patient register** and drug review before summer (sick-day rules, lithium levels); report heat illness and fever clusters on IHIP; AQI counselling; green the practice; advocate.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Heat-stroke survivor review **1-2 weeks** with creatinine, LFT, neurology; return to heavy work after normal labs + re-acclimatisation; indicators - heat deaths, excess mortality, HAP coverage, AQI days.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Core over 40 C with altered sensorium; hot dry skin in collapse; tea-coloured urine; temperature not falling; elderly on diuretics/lithium not drinking; post-flood watery diarrhoea cluster.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**GINA 2026 (ICS-formoterol for mild attacks; oxygen only if SpO2 <92%, target 92-95%)**; **COP30 Belem Health Action Plan (November 2025)**.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Heat exhaustion vs stroke = sensorium; cool first, transfer second; risk multiplier not a new disease.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Collapsed in the heat - roadside to hospital`,steps:[{label:`Altered sensorium, seizure or coma after heat?`,detail:`Yes = heat stroke whatever the skin`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Cool immediately on the spot`,detail:`Cold-water immersion; else ice packs neck/axillae/groin + spray + fan`,tone:`warn`},{label:`ABC, glucose, benzodiazepine if shivering`,detail:`Lorazepam 2-4 mg IV; 25% dextrose 100 mL if low`},{label:`Target under 39 C within 30 min`,detail:`Stop at 38.5-39 C; rectal temperature every 5-10 min`},{label:`Transfer while cooling`,detail:`ICU/dialysis centre; CK, K, creatinine, INR`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Heat exhaustion against heat stroke`,columns:[`Feature`,`Heat exhaustion`,`Heat stroke`],rows:[[`Core temperature`,`37-40 C`,`Over 40 C`],[`Sensorium`,`Normal`,`Confusion, seizure, coma`],[`Skin`,`Sweating`,`Dry (classic) or sweaty (exertional)`],[`Treatment`,`Shade, ORS or IV fluids`,`Emergency cooling, ABC, refer`],[`Antipyretics`,`Not needed`,`Contraindicated - set point normal`]]},{kind:`branch`,heading:`Three pathways from climate to health`,root:`Climate change - risk multiplier`,arms:[{label:`Direct`,tone:`warn`,steps:[`Heat waves - heat stroke, AKI`,`Floods, cyclones - drowning, trauma`,`Interrupted dialysis, ART, TB care`]},{label:`Ecosystem-mediated`,steps:[`Vectors - dengue, malaria at new altitudes`,`Water/food - cholera, leptospirosis`,`Air and nutrition - asthma, hidden hunger`]},{label:`Socially mediated`,steps:[`Livelihood loss, migration`,`Conflict, depression, suicide`]}]}]},"preventive-health-information-gis":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`A **health information system** turns data into **information for action**.`,`It is one of the **six WHO health-system building blocks**.`,`**GIS** is a computer system to capture, store, query, analyse and display **spatially referenced data**.`,`**GPS collects position, remote sensing collects imagery, GIS integrates and analyses.**`,`The **HMN framework (2008)** has six components, from resources to dissemination and use.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Sources: **census, CRS, SRS, notification, hospital records, registers and surveys**.`,`Five GIS components: **hardware, software, data, people and methods**.`,`Data are the costliest component; **trained people** are the limiting one.`,`**Vector data**: points, lines and polygons.`,`**Raster data**: a grid of cells, such as satellite imagery or rainfall.`]},{title:`Key concepts`,points:[`Good data are **complete, timely, accurate, reliable, representative and comparable**.`,`A **nil report is still a report**.`,`**ICD-11** came into effect in January 2022.`,`The **layer concept**: one area held as a stack of thematic layers.`,`Spatial and attribute data are joined by a common key such as the **LGD code**.`,`**Geocoding** turns an address into co-ordinates.`]},{title:`Maps and analysis`,points:[`A **spot map** plots each case, as in **John Snow's 1854** Broad Street pump map.`,`A **choropleth** shades areas by a **rate, never a raw count**.`,`**Buffering** asks what lies within a set distance.`,`**Overlay** asks where conditions coincide.`,`**Network analysis** measures real road travel time.`,`Clusters need a test such as **SaTScan**, not the eye.`]},{title:`Pitfalls`,points:[`**Ecological fallacy**: area associations do not apply to individuals.`,`**MAUP** has a scale effect and a zoning effect.`,`**Spatial autocorrelation**: near things are more alike, so p values are falsely small.`,`A pale area may mean **no reporting, not no disease**.`]},{title:`Sources in India`,points:[`**Census 2027** is India's first digital census; reference date 1 March 2027.`,`**CRS** runs under the RBD Act 1969, amended 2023; register within **21 days**.`,`**SRS** (since 1964-65) is a **dual-record system** giving official IMR, MMR and TFR.`,`**NFHS-6** fact sheet (May 2026) omitted anaemia; NFHS-5 is still used for that.`,`**HMIS** holds monthly facility aggregate data and cannot follow individuals.`,`**IDSP** began November 2004; case-based on **IHIP since April 2021**.`]},{title:`Ayushman Bharat Digital Mission`,points:[`**ABDM** launched nationally on **27 September 2021**, run by the National Health Authority.`,`Each person gets a **14-digit ABHA number**.`,`Building blocks: professionals registry, facility registry, ABHA, consent manager and UHI.`,`**Consent** is the legal basis under the DPDP Act 2023.`,`Free tools: **QGIS, Bhuvan, SaTScan and data.gov.in**.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`**Report every notifiable case** to IHIP and Ni-kshay on time.`,`Write a correct **MCCD**; no modes of dying such as cardiac arrest.`,`Keep a **spot map of the practice population** as a follow-up list.`,`**Never map identifiable patients**; aggregate and suppress small numbers.`,`Reporting completeness and timeliness target is **over 80%**.`]}],tables:[{heading:`GIS functions`,columns:[`Function`,`Question`,`Example`,`Common error`],rows:[[`Thematic mapping`,`Where are the cases?`,`Choropleth of district IMR`,`Mapping counts, not rates`],[`Buffering`,`What lies within a distance?`,`Population within 5 km of a CHC`,`Wrong projection`],[`Overlay`,`Where do conditions coincide?`,`Arsenic aquifer over hand pumps`,`Reading causation`],[`Network analysis`,`Real travel time?`,`108 ambulance siting`,`Straight-line distance`],[`Interpolation`,`Value between points?`,`Fluoride surface from sampled wells`,`Too few points`],[`Cluster detection`,`More than chance?`,`Dengue hotspot`,`Untested visual cluster`]]},{heading:`Sources of health information in India`,columns:[`Source`,`Provides`,`Limitation`],rows:[[`Census`,`Denominators`,`Ten-yearly; projections drift`],[`CRS`,`Legal birth and death record`,`Incomplete medical certification`],[`SRS`,`Official IMR, MMR, TFR`,`No district estimates`],[`NFHS`,`District coverage and nutrition`,`Every 5-6 years, self-reported`],[`HMIS`,`Monthly facility data`,`Aggregate; private sector missing`],[`IDSP on IHIP`,`Near real-time epidemic data`,`Private under-reporting`]]},{heading:`GIS, GPS and remote sensing`,columns:[`Feature`,`GIS`,`GPS`,`Remote sensing`],rows:[[`Question`,`What is where and why?`,`Where is this?`,`What is the surface?`],[`Output`,`Layered maps`,`Latitude and longitude`,`Imagery, land use`],[`Health use`,`Hotspots, microplans`,`Geo-tagging households`,`Predicting breeding sites`],[`Indian example`,`QGIS dengue maps`,`NavIC`,`IRS satellites on Bhuvan`]]}],remember:[`Open with the **GIS definition**: capture, store, query, analyse, display.`,`Five components: **hardware, software, data, people, methods**.`,`**John Snow 1854** opens every uses-of-GIS answer.`,`Two traps: **ecological fallacy and MAUP**.`,`SRS gives rates; **Census gives denominators**.`,`ABDM: **27 September 2021**, 14-digit ABHA.`]},updated:[`MHA 2026: Census 2027 is India's first digital census (app-based and online self-enumeration); houselisting April-September 2026, population enumeration February 2027, reference date 1 March 2027`,`NFHS-6 (2023-24) fact sheet released 29 May 2026 - now quoted as the district coverage source; it omitted anaemia and sex ratio at birth, so NFHS-5 remains the source for those`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Health information systems and GIS`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**HIS** = collection, processing, analysis and transmission of information to run services - product is **information for action**; one of **six WHO building blocks**. **GIS** = computer system to **capture, store, query, analyse and display spatially referenced data**.`],[`Classification - sources`,`**Census, CRS (vital registration), SRS, notification/surveillance, hospital and programme records, disease registers, record linkage, surveys (NFHS, NSS, LASI, GATS), manpower data**.`],[`HMN framework 2008`,`Six components: **resources, indicators, data sources, data management, information products, dissemination and use**; results chain determinants -> inputs -> outputs -> outcomes -> status, with equity.`],[`Good data attributes`,`**Complete (nil report counts), timely, accurate/valid, reliable, representative, comparable (ICD-11 from January 2022), simple, confidential**.`],[`GIS components and data`,`**Hardware, software, data (costliest), people (limiting), methods**; **vector** (point, line, polygon) vs **raster** (grid); spatial + attribute data joined by **LGD code**; WGS84 datum, UTM projection.`],[`GIS vs GPS vs RS`,`**GPS collects position** (NAVSTAR, India's **NavIC**), **remote sensing collects imagery** (IRS, Bhuvan), **GIS integrates and analyses**.`],[`GIS functions`,`Thematic mapping (spot map - **Snow 1854**; **choropleth of rates, never counts**), query, **buffering**, **overlay**, **network analysis** (travel time), interpolation, **cluster detection (SaTScan, Getis-Ord)**.`],[`Pitfalls`,`**Ecological fallacy**, **MAUP (scale and zoning)**, spatial autocorrelation, unstable small-area rates, under-reporting looks like low incidence, boundary changes.`],[`Indian sources now`,`**Census 2027 - first digital census**, houselisting April-September 2026, reference date **1 March 2027**; **CRS (RBD Amendment 2023 - birth certificate single proof from 1 October 2023)**; SRS (dual record, 1964-65); **NFHS-6 fact sheet May 2026**; HMIS; **IDSP/IHIP**.`],[`Digital health`,`**ABDM 27 September 2021** - **14-digit ABHA**, HPR, HFR, consent manager, UHI; 70 crore+ ABHA; **DPDP Act 2023 (rules 2025)** - consent is legal basis; National Geospatial Policy 2022; QGIS, SaTScan free.`],[`Uses of GIS`,`Dengue/malaria hotspots, **Pulse Polio microplans** (brick kilns, nomads), IRS planning, facility siting vs **IPHS norms**, 108 placement, fluoride/arsenic belts, disaster damage.`],[`Family physician role`,`Notify on IHIP and Ni-kshay; **correct MCCD** (no 'cardiac arrest'); create/link ABHA with consent; hand-drawn practice spot map; use NFHS-6 district data; never map identifiable patients.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`**Completeness and timeliness** (IDSP target 80%+); zero reporting, outlier checks, validation visits; **MCCD about 23% (2020)**; every map: title, legend, north arrow, scale, source, denominator.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Labelled household dots for HIV/TB; causation read from overlay; untested 'cluster'; pale area = no reporting; raw-count choropleth.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**Census 2027 first digital census (houselisting 2026)**; **NFHS-6 fact sheet (May 2026) - no anaemia or SRB**.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`SRS gives rates, Census denominators, CRS the legal record, NFHS district coverage; buffer = within a distance, overlay = coincidence.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Mapping a dengue cluster in a block`,steps:[{label:`Line list with address and onset date`,detail:`From IHIP, labs, private clinics`},{label:`Geocode to village/ward (LGD code)`,detail:`GPS-tag households and breeding sites`},{label:`Map rates, not counts`,detail:`Choropleth per 1,000; spot map for houses`},{label:`Test the cluster`,detail:`SaTScan / Getis-Ord - chance excluded?`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Buffer 400 m around cases`,detail:`Source reduction, Breteau survey, fogging`,tone:`good`},{label:`Re-map next period`,detail:`Did the hotspot shrink or move?`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Indian sources of health information`,columns:[`Source`,`Gives`,`Limitation`],rows:[[`Census (next 2027, first digital)`,`Denominators`,`Ten-yearly; projections drift`],[`CRS (RBD Act 1969, amended 2023)`,`Legal births, deaths, MCCD`,`Incomplete MCCD, urban-biased`],[`SRS (1964-65)`,`Official CBR, CDR, IMR, TFR, MMR`,`No district estimates`],[`NFHS-6 (2023-24)`,`District coverage, nutrition, fertility`,`5-6 yearly; anaemia still from NFHS-5`],[`IDSP on IHIP`,`Near real-time epidemic data`,`Private-sector under-reporting`]]}]},"preventive-sex-ratio-pcpndt":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`In India, **sex ratio = females per 1000 males**; elsewhere, males per 100 females.`,`**Child sex ratio** = girls per 1000 boys aged **0-6 years**, from the census only.`,`**Sex ratio at birth** is the better indicator for action; the natural SRB is **about 950**.`,`Census 2011: sex ratio **943**, child sex ratio **918**, lowest since independence.`,`Latest SRS SRB is **917 (2021-23)**; NFHS-6 did not report SRB.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Pre-birth causes**: sex selection before or after conception.`,`**Post-birth causes**: excess female child mortality, neglect and infanticide.`,`**Statistical causes**: under-enumeration of girls.`,`Highest sex ratio: **Kerala 1084**; lowest: **Haryana 879**.`,`Worst child sex ratio: **Haryana 834, Punjab 846**.`]},{title:`Why the ratio fell`,points:[`**Son preference** is the root cause: inheritance, dowry, old-age security, last rites.`,`**Cheap ultrasound** from the mid-1980s is the permissive cause.`,`**Falling fertility plus son preference**: with two children desired, couples select.`,`So ratios are **worst in richer, literate, low-fertility** households.`,`The ratio worsens sharply with **birth order** after one or two daughters.`,`Economic Survey 2017-18 estimated **63 million missing women**.`]},{title:`Consequences`,points:[`**Marriage squeeze** two decades later.`,`**Bride buying and trafficking** from eastern states.`,`More violence and coerced repeated pregnancies for women.`,`The deficit persists in each cohort for **60-70 years**.`]},{title:`The PCPNDT Act`,points:[`PNDT Act 1994, in force **1 January 1996**; amended **14 February 2003** after CEHAT case.`,`**Section 3A** prohibits sex selection before or after conception.`,`**Sections 5 and 6** forbid communicating foetal sex by **words, signs or any manner**.`,`**Section 4(3)** indications: over 35, two or more abortions, teratogens, family history.`,`**Section 24** presumes the woman was **compelled**; she is treated as a victim.`,`Offences are **cognizable, non-bailable and non-compoundable**.`]},{title:`Forms and authorities`,points:[`Register on **Form A**; certificate **Form B** is valid 5 years and displayed.`,`**Form F before every obstetric scan**; keep it 2 years.`,`Monthly returns reach the Appropriate Authority **by the 5th**.`,`**Central Supervisory Board** is chaired by the Union Health Minister.`,`District Appropriate Authority is the **CMO or Civil Surgeon**.`]},{title:`Prevention and programmes`,points:[`**Beti Bachao Beti Padhao** launched **22 January 2015 at Panipat**.`,`BBBP now runs under the **Sambal vertical of Mission Shakti**.`,`**Sukanya Samriddhi Yojana**: savings for a girl under 10; matures at 21.`,`**Apni Beti Apna Dhan** (Haryana 1994) was the first state cash transfer.`,`**National Girl Child Day is 24 January**.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`**Never disclose foetal sex**, not even by a gesture or coloured slip.`,`Refuse sex determination calmly and **explore coercion**; offer One Stop Centre and 181.`,`Report clinics or advertisements to the **district Appropriate Authority** in writing.`,`PCPNDT does **not** restrict lawful abortion under the **MTP Act 2021**.`,`District Task Force reviews **SRB monthly** from CRS and HMIS.`]}],tables:[{heading:`PCPNDT penalties`,columns:[`Offender`,`First conviction`,`Subsequent conviction`],rows:[[`Doctor, owner or employee`,`Up to 3 years and Rs 10,000`,`Up to 5 years and Rs 50,000`],[`Doctor - Medical Council`,`Name removed 5 years`,`Name removed permanently`],[`Person seeking sex selection`,`Up to 3 years and Rs 50,000`,`Up to 5 years and Rs 1,00,000`],[`Advertisement`,`Up to 3 years and Rs 10,000`,`Same`],[`Other contravention (e.g. Form F)`,`Up to 3 months or Rs 1000`,`Plus Rs 500 per day`],[`Pregnant woman`,`Presumed compelled; not punished`,`Same`]]},{heading:`PCPNDT compliance in an ultrasound practice`,columns:[`Step`,`What to do`,`Form`],rows:[[`Register`,`Premises, machine and sonologist; renew every 5 years`,`Form A, Form B`],[`Display`,`Board in English and local language`,`Rule 17`],[`Consent`,`Written consent for invasive tests`,`Form G`],[`Record`,`Before every obstetric scan`,`Form F`],[`Report`,`Monthly returns by the 5th`,`Rule 9`]]},{heading:`Sex ratio by census`,columns:[`Census`,`Overall sex ratio`,`Child sex ratio`],rows:[[`1961`,`941`,`976`],[`1991`,`927`,`945`],[`2001`,`933`,`927`],[`2011`,`943`,`918`]]}],remember:[`India: **females per 1000 males**.`,`2011: **943 and 918**; natural SRB about 950.`,`**Falling fertility plus son preference** drives selection.`,`**Form F before every scan**, kept 2 years, returns by the 5th.`,`PCPNDT bans **sex selection, not abortion**.`,`BBBP: **22 January 2015, Panipat**.`]},updated:[`SRS 2023: sex ratio at birth 917 (2021-23), up from 907 (2018-20) and 914 (2020-22); Chhattisgarh highest (974); NFHS-6 fact sheet did not report SRB`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Sex ratio and the PCPNDT Act`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`India: **sex ratio = females per 1000 males** (UN: males per 100 females); **CSR** = girls per 1000 boys 0-6 (census only); **SRB** = female per 1000 male live births - **best indicator for action**; natural SRB about **950**.`],[`Current figures`,`**Census 2011: sex ratio 943, CSR 918** (lowest since independence; urban 905); Kerala 1084, Haryana 879; CSR worst Haryana 834, Punjab 846; **SRS SRB 917 (2021-23)**; NFHS-5 SRB 929.`],[`Classification of causes`,`**Pre-birth** (sex selection), **post-birth** (excess female child mortality, infanticide, neglect), **statistical** (under-enumeration of girls).`],[`Mechanism`,`**Son preference** (inheritance, dowry, old-age security, last rites) + **cheap ultrasound** + **falling fertility** -> selection; worst in **richer, literate, low-fertility** households and **higher birth orders**; 63 million missing women (Economic Survey 2017-18).`],[`Consequences`,`Marriage squeeze, bride trafficking, violence, repeated coerced abortions; deficit persists 60-70 years.`],[`PCPNDT Act`,`PNDT Act **1994**, in force **1 January 1996**; amended **14 February 2003** (CEHAT 2001) - adds **pre-conception** selection and ultrasound; **S3A** bans sex selection; **S5(2), 6** bar communicating sex by word or sign; **S4(2)** permitted conditions.`],[`Section 4(3) indications`,`**Over 35 years; 2+ spontaneous abortions; teratogen exposure; family history of mental retardation, deformity or genetic disease; other CSB-specified**.`],[`Forms and records`,`**Form A** register -> **Form B** certificate (5 years) displayed; **Form F before every obstetric scan**, kept **2 years**; returns **by the 5th**; Form G consent; Form D lab, E clinic, H register.`],[`Authorities`,`**CSB** (Union Health Minister, every 6 months); SSB (every 4 months); **Appropriate Authority** (district CMO) - register, inspect, **search and seize**; Advisory Committee 9 members, every 60 days.`],[`Penalties`,`Doctor **3 years + Rs 10,000**, then **5 years + Rs 50,000**; SMC suspension, removal 5 years; seeker 3 y + Rs 50,000; **cognizable, non-bailable, non-compoundable**; **S24 woman presumed compelled**.`],[`Related law and schemes`,`**MTP Act 2021** governs abortion (20/24 weeks; Medical Board beyond 24) - PCPNDT does **not** restrict lawful MTP; **BBBP 22 January 2015 Panipat** (now Mission Shakti - Sambal); Sukanya Samriddhi 2015; Girl Child Day 24 January.`],[`Family physician role`,`**Never disclose sex** (word, sign, slip); register machine; complete Form F; record S4(3) indication; refuse firmly, explore coercion, offer OSC/181; report touts; do not refuse lawful MTP; equal care for sick girls.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`**SRB monthly from CRS/HMIS** at District Task Force; SRS 3-year state SRB; inspections, decoys, convictions; small numbers swing wildly.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Sex-determination request; advertisement or tout; unregistered/portable machine; blank Form F; woman pressured to abort a girl; falling SRB in a block; pre-conception selection requests.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`Data refresh - **SRS SRB 917 (2021-23)**; NFHS-6 did not publish SRB.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`PCPNDT prohibits sex selection, not abortion; Form F before every obstetric scan.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Every obstetric scan - PCPNDT compliance`,steps:[{label:`Machine and sonologist registered at this address`,detail:`Form B displayed; notice board in local language`},{label:`Indication recorded; Form G consent if invasive`,detail:`Section 4(3) for amniocentesis/CVS`},{label:`Complete Form F before scanning`,detail:`Signed by doctor and woman; no blank columns`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Request to know foetal sex?`,detail:`Refuse; explore coercion; offer OSC/181; document`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Monthly return by the 5th; records 2 years`,detail:`Notify any change of machine or doctor`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Three sex ratios`,columns:[`Ratio`,`Source`,`Latest`,`Use`],rows:[[`Overall sex ratio`,`Census`,`943 (2011)`,`Survival of women at all ages`],[`Child sex ratio 0-6`,`Census`,`918 (2011)`,`Lags; mixes three causes`],[`Sex ratio at birth`,`SRS, CRS, HMIS, NFHS`,`917 (SRS 2021-23)`,`Fastest signal of sex selection - act on it`]]}]},"preventive-child-women-legislation":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`A **child** is anyone **below 18** under the JJ Act, POCSO and UNCRC.`,`The Child Labour Act uses **below 14 (child)** and **14-18 (adolescent)**.`,`**Art 15(3)** allows special provision for women and children.`,`**Art 21A** gives education 6-14; **Art 24** bars child labour below 14 in hazardous work.`,`**BNS, BNSS and BSA** replaced IPC, CrPC and Evidence Act from **1 July 2024**.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Child laws: **JJ, POCSO, Child Labour, PCMA, RTE and IMS**.`,`Women's laws: **PWDVA, POSH, Dowry, Maternity Benefit, MTP and PCPNDT**.`,`**Child in conflict with law** goes to the **Juvenile Justice Board**.`,`**Child in need of care and protection** goes to the **Child Welfare Committee**.`,`JJ offences: **petty, serious and heinous** (minimum 7 years).`]},{title:`The Juvenile Justice Act`,points:[`Uniform juvenile age of **18 for both sexes** since the 2000 Act.`,`**Age is reckoned on the date of the offence**.`,`Board: **magistrate plus two social workers**, at least one a woman.`,`CWC: **chairperson plus four members**; it alone declares a child free for adoption.`,`**Five absolute bars**: no death, no life without release, no jail, no joint trial, no identity.`,`A 16-18-year-old accused of a heinous offence gets a **preliminary assessment within 3 months**.`,`Since 2022 the **District Magistrate** issues adoption orders.`]},{title:`POCSO and other child laws`,points:[`**POCSO 2012** is gender-neutral; consent is irrelevant below 18.`,`**Section 19**: reporting is **mandatory for every person**, including doctors.`,`**Section 21** punishes failure to report with **up to 6 months**.`,`A girl is examined by a **woman doctor**, even without an FIR.`,`**Child Labour Act 2016**: total ban below 14; adolescents barred from hazardous work.`,`**PCMA 2006**: legal marriage age **18 for females, 21 for males**.`,`**IMS Act** bans promotion of infant milk substitutes up to 2 years.`]},{title:`Laws protecting women`,points:[`**PWDVA 2005** is a civil law covering physical, sexual, verbal, emotional and **economic** abuse.`,`PWDVA gives **protection and residence orders**; the Protection Officer files the DIR.`,`**POSH Act 2013**: Internal Complaints Committee at **10 or more employees**, half women.`,`**Dowry death** within 7 years of marriage is now **BNS Section 80**.`,`**Maternity Benefit 2017**: **26 weeks** for first two children; **creche at 50+** employees.`,`**MTP Act 2021**: one doctor to 20 weeks; two doctors 20-24 weeks; Medical Board beyond 24.`]},{title:`Policy and programmes`,points:[`**NPEW 2001** is still in force; the 2016 draft was never notified.`,`NPEW strategies include **gender budgeting** since 2005-06.`,`**Mission Vatsalya** runs child protection and **Childline 1098**.`,`**Mission Shakti**: One Stop Centres (Sakhi), **181 helpline** and PMMVY.`,`**PMMVY**: Rs 5,000 for the first child; Rs 6,000 for a second girl child.`]},{title:`The doctor's mandatory duties`,points:[`Report every suspected **sexual offence against a child** to police under POCSO.`,`A **pregnant girl below 18** is proof of a POCSO offence; report it.`,`Give **free treatment** to every rape or acid attack victim; refusal is punishable.`,`Report an **abandoned child within 24 hours** to 1098, police or CWC.`,`For domestic violence, **police reporting is the woman's choice**, unlike POCSO.`,`Never write 'rape' on a certificate; **record findings and consistency**.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`STI in a prepubertal child or genital injury: **mandatory POCSO report**.`,`Injuries inconsistent with history: **admit for safety** and inform CWC.`,`Married girl under 18: inform the **Child Marriage Prohibition Officer**.`,`NFHS-5: **23.3% of women 20-24** married before 18.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Mandatory duties on the doctor`,columns:[`Law`,`Trigger`,`Doctor must`,`Penalty`],rows:[[`POCSO Sec 19 and 21`,`Sexual offence on anyone below 18`,`Report to police or SJPU`,`Up to 6 months`],[`JJ Act 2015`,`Abandoned or separated child`,`Inform 1098 or CWC within 24 hours`,`Up to 6 months`],[`Criminal Law Amendment 2013`,`Rape or acid attack victim`,`Free treatment; inform police`,`Up to 1 year`],[`PCPNDT`,`Request for foetal sex`,`Never disclose; Form F`,`Up to 3 years`],[`MTP Act Sec 5A`,`Any termination`,`Keep identity confidential`,`Up to 1 year`],[`PWDVA`,`Woman reports violence`,`Medical aid; DIR if she wishes`,`Professional misconduct`]]},{heading:`Acts at a glance`,columns:[`Act`,`Year`,`Key provision`],rows:[[`Juvenile Justice Act`,`2015, amended 2021`,`Preliminary assessment for 16-18 heinous`],[`POCSO`,`2012, amended 2019`,`Mandatory reporting`],[`Child Labour Act`,`1986, amended 2016`,`Total ban below 14`],[`Prohibition of Child Marriage`,`2006`,`18 girls, 21 boys`],[`RTE Act`,`2009`,`Free education 6-14; 25% private seats`],[`PWDVA`,`2005`,`Civil remedy, residence order`],[`POSH Act`,`2013`,`ICC at 10+ employees`],[`Maternity Benefit`,`1961, amended 2017`,`26 weeks; creche at 50+`]]},{heading:`Juvenile Justice bodies`,columns:[`Body`,`Composition`,`Function`],rows:[[`Juvenile Justice Board`,`Magistrate plus 2 social workers`,`Child in conflict with law`],[`Child Welfare Committee`,`Chairperson plus 4 members`,`Child in need of care`],[`SJPU`,`Trained police officer`,`Produce child within 24 hours`],[`CARA`,`Statutory apex body`,`Adoption and foster care`]]}],remember:[`Juvenile age **18 for both sexes**; age on the date of the offence.`,`Two streams: **conflict with law to JJB, care and protection to CWC**.`,`POCSO **Section 19 binds the doctor**; Section 21 punishes silence.`,`NPEW **2001 is still in force**.`,`Maternity: **26 weeks**, 12 from third child; creche at 50.`,`IPC 304B and 498A are now **BNS 80 and 85-86**.`]},master:{heading:`Master revision table - Legislation for children and women`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Child = below 18** (JJ Act, POCSO, UNCRC ratified 1992); Child Labour Act: **under 14 child, 14-18 adolescent**; constitutional base **Arts 15(3), 21A, 23, 24, 39(e)(f), 45, 47**.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Child laws**: JJ 2015, POCSO 2012, Child Labour 2016, PCMA 2006, RTE 2009, IMS Act; **women's laws**: PWDVA 2005, POSH 2013, Dowry 1961, Maternity Benefit 2017, MTP 2021, PCPNDT, criminal law (**BNS from 1 July 2024**).`],[`JJ Act structure`,`**Child in conflict with law -> JJ Board** (magistrate + 2 social workers, 1 woman; inquiry 4 months); **CNCP -> CWC** (chair + 4; adoption-free declaration); offences petty/serious/**heinous (min 7 y)** - 16-18 heinous: **preliminary assessment in 3 months**.`],[`JJ bars and disposals`,`**No death, no life without release, no lock-up/jail, no joint trial, no identity disclosure**; produce within **24 h**; probation up to 3 years, special home up to 3 years; after-care 18-21 (to 23); **2021 amendment - DM issues adoption orders**.`],[`POCSO`,`Gender-neutral, consent irrelevant under 18; penetrative assault **min 10 y (20 if under 16)**, aggravated **20 y to life/death**; **S19 mandatory reporting by every person**, **S21 up to 6 months** (1 year head of institution); **S27 exam by woman doctor for a girl**, no FIR needed; trial within 1 year.`],[`Child labour, marriage, education`,`CALPRA 2016 - total ban under 14 (family enterprise after school allowed), adolescents out of hazardous work; **PCMA 18/21**, voidable, void if trafficked; **Independent Thought 2017**; **RTE 6-14, 25% seats**; **IMS Act - no promotion to 2 years**.`],[`Women - PWDVA and POSH`,`**PWDVA civil law**, physical/sexual/verbal/emotional/**economic** abuse; protection and **residence orders**; DIR by Protection Officer. **POSH: ICC at 10+ employees**, senior woman presiding, half women, NGO member; complaint 3 months, inquiry 90 days.`],[`Dowry and criminal law`,`Dowry - min 5 years; **dowry death within 7 years of marriage - IPC 304B now BNS 80**; cruelty **498A now BNS 85-86**; **CLA 2013** - refusal of free treatment to victims is an offence; 2018 - death for rape of girl under 12.`],[`Maternity and MTP`,`**Maternity Benefit 2017: 26 weeks** (first two), 12 weeks third/adoption, **creche at 50+**; now under **SS Code (in force 21 November 2025)**; **MTP 2021: 20 w one RMP, 20-24 w two (special categories), beyond 24 Medical Board**; S5A confidentiality.`],[`Policy and schemes`,`**NPEW 2001 still in force** (2016 draft never notified); **Mission Vatsalya** (CWC, JJB, DCPU, **1098** with 112); **Mission Shakti** (Sambal - OSC, **181**, BBBP; Samarthya - PMMVY Rs 5,000, 6,000 for second girl); POSHAN 2.0.`],[`Family physician duties`,`**POCSO report - no discretion**; pregnant girl under 18 = POCSO report (identity may be withheld for MTP, SC 2022); **free treatment** to survivors; abandoned child to 1098/CWC **within 24 h**; DV - medical aid, OSC/181, **police reporting her choice**.`],[`Medico-legal care`,`Consent from child **12+** else guardian; woman doctor for a girl; body diagram, chain of custody, verbatim history; **never certify 'rape'** - record findings.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`NCRB Crime in India; **NFHS-5 23.3% women 20-24 married before 18**, about 29-30% spousal violence; JJB, CWC, OSC functioning; POCSO court pendency.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Signs of sexual abuse; injuries inconsistent with history; pregnancy under 18; married girl under 18; child with non-guardian/trafficking signs; repeated unexplained injuries in a woman; child with occupational injury.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`No change needed - **BNS 2024** and **Labour Codes 2025** already incorporated.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Two streams, two bodies - JJB and CWC never cross; POCSO S19 binds the doctor.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Suspected sexual abuse of a child in the OPD`,steps:[{label:`Disclosure, injury, STI or pregnancy under 18`,detail:`Treat urgent needs first, free of cost`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Report to police/SJPU - POCSO S19`,detail:`Mandatory, no exemption; failure up to 6 months`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Examination with consent`,detail:`Girl by woman doctor; trusted adult present; no FIR needed`},{label:`Document and preserve evidence`,detail:`Verbatim history, body diagram, chain of custody`},{label:`Link to CWC, counselling, follow-up`,detail:`Police inform CWC and Special Court within 24 h`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`JJ Board against Child Welfare Committee`,columns:[`Feature`,`JJ Board`,`CWC`],rows:[[`For`,`Child in conflict with law`,`Child in need of care and protection`],[`Members`,`Magistrate + 2 social workers (1 woman)`,`Chair + 4 (1 woman, 1 child expert)`],[`Key power`,`Inquiry in 4 months; preliminary assessment 16-18 heinous`,`Final authority; declares free for adoption`],[`Placement`,`Observation home, special home, place of safety`,`Children's home, foster care, sponsorship`]]}]},"preventive-concept-health-determinants":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**WHO 1948**: a state of complete physical, mental and social well-being, not merely absence of disease.`,`The WHO Constitution came into force on **7 April 1948** (World Health Day).`,`Operational addition: able to lead a **socially and economically productive life**.`,`Criticisms: 'complete' is **unattainable, static and unmeasurable**.`,`**Huber (2011)**: health is the ability to adapt and to self-manage.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Concepts: **biomedical, ecological, psychosocial and holistic**.`,`Dimensions: **physical, mental, social and spiritual**, plus emotional and vocational.`,`**Positive health** is an ideal; the **spectrum of health** is the working model.`,`Indicators: mortality, morbidity, disability, nutritional, health-care delivery, utilisation, composite.`]},{title:`Key concepts`,points:[`**Standard of living** is the usual scale of expenditure.`,`**Level of living** (UN) has 9 components, from health to human rights.`,`**Spectrum of health** runs from positive health to death and is dynamic.`,`**Iceberg of disease**: subclinical and undiagnosed cases lie below; justification for screening.`,`Large submerged part: **hypertension, diabetes, anaemia**; almost none in **rabies**.`,`**Quality of life (WHO)** is the person's own perception of their position in life.`]},{title:`Determinants of health`,points:[`**Biological**: genes, age and sex.`,`**Behavioural**: tobacco, alcohol, diet and inactivity; dominant drivers of NCDs.`,`**Environmental**: water, sanitation, housing, air pollution and cooking fuel.`,`**Socio-economic**: income, occupation and especially **female literacy**.`,`**Health services**: access, affordability and quality.`,`In India, **caste, gender and rural residence** are key structural determinants.`]},{title:`Social determinants`,points:[`Conditions in which people are **born, grow, live, work and age**.`,`Marmot called them **the causes of the causes**.`,`**CSDH 2008**: improve daily living conditions and tackle unequal **power, money and resources**.`,`**Dahlgren-Whitehead rainbow**: individual core, then lifestyle, community, living conditions, society.`,`**Rio Declaration 2011** committed states to **Health in All Policies**.`,`Health is a **State subject**; family planning is on the **Concurrent List**.`]},{title:`Indicators and indices`,points:[`An ideal indicator is **valid, reliable, sensitive, specific, feasible and relevant**.`,`**IMR is the most sensitive single indicator**: India **25 (SRS 2023)**.`,`MMR **88 per lakh** (SRS 2021-23); life expectancy **70.0 years** (2016-20).`,`**PQLI** = IMR, life expectancy at age 1 and literacy, scaled 0-100.`,`**HDI** = geometric mean of health, education and income; India **0.685, rank 130**.`,`**DALY = YLL + YLD**; one DALY is one lost healthy year.`,`**Sullivan index** = life expectancy minus duration of disability.`]},{title:`Health for All and SDG 3`,points:[`**Alma-Ata 1978** launched primary health care for Health for All by 2000.`,`**Astana 2018** linked primary health care to UHC.`,`SDG 3 by 2030: **MMR below 70**, NMR 12 or less, U5MR 25 or less.`,`SDG 3 also aims to cut **premature NCD mortality by one-third**.`,`NHP 2017: health spending **2.5% of GDP** by 2025.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`**Take a social history every time**: income, housing, fuel, violence.`,`**Link patients to entitlements**: PM-JAY, PMMVY, Ni-kshay Poshan, ration card.`,`Keep a **family folder** for every household.`,`Practise **proportionate universalism**: care for all, more for the disadvantaged.`,`Out-of-pocket spending is **43.4%** of total health expenditure (2022-23).`]}],tables:[{heading:`Composite indices`,columns:[`Index`,`Components`,`Range`,`Origin`],rows:[[`PQLI`,`IMR, life expectancy at 1, literacy`,`0-100, simple average`,`Morris 1979`],[`HDI`,`Life expectancy, schooling, GNI per capita`,`0-1, geometric mean`,`UNDP 1990`],[`DALY`,`YLL plus YLD`,`1 = one healthy year lost`,`World Bank and WHO 1993`],[`QALY`,`Life years x utility weight`,`0 (death) to 1`,`Health economics`],[`WHOQOL-BREF`,`26 items, 4 domains`,`Domain scores`,`WHO 1998`]]},{heading:`Indicators of health (Park)`,columns:[`Group`,`Examples`,`Comment`],rows:[[`Mortality`,`CDR, IMR, MMR, life expectancy`,`IMR most sensitive`],[`Morbidity`,`Incidence, prevalence, admissions`,`Burden that does not kill`],[`Disability`,`Work-loss days, Sullivan index, DALY`,`Quality of life`],[`Nutritional`,`Under-five anthropometry, low birth weight`,`Food and infection`],[`Health-care delivery`,`Doctor-population, population-bed ratio`,`Inputs, not outcomes`],[`Utilisation`,`Immunisation, institutional births`,`Whether services are used`],[`Composite`,`PQLI, HDI, MPI`,`Several dimensions in one`]]},{heading:`Acting on each determinant`,columns:[`Determinant`,`Action`,`Programme link`],rows:[[`Biological`,`Genetic counselling, newborn screening`,`RBSK, NP-NCD`],[`Behavioural`,`Brief advice, lifestyle counselling`,`NTCP quitline`],[`Environmental`,`Safe water, clean fuel`,`Jal Jeevan Mission, PMUY`],[`Socio-economic`,`Enrol in entitlements`,`PM-JAY, PDS, PMMVY`],[`Gender and violence`,`Private screening, safety plan`,`One Stop Centre, 181`]]}],remember:[`Quote **WHO 1948 verbatim**, then the criticisms.`,`**IMR** is the most sensitive indicator of community health.`,`**PQLI = IMR + life expectancy at 1 + literacy.**`,`**DALY = YLL + YLD.**`,`Social determinants are **the causes of the causes**.`,`SDG 3: **MMR below 70, NMR 12, U5MR 25**.`]},updated:[`SRS 2023 / SRS 2021-23: IMR 25, U5MR 29, MMR 88, CDR 6.4 - replaces IMR 27, U5MR 31, MMR 93 (SRS 2021)`,`NFHS-6 (2023-24): institutional births 90.6%, stunting 29.3%, wasting 19.0%, TFR 2.0 for district comparison (anaemia still NFHS-5)`,`NHA 2022-23: out-of-pocket 43.4% of total health expenditure cited as structural determinant`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Concept of health, determinants and indicators`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**WHO 1948**: 'a state of complete physical, mental and social well-being and not merely the absence of disease or infirmity'; operational - **socially and economically productive life**; Huber 2011 - **ability to adapt and self-manage**.`],[`Criticisms`,`**'Complete' unattainable/unmeasurable, static, medicalising**.`],[`Classification - concepts and dimensions`,`Concepts: **biomedical, ecological, psychosocial, holistic**; dimensions: **physical, mental, social, spiritual** + emotional, vocational, others; positive health = ideal.`],[`Key concepts`,`**Spectrum of health** (dynamic); **iceberg** (large submerged - hypertension, diabetes, anaemia; none in rabies); standard vs **level of living (9 UN components)**; WHO QOL definition.`],[`Determinants`,`**Biological, behavioural, environmental, socio-economic, health services**, + ageing, gender, ICT; **Lalonde 1974**; **Dahlgren-Whitehead rainbow**; **SDH = conditions where people are born, grow, live, work, age** - 'causes of the causes'.`],[`SDH frameworks`,`**CSDH 2008 (Marmot)** - improve daily living, tackle unequal power/money/resources, measure; **Solar-Irwin** structural -> intermediary; **Rio 2011 Health in All Policies**; India - caste, gender, rural, **OOPE 43.4% of THE (2022-23)**.`],[`Indicator classification`,`**Mortality, morbidity, disability, nutritional, health-care delivery, utilisation, social/mental, environmental, socio-economic, policy, QOL, composite**; ideal indicator valid, reliable, sensitive, specific, feasible, relevant.`],[`Mortality figures`,`**IMR 25, U5MR 29 (SRS 2023)**, NMR about 19; **MMR 88 (SRS 2021-23)**; **CDR 6.4 (2023)**; life expectancy 70.0 (2016-20).`],[`Composite indices`,`**PQLI** = IMR + LE at 1 + literacy (0-100); **HDI** = geometric mean of LE, schooling, GNI PPP - **India 0.685, rank 130/193 (HDR 2025)**; **DALY = YLL + YLD**; QALY; HALE; Sullivan index; MPI.`],[`Global framework`,`**HFA 2000 (WHA 30.43, 1977)**, **Alma-Ata 1978**, **Astana 2018**; **SDG 3**: MMR under 70, NMR 12, U5MR 25, NCD premature deaths down one-third, UHC; NHP 2017 targets; SDG India Index.`],[`Responsibility`,`Individual, community (VHSNC, RKS, MAS), State (**Article 47**), international (WHO, IHR); **health = State List; family planning = Concurrent List**.`],[`Family physician role`,`**Social history every time**; link to entitlements (PM-JAY, PMMVY, Ni-kshay Poshan); family folder; **proportionate universalism**; screen the iceberg (30+ NP-NCD); advocate locally.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`BP/diabetes control, immunisation, 4+ ANC; compare with **NFHS-6 district fact sheets (institutional births 90.6%, stunting 29.3%)**; WHOQOL-BREF or EQ-5D in chronic care.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`SAM in food-insecure home; domestic violence or child abuse; suicidal ideation after debt or crop failure; diarrhoea/fever cluster; catastrophic spending stopping treatment; elderly alone unable to do ADLs.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`Data refresh - **SRS 2023, MMR 88 (2021-23), NFHS-6 (2023-24), NHA 2022-23**.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`IMR is the most sensitive indicator of community health; PQLI measures results not income.`]]},flows:[{kind:`branch`,heading:`Determinants of health - from upstream to the person`,root:`Health of a person`,arms:[{label:`Structural (upstream)`,tone:`warn`,steps:[`Income, education, occupation`,`Caste, gender, political context`,`Catastrophic out-of-pocket spending`]},{label:`Environmental`,steps:[`Water, sanitation, housing`,`Cooking fuel, air pollution, vectors`]},{label:`Behavioural`,steps:[`Tobacco, alcohol, diet, inactivity`,`Sexual behaviour`]},{label:`Biological and health services`,steps:[`Genes, age, sex`,`Access, affordability, quality of care`]}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Composite indices`,columns:[`Index`,`Components`,`Scale`],rows:[[`PQLI`,`IMR, life expectancy at 1, literacy`,`0-100, simple average`],[`HDI`,`LE at birth, schooling, GNI per capita PPP`,`0-1, geometric mean; India 0.685`],[`DALY`,`YLL + YLD`,`1 DALY = 1 healthy year lost`],[`QALY`,`Life years x utility`,`0 death to 1 full health`]]}]},"preventive-health-care-delivery-nhm":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Primary health care (Alma-Ata 1978)** is essential care, universally accessible, at affordable cost.`,`It is the **first level of contact** with the health system.`,`Four principles: **equitable distribution, community participation, intersectoral coordination, appropriate technology**.`,`Eight elements by the mnemonic **ELEMENTS**.`,`**Astana 2018** renewed PHC as the cornerstone of UHC and the SDGs.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Primary level**: sub-health centre and PHC.`,`**Secondary level**: CHC, sub-district and district hospital.`,`**Tertiary level**: medical colleges and specialised institutes.`,`**Selective PHC (GOBI-FFF)** was criticised for narrowing comprehensive PHC.`]},{title:`How the system evolved`,points:[`**Bhore Committee (1946)** proposed the integrated three-tier system and the PHC.`,`**Kartar Singh Committee (1973)** created the **multipurpose worker**.`,`**NHP 1983** was India's response to Alma-Ata; NHP 2002 and 2017 followed.`,`India has **no gatekeeping**, so tertiary hospitals overcrowd while PHCs are under-used.`,`CHCs lack about **80% of required specialists** (RHS 2022-23).`]},{title:`The three tiers (IPHS 2022)`,points:[`**ASHA** one per **1000**; selected by the gram sabha.`,`**SHC-AAM** per **5000 plains, 3000 hilly**; led by a **Community Health Officer**.`,`**PHC** per **30,000 or 20,000**; MBBS officer and **6 beds**.`,`**CHC** per **1,20,000 or 80,000**; four specialists plus anaesthetist and **30 beds**.`,`IPHS 2022 defines **essential and desirable** services for the 12 AAM packages.`,`Free drugs: about **105 at SHC-AAM and 172 at PHC-AAM**.`]},{title:`Frontline workers and FRU`,points:[`ASHA is **activist, facilitator and provider**; one facilitator per 20 ASHAs.`,`ASHA does **HBNC and HBYC** home visits and fills the CBAC form.`,`CHO is a BSc Nursing or BAMS graduate with a **6-month certificate**.`,`**FRU** needs **caesarean section, newborn care and 24-hour blood storage**.`,`FRU norm is **one per 5 lakh**; missing one criterion means not an FRU.`,`MBBS doctors are trained in **EmOC (16 weeks)** and **LSAS (18 weeks)**.`]},{title:`National Health Mission`,points:[`**NRHM** launched **12 April 2005**; **NUHM** in 2013; together the NHM.`,`NHM was extended for **another five years in January 2025**.`,`Core strategies: ASHA, IPHS upgrade, **district PIPs, untied funds**, Rogi Kalyan Samiti.`,`Funding **60:40**, or **90:10** for north-eastern and Himalayan states.`,`Schemes include **JSY, JSSK, RBSK, RKSK, 108 ambulances** and dialysis.`,`Urban: **UPHC per 50,000**; Mahila Arogya Samiti in slums.`]},{title:`Quality and finance`,points:[`**NQAS** certification and **Kayakalp** awards measure quality.`,`**LaQshya** improves labour rooms; **MusQan** gives child-friendly care.`,`Out-of-pocket spending was **43.4%** of total health expenditure in 2022-23.`,`**NHP 2017**: 2.5% of GDP by 2025; **two-thirds to primary care**.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Eclampsia or PPH: stabilise and send by **108 to the nearest functional FRU**.`,`Sick newborn goes to the **SNCU or NBSU**.`,`Give **return referral notes** and keep family folders.`,`Judge an FRU by **caesareans and blood units issued per month**.`,`Caesarean rate in WHO acceptable range is **5-15%**.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Public health facilities (IPHS 2022)`,columns:[`Facility`,`Population plains or hilly`,`Staff and beds`,`Refers to`],rows:[[`ASHA`,`1 per 1000`,`Village woman, incentives`,`SHC-AAM`],[`SHC-AAM`,`5000 or 3000`,`CHO, MPW female and male`,`PHC`],[`PHC-AAM`,`30,000 or 20,000`,`MBBS MO, nurses; 6 beds`,`CHC or FRU`],[`CHC`,`1,20,000 or 80,000`,`Four specialists, anaesthetist; 30 beds`,`District hospital`],[`FRU`,`1 per 5 lakh`,`Obstetrician, anaesthetist, blood storage`,`District hospital`],[`UPHC`,`50,000 urban`,`MO, nurses, lab`,`UCHC`]]},{heading:`NRHM and NUHM`,columns:[`Feature`,`NRHM`,`NUHM`],rows:[[`Launched`,`12 April 2005`,`2013`],[`Population`,`Rural; 18 high-focus states`,`Towns above 50,000`],[`Primary unit`,`SHC and PHC`,`UPHC, Urban AAM`],[`Community link`,`ASHA; VHSNC`,`Urban ASHA; Mahila Arogya Samiti`]]},{heading:`Committees that shaped the system`,columns:[`Committee`,`Year`,`Key contribution`],rows:[[`Bhore`,`1946`,`Three-tier system and PHC`],[`Mudaliar`,`1962`,`Consolidate existing PHCs`],[`Kartar Singh`,`1973`,`Multipurpose workers`],[`Srivastava`,`1975`,`Village health guides, referral chain`]]}],remember:[`Alma-Ata: **equity, participation, intersectoral, appropriate technology**.`,`Mnemonic **ELEMENTS** for the eight elements.`,`Norms: **5000, 30,000, 1,20,000** (3000, 20,000, 80,000 hilly).`,`FRU = **caesarean + newborn care + 24-hour blood**.`,`NRHM **12 April 2005**; NUHM 2013.`,`Bhore 1946 three-tier; **Kartar Singh 1973** multipurpose worker.`]},updated:[`MoHFW 2025: Union Cabinet (January 2025) approved continuation of NHM for another five years - replaces 'extended to March 2026'`,`NHA 2022-23: OOPE 43.4% of THE, GHE about 1.4-1.5% of GDP - replaces 39% (2021-22)`,`NFHS-6: institutional delivery 90.6% as outcome indicator`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Health care delivery, NHM and IPHS`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**PHC (Alma-Ata 1978)** = essential care, practical and acceptable methods, universally accessible, full community participation, affordable - **first level of contact**.`],[`Principles and elements`,`**Equitable distribution, community participation, intersectoral coordination, appropriate technology**; 8 elements **ELEMENTS**; selective PHC (**GOBI-FFF**); **Astana 2018** - PHC for UHC and SDGs.`],[`Classification - levels`,`**Primary** (SHC, PHC), **secondary** (CHC, SDH, DH), **tertiary** (medical colleges); referral up and return referral down; no gatekeeping in India.`],[`IPHS 2022 norms`,`**ASHA 1/1000; SHC-AAM 5,000/3,000; PHC 30,000/20,000 (6 beds); CHC 1,20,000/80,000 (30 beds, 4 specialists + anaesthetist)**; UPHC 50,000; Urban AAM 15-20,000; essential vs desirable services.`],[`Frontline workers`,`**ASHA** - married/widowed woman 25-45, gram sabha selection, activist-facilitator-provider, drug kit, HBNC/HBYC, CBAC; **CHO** - BSc Nursing/BAMS + 6-month certificate, eSanjeevani; ASHA facilitator 1:20.`],[`FRU`,`**Caesarean + newborn care + 24-hour blood storage** - all three; **1 per 5 lakh**; EmOC 16 weeks and **LSAS 18 weeks** training for MBBS doctors.`],[`History`,`**Bhore 1946** (3-tier), Mudaliar 1962, **Kartar Singh 1973** (MPW pair; 16 SCs per PHC of 50,000), Srivastava 1975; NHP 1983, 2002, **2017**.`],[`NHM`,`**NRHM 12 April 2005** (18 high-focus states) + **NUHM 2013** = NHM; **continuation for another five years approved January 2025**; components RMNCH+A, CD, NCD, HSS; PIPs, untied funds, RKS, VHSNC, **60:40 (90:10)**.`],[`Flagship schemes`,`JSY 2005, JSSK 2011, RBSK/RKSK, 102/108, MMUs, **PM National Dialysis Programme 2016**, Free Drugs (**105 SHC, 172 PHC**) and Diagnostics (**14, 63 tests**); PM-ABHIM; NQAS, Kayakalp, LaQshya, MusQan.`],[`Gaps`,`**Specialist shortfall about 80% at CHCs** (RHS 2022-23); vertical fragmentation; urban poor without primary entry; **OOPE 43.4% of THE (NHA 2022-23)**.`],[`Family physician role`,`First-contact, continuing, comprehensive, coordinating; mentor ASHA/ANM/CHO; refer eclampsia/PPH by 108 to a **functional FRU**; return referral; ensure free entitlements; fill CHC specialist gap; report on HMIS/IHIP.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`RHS shortfall; HMIS OPD/IPD, bed occupancy, **C-section 5-15% at FRUs**; FRU judged by **C-sections and blood units per month**; CRM, NQAS; NHA; outcome IMR, MMR, TFR, **institutional delivery 90.6% (NFHS-6)**.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`PPH/eclampsia at SHC - stabilise (MgSO4, oxytocin) and send to FRU; newborn danger signs; ASHA-reported cluster; 'FRU' without blood; informal payments.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**NHM continued for another 5 years (Cabinet, January 2025)**; **NHA 2022-23 OOPE 43.4%**; **NFHS-6 institutional births 90.6%**.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`FRU = caesarean + newborn care + blood storage - missing one means not an FRU.`]]},flows:[{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Three-tier ladder with population norms (plains / hilly-tribal)`,steps:[{label:`ASHA - village`,detail:`1 per 1,000; HBNC, CBAC, drug kit`},{label:`SHC-Ayushman Arogya Mandir`,detail:`5,000 / 3,000; CHO, MPW-F, MPW-M; 12 packages`},{label:`PHC-AAM`,detail:`30,000 / 20,000; MO, 6 beds, 24x7 delivery`},{label:`CHC (FRU if blood storage)`,detail:`1,20,000 / 80,000; 30 beds; specialists`},{label:`Sub-district / district hospital`,detail:`Secondary care; SNCU, blood bank, DEIC`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`flow`,heading:`Obstetric emergency at a sub-centre - referral chain`,steps:[{label:`PPH or eclampsia recognised`,detail:`Bleeding, fits, BP 160/110`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Stabilise before transfer`,detail:`Oxytocin 10 IU IM; MgSO4 loading 4 g IV + 10 g IM; IV line`},{label:`Call 108; phone the facility`,detail:`Choose a functional FRU, not the nearest board`,tone:`decision`},{label:`FRU: caesarean, anaesthesia, blood`,detail:`All three available 24x7`},{label:`Return referral to SHC team`,detail:`Follow-up, contraception, MDSR if death`,tone:`good`}]}]},"preventive-ayushman-bharat":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Universal health coverage**: everyone gets needed quality care **without financial hardship**.`,`**Ayushman Bharat** was announced in the **Union Budget 2018-19** to deliver the NHP 2017 vision.`,`First HWC opened at **Jangla, Bijapur, on 14 April 2018**.`,`**PM-JAY** was launched at **Ranchi on 23 September 2018**.`,`Hospitalisation was the largest driver of **catastrophic out-of-pocket spending**.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Two pillars**: Ayushman Arogya Mandir (primary care) and PM-JAY (hospital care).`,`**Two enablers**: ABDM (digital) and PM-ABHIM (infrastructure), both 2021.`,`It shifts care from selective to **comprehensive primary health care**.`,`PM-JAY replaced fragmented insurance such as **RSBY 2008** (Rs 30,000 cover).`]},{title:`Key concepts`,points:[`**Continuum of care**: AAM to PHC, CHC or hospital and back, linked by ABHA.`,`**Population empanelment**: everyone 30 and above completes the **CBAC** with the ASHA.`,`**Task sharing**: the medical officer starts treatment; the **CHO continues** it in the village.`,`**Strategic purchasing** buys care at **bundled package rates**.`,`PM-JAY covers **only in-patient care**, while outpatient drugs drive most out-of-pocket spending.`]},{title:`Ayushman Arogya Mandir`,points:[`Sub-centres and PHCs were upgraded; renamed **Ayushman Arogya Mandir in 2023**.`,`Over **1.75 lakh** are now functional.`,`**12 service packages**: RCH, disease control, NCDs, oral, eye, ENT, elderly, emergency, mental health.`,`Led by a **Community Health Officer** with fixed salary plus performance pay.`,`**eSanjeevani** teleconsultation has crossed 30 crore consultations.`,`**Jan Arogya Samitis** manage untied funds; wellness includes yoga and tobacco cessation.`]},{title:`PM-JAY`,points:[`**Rs 5 lakh per family per year**, cashless and paperless.`,`Eligibility from **SECC 2011** deprivation and occupational criteria; about **12 crore families**.`,`**No cap on family size or age**; pre-existing conditions covered from day one.`,`Covers **3 days before and 15 days after** hospitalisation; nationally portable.`,`**Health Benefit Package 2022**: about 1949 procedures in 27 specialities.`,`Run by the **National Health Authority** and State Health Agencies; helpline **14555**.`]},{title:`Vay Vandana, ABDM and PM-ABHIM`,points:[`**Vay Vandana (29 October 2024)**: all aged **70 and above**, regardless of income.`,`Eligible families get an **extra Rs 5 lakh top-up** for members 70 and above.`,`**ABDM** gives a **14-digit ABHA**; records stay federated and move only with consent.`,`**PM-ABHIM** (Rs 64,180 crore) builds IPHLs, BPHUs and **critical care blocks**.`,`PM-ABHIM also creates the **National Institute for One Health**.`]},{title:`Criticisms and indicators`,points:[`**No outpatient cover** under PM-JAY.`,`**Uneven private empanelment**, concentrated in southern and western cities.`,`**Dated SECC 2011** lists exclude some poor families.`,`Out-of-pocket share fell to 39.4% in 2021-22 but rose to **43.4% in 2022-23**.`,`AAM performance depends on **CHO posts, supply chains and diagnostics**.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Register on the **Health Professionals and Health Facility Registries**.`,`**Check eligibility** and refer to empanelled hospitals.`,`Help everyone 70 and over create the **Vay Vandana card** before admission.`,`Trace **CBAC screen-positives** not yet confirmed or treated.`,`Report any **informal charge** for a covered package to **14555**.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Ayushman Bharat components`,columns:[`Component`,`Launched`,`Provides`,`Family physician action`],rows:[[`Ayushman Arogya Mandir`,`April 2018, renamed 2023`,`12 packages, free drugs and tests`,`Teleconsultation hub, trace positives`],[`PM-JAY`,`23 September 2018`,`Rs 5 lakh per family per year`,`Check eligibility, refer, follow up`],[`Vay Vandana`,`29 October 2024`,`Cover for all aged 70+`,`Help e-KYC and card creation`],[`ABDM`,`27 September 2021`,`ABHA, registries, consent exchange`,`Register, create ABHA`],[`PM-ABHIM`,`25 October 2021`,`IPHLs, BPHUs, critical care blocks`,`Use IPHL tests; report to IHIP`]]},{heading:`The 12 AAM service packages`,columns:[`Packages`,`Services`,`Phase`],rows:[[`1-4`,`Pregnancy, newborn, child and adolescent, family planning`,`First`],[`5-7`,`Communicable disease, acute illness, NCD screening`,`First`],[`8-9`,`Oral health, eye and ENT`,`Later`],[`10-12`,`Elderly and palliative, emergency, mental health`,`Later`]]},{heading:`Free drugs and tests at AAMs`,columns:[`Level`,`Drugs`,`Tests`],rows:[[`SHC-AAM`,`About 105`,`About 14`],[`PHC-AAM`,`About 172`,`About 63`],[`Team at SHC-AAM`,`CHO, two MPWs, 5-6 ASHAs`,`Point-of-care tests`]]}],remember:[`Two pillars: **AAM and PM-JAY**; enablers **ABDM and PM-ABHIM**.`,`**Rs 5 lakh** per family per year; no cap on size or age.`,`**Jangla, Bijapur, 14 April 2018**; Ranchi, 23 September 2018.`,`**Vay Vandana**: 70 and above from 29 October 2024.`,`ABHA is **14 digits**.`,`Main criticism: **no outpatient cover**.`]},updated:[`NHA 2022-23 (May 2026): OOPE share rose to 43.4% of THE after the 2021-22 low of 39.4% - replaces 'about 39%' as the financial protection indicator`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Ayushman Bharat (AAM, PM-JAY, ABDM, PM-ABHIM)`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`India's flagship step to **UHC** (care without financial hardship), announced **Budget 2018-19** to deliver NHP 2017.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Two pillars**: **Ayushman Arogya Mandir** (primary care) + **PM-JAY** (hospital cover); **two enablers**: **ABDM** (digital, 2021) + **PM-ABHIM** (infrastructure, 2021).`],[`Milestones`,`First HWC **Jangla, Bijapur 14 April 2018**; PM-JAY **Ranchi 23 September 2018**; HWCs **renamed AAM 2023**; **Vay Vandana 29 October 2024** (all 70+); ABDM **27 September 2021**; PM-ABHIM **25 October 2021**.`],[`Concepts`,`Continuum of care AAM -> PHC/CHC/empanelled hospital -> back, linked by **ABHA**; **population empanelment + CBAC for 30+**; protocol-based care with **CHO** continuing treatment; **strategic purchasing** with package rates.`],[`AAM - 12 packages`,`1-4 pregnancy, newborn/infant, child/adolescent, family planning; 5-7 communicable disease, acute OPD, **NCD screening and management**; 8-12 oral, eye/ENT, **elderly and palliative**, emergency, **mental health**.`],[`AAM delivery`,`Over **1.75 lakh** functional; CHO + 2 MPWs + 5-6 ASHAs; **105 drugs/14 tests (SHC), 172/63 (PHC)**; wellness (yoga, Eat Right); **Jan Arogya Samiti**; **eSanjeevani** 30 crore+ consultations.`],[`PM-JAY`,`**Rs 5 lakh/family/year**, cashless, paperless, **portable**; **SECC 2011 D1-D5, D7 + 11 urban occupational categories**, 12 crore families (about 55 crore people); **no cap on family size/age; pre-existing covered day 1**; 3 days pre, 15 days post.`],[`PM-JAY operations`,`**HBP 2022: about 1,949 procedures, 27 specialities**; NHA + SHAs in trust/insurance/mixed mode; **Arogya Mitras**; **60:40 (90:10)**; cards 40 crore+, admissions 8 crore+ (2025); helpline **14555**; anti-fraud AI unit.`],[`Vay Vandana`,`All **70+ regardless of income**; extra **Rs 5 lakh top-up** shared by 70+ members in eligible families or new Rs 5 lakh cover; CGHS/ECHS/CAPF users choose one.`],[`ABDM and PM-ABHIM`,`**14-digit ABHA**, HPR, HFR, consent manager, HIE - **federated**, **DPDP Act 2023**; PM-ABHIM **Rs 64,180 crore (2021-26)**: 17,788 rural SHC-AAMs, 11,024 urban AAMs, **BPHUs, IPHLs in all districts**, critical care blocks (districts over 5 lakh), NIOH.`],[`Financial protection`,`**OOPE 64% (2013-14) -> 39.4% (2021-22) -> 43.4% (2022-23)**; main criticism - **PM-JAY is in-patient only**, while OPD drugs and tests drive most OOPE.`],[`Family physician role`,`Register on HPR/HFR, create ABHA with consent; empanel; check eligibility and refer; help 70+ get Vay Vandana card; post-discharge follow-up; eSanjeevani hub; trace CBAC positives; report charges to 14555.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`AAM: functional number, **30+ screening coverage**, footfall, teleconsultations, drug/test availability; PM-JAY: cards, admissions, **claim settlement time**, public vs private share, fraud flags; outcome OOPE and catastrophic spending.`],[`Criticisms`,`In-patient only; uneven private empanelment; low package rates; dated SECC 2011 lists; awareness; fraud; CHO vacancies and stock-outs.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Beneficiary asked to pay; CBAC positive not confirmed for weeks; 70+ admitted without card; AAM stock-out of antihypertensives/insulin; cluster of identical high-value procedures.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`Data refresh - **NHA 2022-23 OOPE 43.4%**.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Two pillars and two enablers; PM-JAY Rs 5 lakh per family per year, no cap on family size or age.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Continuum of care under Ayushman Bharat`,steps:[{label:`ASHA enumerates household; CBAC for 30+`,detail:`Population empanelment at SHC-AAM`},{label:`CHO screens and treats by protocol`,detail:`BP, glucose, OVE, CBE, VIA; free drugs and tests`},{label:`Needs a doctor or admission?`,detail:`eSanjeevani teleconsultation; PHC MO; refer`,tone:`decision`},{label:`PM-JAY empanelled hospital`,detail:`Cashless Rs 5 lakh/family/year; Vay Vandana for 70+`},{label:`Back to AAM with ABHA-linked record`,detail:`CHO continues protocol and follow-up`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`branch`,heading:`Ayushman Bharat - pillars and enablers`,root:`Ayushman Bharat (2018) - towards UHC`,arms:[{label:`Ayushman Arogya Mandir`,tone:`good`,steps:[`Comprehensive primary care`,`12 packages, free drugs and tests`,`CHO-led, eSanjeevani`]},{label:`PM-JAY`,steps:[`Rs 5 lakh/family/year hospital cover`,`SECC 2011 + all aged 70+ (2024)`,`Helpline 14555`]},{label:`ABDM`,steps:[`14-digit ABHA`,`HPR, HFR, consent manager`]},{label:`PM-ABHIM`,steps:[`IPHLs, BPHUs, critical care blocks`,`Rs 64,180 crore, 2021-26`]}]}]},"preventive-infant-child-mortality":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**IMR** = deaths under 1 year per 1000 live births in the same year.`,`IMR is the **most sensitive single indicator** of community health and level of living.`,`India (SRS 2023): **IMR 25, U5MR 29**; NMR about **19** (2022).`,`Kerala IMR **5**; Madhya Pradesh, Chhattisgarh and Uttar Pradesh **37**.`,`**Neonatal deaths are about 70%** of infant deaths.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**NMR**: deaths under 28 days; early neonatal 0-6 days, late 7-27 days.`,`**Post-neonatal**: 28 days to under 1 year; reflects environment and infection.`,`**Perinatal rate**: stillbirths 28 weeks+ plus deaths 0-6 days, over **total births**.`,`**Stillbirth rate** also uses total births; IMR and U5MR use live births.`,`**U5MR** is UNICEF's leading indicator.`]},{title:`Causes and risk factors`,points:[`Neonatal deaths: **prematurity and LBW, asphyxia, and sepsis or pneumonia**.`,`About **half of neonatal deaths** happen in the first 48 hours.`,`After the newborn period, **pneumonia and diarrhoea** lead; undernutrition underlies nearly half.`,`**Low birth weight** (under 2500 g, about 18%) is the strongest predictor of neonatal death.`,`Maternal risks: age under 19 or over 35, **birth interval under 2 years**, anaemia.`,`Cultural risks: **prelacteal feeds, discarding colostrum**, early bathing, son preference.`]},{title:`RMNCH+A continuum of care`,points:[`**RMNCH+A (2013)** runs a life-cycle and home-community-facility continuum.`,`Space births **3 years apart**; give antenatal corticosteroids in preterm labour.`,`Essential newborn care: dry, **delayed cord clamping**, skin-to-skin, breastfeed within 1 hour.`,`Facility tiers: **Newborn Care Corner, NBSU at FRU, SNCU at district hospital**.`,`**HBNC**: ASHA visits on days **3, 7, 14, 21, 28 and 42**.`,`**HBYC**: ASHA visits at **3, 6, 9, 12 and 15 months**.`,`WHO 2022: **immediate KMC from birth** for low birth weight babies.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Vitamin K 1 mg IM** at birth (0.5 mg if under 1000 g).`,`Antenatal **dexamethasone 6 mg IM 12-hourly x 4** at 24-34 weeks.`,`Diarrhoea: **low-osmolarity ORS plus zinc 20 mg for 14 days**.`,`Never give antidiarrhoeals to children.`,`Pneumonia: **amoxicillin 40-45 mg/kg twice daily for 5 days**.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`PSBI with no referral: **gentamicin IM once daily plus oral amoxicillin** for 7 days.`,`ASHA gives a **pre-referral dose** for chest indrawing under SAANS.`,`Plan B dehydration: **ORS 75 mL/kg over 4 hours**.`,`Vitamin A in measles on **day 1 and day 2**.`,`SAM with complications goes to the **NRC**.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Newborn not feeding or moving only on stimulation: **PSBI, refer now**.`,`**RR 60/min or more** or severe chest indrawing in a young infant.`,`Temperature **below 35.5 C or 37.5 C or above**.`,`**Jaundice in the first 24 hours**.`,`Birth weight **under 1800 g** or under 34 weeks needs facility care.`]},{title:`Prevention and programmes`,points:[`**INAP 2014**: single-digit NMR and stillbirth rate by 2030.`,`**SDG 3.2**: NMR 12 or less, U5MR 25 or less by 2030.`,`**SAANS (2019)** for pneumonia; **STOP Diarrhoea** campaign from 2024.`,`Full immunisation **76.4%**; early breastfeeding only **41.8%** (NFHS-5).`,`Institutional births **90.6%** (NFHS-6).`,`Counsel **24 months from birth to next conception**.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in newborn and child survival`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Vitamin K1`,`1 mg IM (0.5 mg if under 1000 g)`,`Once at birth`,`Local pain`],[`Dexamethasone (antenatal)`,`6 mg IM 12-hourly`,`4 doses, 24-34 weeks`,`Maternal hyperglycaemia`],[`Low-osmolarity ORS`,`50-100 mL per stool under 2 years`,`Until diarrhoea stops`,`Vomiting if given fast`],[`Zinc`,`20 mg oral daily (10 mg under 6 months)`,`14 days`,`Vomiting, metallic taste`],[`Amoxicillin (pneumonia)`,`40-45 mg/kg oral twice daily`,`5 days`,`Rash, diarrhoea`],[`Amoxicillin (PSBI)`,`50 mg/kg oral twice daily`,`7 days`,`With gentamicin`],[`Gentamicin`,`5-7.5 mg/kg IM once daily`,`2-7 days per protocol`,`Nephro- and ototoxicity`],[`Vitamin A`,`1 lakh IU at 9 months, then 2 lakh IU`,`6-monthly to 5 years`,`Bulging fontanelle`],[`IFA syrup`,`1 mL (20 mg iron) oral`,`Twice weekly, 6-59 months`,`With 6-monthly albendazole`]]},{heading:`Childhood mortality indicators`,columns:[`Indicator`,`Numerator`,`Denominator`,`India`],rows:[[`NMR`,`Deaths under 28 days`,`1000 live births`,`19 (2022)`],[`IMR`,`Deaths under 1 year`,`1000 live births`,`25 (2023)`],[`U5MR`,`Deaths under 5 years`,`1000 live births`,`29 (2023)`],[`PMR`,`Stillbirths plus deaths 0-6 days`,`1000 total births`,`SRS annual`],[`SBR`,`Fetal deaths 28 weeks+`,`1000 total births`,`Target single digit`]]},{heading:`Newborn and child programmes`,columns:[`Programme`,`Year`,`Core activity`],rows:[[`HBNC`,`2011`,`Home visits days 3 to 42`],[`INAP`,`2014`,`Single-digit NMR and SBR by 2030`],[`MAA`,`2016`,`Breastfeeding promotion`],[`HBYC`,`2018`,`Home visits 3-15 months`],[`SAANS`,`2019`,`Pneumonia detection and treatment`],[`STOP Diarrhoea`,`2024`,`ORS-zinc and WASH`]]}],remember:[`**IMR 25, U5MR 29** (SRS 2023); always quote the year.`,`Neonatal deaths are **about 70%** of infant deaths.`,`Stillbirth and perinatal rates use **total births**.`,`HBNC **3, 7, 14, 21, 28, 42**; HBYC 3, 6, 9, 12, 15 months.`,`Diarrhoea = **ORS plus zinc 20 mg for 14 days**.`,`**Kerala 5 versus MP 37** shows social determinants.`]},updated:[`SRS 2023: IMR 25 (26 in 2022), U5MR 29; Kerala 5 lowest, MP/Chhattisgarh/UP 37 highest - replaces IMR 27, U5MR 31, Kerala 6, MP 43 (SRS 2021)`,`NFHS-6 (2023-24): institutional births 90.6%, under-five stunting 29.3% added to coverage indicators`,`MoHFW 2026: single-dose HPV vaccine for girls aged 14 added to UIP immunisation point`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Infant, neonatal and under-five mortality`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**IMR** = deaths under 1 year / 1000 live births (a ratio); **NMR** under 28 days (early 0-6, late 7-27); **PNMR** 28 days-1 year; **PMR** = (SB 28 w+ + early neonatal) / (LB + SB) x 1000; **U5MR** per 1000 LB.`],[`Current figures`,`**IMR 25, U5MR 29 (SRS 2023)**, NMR about 19; **Kerala 5** vs **MP, Chhattisgarh, UP 37**; U5MR above 120 in 1990; neonatal deaths about **70%+ of infant deaths**.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Neonatal: **prematurity/LBW, asphyxia, sepsis/pneumonia** (three-quarters); half of neonatal deaths in **first 48 h**; post-neonatal: **pneumonia, diarrhoea**, measles, malaria, injuries; **undernutrition underlies half**.`],[`Determinants`,`Biological (LBW 18%, gestation, birth order), maternal (age, **interval under 2 years**, anaemia; 23.3% married before 18), service (place of birth, SNCU), socio-cultural (female literacy, prelacteals, colostrum discarded, **son preference**), environmental.`],[`RMNCH+A strategy`,`2013, life-cycle + home-community-facility continuum, **184 high-priority districts**, **5x5 matrix**; space births **3 years**; ANC 4+; **antenatal steroids 24-34 weeks**; LaQshya labour rooms.`],[`Essential newborn care`,`Dry, **delayed cord clamping 1-3 min**, skin-to-skin, **breastfeed within 1 h**, **vitamin K 1 mg IM**, no bath 24 h, **Golden Minute** resuscitation (NSSK).`],[`Facility tiers and home visits`,`**NBCC** every delivery point, **NBSU** (4 beds) FRU, **SNCU** (12+) DH, MNCU; **HBNC days 3, 7, 14, 21, 28, 42** (+ day 1 home birth); **HBYC 3, 6, 9, 12, 15 months**; **immediate KMC** for LBW (WHO 2022).`],[`Child programmes`,`IMNCI, **SAANS 2019**, **STOP Diarrhoea 2024**, MAA 2016, RBSK, Child Death Review; UIP 12 diseases incl. PCV, rotavirus; **HPV for girls 14 (2026)**; Anaemia Mukt Bharat, POSHAN 2.0, WASH, PMUY.`],[`Treatment - doses`,`**ORS** 50-100 mL/stool under 2 y, Plan B 75 mL/kg over 4 h; **zinc 20 mg x 14 days** (10 mg under 6 m); **amoxicillin 40-45 mg/kg BD x 5 days**; PSBI **gentamicin 5-7.5 mg/kg IM OD + amoxicillin 50 mg/kg BD x 7 days**; dexamethasone 6 mg IM 12-hourly x 4.`],[`Vitamin A and IFA`,`**1 lakh IU at 9 m, 2 lakh IU 6-monthly to 5 y**; IFA syrup 1 mL twice weekly 6-59 m; albendazole 200/400 mg 6-monthly.`],[`Family physician role`,`Preconception and early ANC; teach **newborn danger signs** (not feeding, convulsions, **RR 60+**, chest indrawing, **temp 37.5+ or under 35.5**); immunisation/growth register via U-WIN; **ORS + zinc, no antidiarrhoeals**; spacing 24 months; Child Death Review.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`SRS (IMR, NMR, U5MR, PMR, SBR); NFHS-6 **institutional births 90.6%**, stunting 29.3%; NFHS-5 full immunisation 76.4%, EBF 63.7%, early BF 41.8%; HBNC completion, SNCU outcomes.`],[`Targets`,`**SDG 3.2: NMR 12 or less, U5MR 25 or less by 2030**; **INAP 2014 single-digit NMR and SBR by 2030**; NHP 2017 U5MR 23, NMR 16 by 2025.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Not feeding/lethargic (PSBI); RR 60+ or severe indrawing; temp under 35.5 or 37.5+; jaundice day 1 or yellow soles; weight under 1800 g or under 34 weeks; severe dehydration; SAM.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**SRS 2023: IMR 25, U5MR 29**; **NFHS-6 coverage data**; **HPV girls 14 in UIP (2026)**.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`IMR is the most sensitive indicator; next gains are in the first week of life; always quote the SRS year.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Sick young infant at home or SHC - IMNCI logic`,steps:[{label:`Check danger signs`,detail:`Not feeding, convulsions, RR 60+, severe indrawing, temp 37.5+ or under 35.5, movement only on stimulation`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Any sign = possible serious bacterial infection`,detail:`Pre-referral gentamicin + amoxicillin; keep warm, breastfeed`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Refer to SNCU/NBSU`,detail:`108; skin-to-skin during transport`},{label:`Referral refused or impossible`,detail:`Gentamicin 5-7.5 mg/kg IM OD + oral amoxicillin 50 mg/kg BD x 7 days`},{label:`Follow-up by ASHA (HBNC)`,detail:`Days 3, 7, 14, 21, 28, 42`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Childhood mortality indicators`,columns:[`Indicator`,`Numerator`,`Denominator`,`India latest`],rows:[[`NMR`,`Deaths under 28 days`,`1000 live births`,`About 19`],[`IMR`,`Deaths under 1 year`,`1000 live births`,`25 (SRS 2023)`],[`U5MR`,`Deaths under 5 years`,`1000 live births`,`29 (SRS 2023)`],[`PMR`,`SB 28 w+ + deaths 0-6 days`,`1000 total births`,`SRS annual`],[`SBR`,`Fetal deaths 28 w+`,`1000 total births`,`INAP: single digit by 2030`]]}]},"preventive-maternal-health-mmr":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Maternal death**: death in pregnancy or **within 42 days**, from causes related to pregnancy.`,`Accidental or incidental causes are **excluded**.`,`**MMR** = maternal deaths per **1,00,000 live births**; it measures obstetric care quality.`,`India MMR **88 (SRS 2021-23)**; SDG 3.1 target is **below 70** by 2030.`,`**Maternal mortality rate** uses women aged 15-49 as the denominator.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Direct obstetric deaths**: haemorrhage, hypertension, sepsis, obstructed labour, unsafe abortion.`,`**Indirect deaths**: anaemia, heart disease, hepatitis, malaria and TB aggravated by pregnancy.`,`**Late maternal death**: after 42 days but within 1 year.`,`**Maternal near miss** is 5-10 times commoner than death and a surviving witness.`]},{title:`Causes and risk factors`,points:[`**Haemorrhage (38%)** is the commonest cause in India.`,`Sepsis 11%, abortion 8%, obstructed labour 5%, hypertensive disorders 5%.`,`**Most deaths occur within 24 hours** of birth; PPH can kill within 2 hours.`,`**Anaemia** affects 52.2% of pregnant women and amplifies every haemorrhage.`,`Highest MMR: **Assam, Madhya Pradesh, Uttar Pradesh**; lowest **Kerala 19**.`]},{title:`The three delays`,points:[`**Delay 1**: deciding to seek care, from unrecognised danger signs or cost.`,`**Delay 2**: reaching a facility, from distance and transport.`,`**Delay 3**: receiving adequate care, from no staff, blood or theatre.`,`Indian reviews repeatedly find **delay 3 and multiple referrals**.`,`About **15% of women** need emergency obstetric care; complications cannot be predicted.`]},{title:`Emergency obstetric care`,points:[`**BEmOC** has seven signal functions, from antibiotics to newborn resuscitation.`,`**CEmOC** adds **caesarean section and blood transfusion** (nine in all).`,`**FRU** needs **caesarean, anaesthesia and 24-hour blood storage**, all three.`,`UN norm: **5 EmOC facilities per 5 lakh**, including 1 comprehensive.`,`Caesarean rate of **5-15%** of all births is the acceptable range.`,`Make a **birth preparedness plan**: facility, money, transport, blood donor.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**IFA**: 60 mg iron plus 500 mcg folic acid daily, 180 days before and after birth.`,`**Calcium 500 mg twice daily** from 14 weeks; keep 2 hours apart from iron.`,`**Aspirin 75-150 mg at night** from 12 to 36 weeks if at high risk of pre-eclampsia.`,`**Oxytocin 10 IU IM** within 1 minute of birth (AMTSL).`,`**Misoprostol 600 mcg orally** where oxytocin is unavailable.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`PPH: **tranexamic acid 1 g IV** within 3 hours of birth.`,`WHO 2025: act at **300 mL plus abnormal vital signs**, measured with a calibrated drape.`,`Give the **MOTIVE bundle** at once: massage, oxytocic, tranexamic acid, IV fluids, examination, escalation.`,`Eclampsia: **MgSO4 4 g IV plus 10 g IM**, then 5 g IM 4-hourly for 24 hours.`,`Check **knee jerk, RR over 16, urine over 30 mL/h**; antidote calcium gluconate 1 g IV.`,`Give the **MgSO4 loading dose before referral**.`]},{title:`Prevention and programmes`,points:[`**JSY (2005)** gives cash for institutional delivery.`,`**JSSK (2011)** makes delivery and caesarean free, with transport both ways.`,`**PMSMA** gives specialist ANC on the **9th of every month**; E-PMSMA tracks high-risk women.`,`**LaQshya (2017)** improves labour rooms with respectful care and a birth companion.`,`**SUMAN (2019)** guarantees **zero denial** of services.`,`**MDSR**: notify every maternal death **within 24 hours**; non-punitive review chaired by the DM.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs in safe motherhood`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`IFA`,`60 mg iron + 500 mcg folic acid oral daily`,`180 days antenatal and postnatal`,`Not with tea, milk or calcium`],[`Folic acid`,`400 mcg oral daily`,`Preconception to 12 weeks`,`Prevents neural tube defects`],[`Calcium carbonate`,`500 mg oral twice daily`,`14 weeks to 6 months postpartum`,`2 hours apart from iron`],[`Aspirin (high risk)`,`75-150 mg oral at night`,`12 to 36 weeks`,`For pre-eclampsia prevention`],[`Oxytocin`,`10 IU IM; 20-40 IU in 1 L for PPH`,`Within 1 minute of birth`,`Store at 2-8 C`],[`Misoprostol`,`600 mcg oral`,`Once after birth`,`Shivering, fever`],[`Tranexamic acid`,`1 g IV over 10 minutes`,`Repeat once after 30 minutes`,`Useless after 3 hours`],[`Magnesium sulphate`,`4 g IV + 10 g IM, then 5 g IM 4-hourly`,`24 hours`,`Check reflexes, RR, urine`],[`Nifedipine or labetalol`,`Nifedipine 10 mg oral`,`If BP 160/110 or more`,`Give before referral`]]},{heading:`Safe motherhood schemes and the delay they fix`,columns:[`Scheme`,`Year`,`Entitlement`,`Delay`],rows:[[`JSY`,`2005`,`Cash for institutional delivery`,`Delay 1`],[`JSSK`,`2011`,`Free delivery, drugs, blood, transport`,`Delays 1 and 2`],[`PMSMA`,`2016`,`Specialist ANC on the 9th`,`Delay 1`],[`LaQshya`,`2017`,`Quality labour room`,`Delay 3`],[`SUMAN`,`2019`,`Zero denial of service`,`Delays 2 and 3`],[`MDSR`,`2017`,`Review every maternal death`,`All three`]]},{heading:`Levels of emergency obstetric care`,columns:[`Level`,`Facility`,`Functions`],rows:[[`L1`,`SHC-AAM`,`Normal delivery, AMTSL, refer`],[`BEmOC (L2)`,`24x7 PHC`,`Seven signal functions`],[`CEmOC (L3)`,`FRU`,`Seven plus caesarean and transfusion`]]}],remember:[`MMR per **1,00,000 live births**; MMRate per women 15-49.`,`India MMR **88 (2021-23)**; SDG target below 70.`,`**Haemorrhage 38%** is the commonest cause.`,`Three delays: **deciding, reaching, receiving**.`,`FRU = **caesarean + anaesthesia + blood storage**.`,`MgSO4 **4 g IV + 10 g IM**; antidote calcium gluconate.`]},updated:[`SRS Special Bulletin 2021-23: MMR 88 per lakh live births (88 also in 2020-22) - replaces 93 (2019-21) as the latest figure`,`WHO/FIGO/ICM 2025 PPH guideline: calibrated drape, act at 300 mL with abnormal vital signs (or 500 mL), MOTIVE first-response bundle (E-MOTIVE trial)`,`NFHS-6 (2023-24): institutional births 90.6% added to coverage indicators`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Maternal mortality and safe motherhood`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Maternal death** = death while pregnant or **within 42 days** of termination, from any cause related to/aggravated by pregnancy, **not accidental/incidental**; late maternal death 42 days-1 year; **MMR per 1,00,000 live births** (obstetric risk); MMRate per 1,00,000 women 15-49.`],[`Relevant classification`,`**Direct** (haemorrhage, hypertension, sepsis, obstructed labour, unsafe abortion) vs **indirect** (anaemia, heart disease, hepatitis, malaria, TB); **near miss** 5-10x deaths.`],[`Figures`,`**India MMR 130 (2014-16) -> 97 (2018-20) -> 93 (2019-21) -> 88 (2020-22, 2021-23)**; Kerala lowest, EAG + Assam highest; global 197 (2023); **SDG 3.1 under 70 by 2030**.`],[`Causes and timing`,`**Haemorrhage 38%**, sepsis 11%, abortion 8%, obstructed labour 5%, hypertensive 5%, others incl. anaemia 34%; **most deaths within 24 h of birth**; anaemia in 52.2% pregnant women (NFHS-5).`],[`Three delays`,`**Delay 1 deciding** (danger signs, cost, family), **delay 2 reaching** (transport, distance), **delay 3 receiving care** (staff, blood, theatre, referral ping-pong - commonest in Indian reviews).`],[`EmOC`,`**BEmOC 7 signal functions** (parenteral antibiotics, uterotonics, anticonvulsants, MRP, retained products, assisted delivery, newborn resuscitation) + **caesarean and transfusion = CEmOC (9)**; 5 EmOC incl. 1 comprehensive per 5 lakh; **C-section 5-15%**.`],[`Care levels`,`**L1** SHC-AAM (normal delivery, AMTSL), **L2** 24x7 PHC (BEmOC), **L3** FRU (CEmOC); **FRU = caesarean + anaesthesia + 24-h blood storage**; Dakshata, NPM units; birth preparedness on MCP card; **102/108**, birth waiting homes.`],[`Schemes`,`**JSY 2005** (LPS rural Rs 1400 + ASHA 600; urban 1000 + 400); **JSSK 2011** free delivery, C-section, diet, blood, transport both ways; **PMSMA 9th of month** (E-PMSMA 2022); **LaQshya 2017**; **SUMAN 2019**; MDSR 2017.`],[`Prevention drugs`,`**IFA 60 mg + 500 mcg daily x 180 days** antenatal and postnatal; **folic acid 400 mcg** preconception-12 w; **calcium 500 mg BD** from 14 w; **aspirin 75-150 mg at night 12-36 w** if high pre-eclampsia risk.`],[`PPH - first line`,`**Oxytocin 10 IU IM within 1 min** (AMTSL); misoprostol 600 mcg oral if no oxytocin; **WHO 2025: calibrated drape, act at 300 mL + abnormal signs; MOTIVE bundle - Massage, Oxytocic, Tranexamic acid 1 g IV (within 3 h), IV fluids, Examination, Escalation**.`],[`Eclampsia`,`**MgSO4 Pritchard: 4 g IV over 15-20 min + 10 g IM (5 g each buttock), then 5 g IM 4-hourly x 24 h**; check knee jerk, RR over 16, urine over 30 mL/h; antidote **calcium gluconate 1 g IV**; labetalol or nifedipine 10 mg if BP 160/110+; **loading dose before referral**.`],[`Sepsis`,`Cefazolin 2 g before caesarean; puerperal sepsis ampicillin + gentamicin + metronidazole and refer.`],[`MDSR`,`Every maternal death notified to block **within 24 h**; facility review + **community verbal autopsy**; **district committee chaired by DM**; non-punitive, confidential, action plan.`],[`Family physician role`,`Register in first trimester, Hb/BP trends, PMSMA visit; birth plan with named FRU and blood donor; refer with MgSO4/oxytocin already given; PPIUCD; report maternal death in 24 h.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`MMR (SRS 3-year pooled), lifetime risk; **institutional births 90.6% (NFHS-6)**; skilled attendance 89.4%, ANC 4+ 58.1% (NFHS-5); functional FRUs per 5 lakh; MDSR review rate.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Any bleeding; severe headache, visual blurring, epigastric pain, BP 160/110; convulsions; fever with foul lochia; labour over 12 h; breathlessness at rest or Hb under 7 near term; repeated referral without treatment.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**SRS 2021-23 MMR 88**; **WHO 2025 PPH guideline (300 mL trigger, calibrated drape, MOTIVE bundle)**.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`JSY gives money, JSSK removes costs, PMSMA brings the specialist, LaQshya fixes quality, SUMAN guarantees service.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`PPH - first response (WHO 2025, E-MOTIVE)`,steps:[{label:`Measure blood loss with a calibrated drape`,detail:`Every vaginal birth`},{label:`300 mL with abnormal vital signs, or 500 mL?`,detail:`Diagnose PPH - do not wait for 1 litre`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Start the MOTIVE bundle together`,detail:`Uterine massage, oxytocin, tranexamic acid 1 g IV within 3 h, IV fluids`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Examine genital tract and placenta`,detail:`Tears, retained tissue`},{label:`Still bleeding - escalate`,detail:`Refer to FRU with blood; balloon tamponade, surgery`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Three delays and the scheme that fixes each`,columns:[`Delay`,`Cause`,`Fix`],rows:[[`1 Deciding to seek care`,`Danger signs not known, cost, family decision`,`Birth preparedness, JSY, PMSMA, ASHA`],[`2 Reaching the facility`,`Distance, no transport`,`102/108, JSSK transport, birth waiting homes`],[`3 Receiving adequate care`,`No staff, blood, drugs, theatre`,`Functional FRU, LaQshya, SUMAN, MDSR`]]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Eclampsia - MgSO4 Pritchard regimen`,steps:[{label:`Loading - 4 g IV over 15-20 min`,detail:`20% solution`},{label:`Plus 10 g deep IM`,detail:`5 g each buttock (50%) - give before referral`},{label:`Maintenance 5 g IM 4-hourly x 24 h`,detail:`Only if knee jerk present, RR over 16, urine over 30 mL/h`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Toxicity`,detail:`Calcium gluconate 1 g IV slowly`,tone:`warn`}]}]},"preventive-school-health":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**School health service** is an economical, powerful way to raise community health.`,`School-age children are about **a quarter of India's population**.`,`The **School Health Committee (1960, Renuka Ray)** first recommended the service.`,`Habits formed at school **last into adult life**.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Park's aspects: **appraisal, remedial care, communicable disease control, healthful environment**.`,`Also **nutrition, first aid, mental, dental and eye health**.`,`Also **health education, handicapped children and school health records**.`,`Problems are classified **by age band**: 5-9, 10-14 and 15-19 years.`]},{title:`Health problems of school children`,points:[`Commonest: **malnutrition, infections, worms, dental caries, eye, ear and skin disease**.`,`Anaemia **lowers attention, learning and attendance**.`,`**Suicide** is a leading cause of death in adolescents; exam stress is a trigger.`,`About **1 in 12 students aged 13-15** use tobacco (GYTS-4).`,`Use **BMI-for-age z-scores** in children; never adult BMI cut-offs.`,`**Screening without remedial care** is the commonest failure.`]},{title:`Appraisal and environment`,points:[`Examine at **school entry and every 4 years**; teachers do daily morning inspection.`,`Appraise **teachers too**; a teacher with open TB can infect a class.`,`Refer vision worse than **6/9** in either eye.`,`Classroom: **no more than 40 students**, at least 10 sq ft each.`,`Windows at least **25% of floor area**; light from the **left**.`,`**1 urinal per 60 and 1 latrine per 100**; separate girls' toilets.`,`**COTPA Section 6** bans tobacco sale within 100 yards of schools.`]},{title:`Nutrition`,points:[`**PM POSHAN (2021)** gives a hot cooked meal in classes 1-8.`,`Primary: **450 kcal and 12 g protein**; upper primary **700 kcal and 20 g protein**.`,`A school meal is a **supplement, not a substitute**.`,`It should give **one-third of energy and half of protein** needs.`,`**FSSAI 2020** restricts junk food on campus and within 50 metres.`]},{title:`Prevention and programmes`,points:[`**RBSK (2013)** screens 0-18 years for the **4Ds**: defects, deficiencies, diseases, developmental delays.`,`RBSK covers **32 conditions**; schools once a year, anganwadis twice a year.`,`RBSK team: **two AYUSH doctors, an ANM and a pharmacist**; positives go to the **DEIC**.`,`**SHWP (2020)**: **two Health and Wellness Ambassadors** per school and **11 themes**.`,`**National Deworming Day**: 10 February and 10 August, ages 1-19.`,`**RKSK (2014)** runs Adolescent Friendly Health Clinics.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Albendazole 400 mg chewed** once (200 mg at 1-2 years), twice a year.`,`**Pink IFA** weekly for 5-9 years; **blue IFA** weekly for 10-19 years.`,`Give IFA **after a meal**, supervised by the teacher.`,`**Hb below 8 g/dL** needs treatment dose and referral, not weekly prophylaxis.`,`**Td at 10 and 16 years**; single-dose HPV for girls aged 14.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Keep a **referral tracking register** and close the loop for every referred child.`,`Obese child: measure BP, look for **acanthosis nigricans**.`,`Advise **60 minutes of activity daily** and under 2 hours of recreational screen time.`,`Suicidal talk or self-harm marks: **same-day mental health referral**.`,`Vomiting cluster after the school meal: **stop the meal and notify**.`,`Signs of abuse: **mandatory POCSO report**.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Drugs and vaccines in school`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Albendazole`,`400 mg chewed (200 mg at 1-2 years)`,`10 February and 10 August`,`Skip if the child is sick`],[`Pink IFA`,`45 mg iron + 400 mcg folic acid oral`,`Weekly, 5-9 years`,`Nausea, black stools`],[`Blue IFA`,`60 mg iron + 500 mcg folic acid oral`,`Weekly, 10-19 years`,`Take after a meal`],[`Td vaccine`,`0.5 mL IM deltoid`,`At 10 and 16 years`,`Local pain, fever`],[`HPV vaccine (quadrivalent)`,`0.5 mL IM deltoid`,`Single dose, girls aged 14`,`Syncope; observe 30 minutes`]]},{heading:`Problems by age band`,columns:[`Age band`,`Main problems`,`School action`],rows:[[`5-9 years`,`Worms, anaemia, caries, scabies`,`Deworming, pink IFA, screening`],[`10-14 years`,`Refractive error, ADHD, learning disability`,`Blue IFA, Snellen screen`],[`15-19 years`,`Obesity, depression, substance use`,`SHWP sessions, counselling, AFHC`]]},{heading:`School health components`,columns:[`Component`,`Who delivers`,`Frequency`],rows:[[`RBSK screening`,`Mobile health team`,`Once a year`],[`Weekly IFA`,`Teacher`,`Weekly`],[`Deworming`,`Teacher with ANM`,`Twice a year`],[`SHWP sessions`,`Two ambassadors`,`Weekly`],[`PM POSHAN`,`Cook-cum-helpers`,`Every school day`]]}],remember:[`Classroom: **40 students, 10 sq ft each, light from the left**.`,`SHWP: **two ambassadors, 11 themes**.`,`RBSK: **4Ds, 32 conditions**.`,`**Pink 5-9, blue 10-19**, weekly.`,`Deworming on **10 February and 10 August**.`,`Measure **referral completion**, not just screening.`]},updated:[`NFHS-6 (2023-24): under-five stunting 29.3%, wasting 19.0% - replaces NFHS-5 35.5% and 19.3% (anaemia still NFHS-5)`,`MoHFW 2026: single-dose quadrivalent HPV vaccine for girls aged 14 nationally from 28 February 2026 - replaces 'planned for 9-14 years'`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - School health services and SHWP`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**School health service** = economical and powerful means of raising community and future-generation health by promoting the health of school children and staff.`],[`Classification - aspects (Park)`,`**Health appraisal, remedial measures and follow-up, communicable disease prevention, healthful environment, nutrition, first aid, mental, dental, eye health, health education, handicapped children, records**.`],[`History`,`**School Health Committee 1960 (Renuka Ray)**; RBSK 2013; RKSK 2014; **AB-SHWP February 2020**.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`Worms, anaemia, caries, refractive error, skin, ENT; under-fives **stunting 29.3%, wasting 19.0% (NFHS-6)**; anaemia 67.1% (6-59 m), **59.1% girls 15-19** (NFHS-5); tobacco 1 in 12 aged 13-15 (GYTS-4); suicide and exam stress.`],[`Age bands`,`**5-9**: worms, anaemia, caries, refraction, scabies; **10-14**: refraction, ADHD, SLD, bullying; **15-19**: anaemia, obesity, menstrual issues, depression, substance use, injury, SRH.`],[`Appraisal`,`Pupils **and teachers**; at entry and **every 4 years**, daily teacher inspection; height, weight, BMI-for-age (WHO 2007 +1/+2 SD), **vision refer if worse than 6/9**, hearing, teeth, scoliosis, BP if obese.`],[`Environment`,`Away from roads/markets; **40 students per class, 10 sq ft each, windows 25% of floor area, light from left**; **1 urinal/60, 1 latrine/100**, girls' toilets with menstrual hygiene; **COTPA S6 - no tobacco within 100 yards**; FSSAI 2020 HFSS ban 50 m.`],[`Nutrition`,`**PM POSHAN 2021: 450 kcal + 12 g protein (primary), 700 kcal + 20 g (upper primary)**; meal = supplement, one-third energy, half protein; fortified rice; tasted before serving.`],[`Programmes`,`**RBSK 4 Ds, 32 conditions, 0-18 y** - mobile team (2 AYUSH doctors, ANM, pharmacist), school yearly, anganwadi twice; **DEIC**; **AMB pink weekly 5-9, blue weekly 10-19**; **NDD 10 Feb, 10 Aug**; **SHWP - 2 Health and Wellness Ambassadors, 11 themes**.`],[`Drugs and vaccines`,`**Albendazole 400 mg chewed** (200 mg at 1-2 y) twice yearly; pink IFA 45 mg + 400 mcg; blue IFA 60 mg + 500 mcg weekly after meal; **Td 0.5 mL IM at 10 and 16**; **HPV single dose 0.5 mL IM for girls 14 (from 28 February 2026)**; Hb under 8 = treat and refer.`],[`Family physician role`,`Situation analysis of schools; micro-plan RBSK, NDD, WIFS; **referral register - close the loop**; catch-up vaccines; POCSO reporting; obese child - BP, acanthosis, 60 min activity, screen time under 2 h.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`Input (ambassadors, toilets, stock), process (**screened, WIFS compliance, NDD coverage**), output (**referral completion**, spectacles, surgeries), outcome (anaemia, worms, absenteeism).`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Suicidal talk or self-harm; vomiting cluster after school meal; Hb under 8; sudden vision loss, new squint, white reflex; obesity with hypertension; signs of abuse.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**HPV vaccine for girls aged 14 (February 2026)**; **NFHS-6 under-five stunting 29.3%**.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Screening without completed treatment is the commonest failure - measure referral completion.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`School health visit - screen to closed loop`,steps:[{label:`RBSK team screens (yearly in school)`,detail:`4 Ds: defects, deficiencies, diseases, delays`},{label:`Positive finding?`,detail:`Vision worse than 6/9, Hb under 8, murmur, delay`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Refer to CHC / DEIC with register entry`,detail:`Spectacles, surgery, therapy free`},{label:`Lost after referral`,detail:`Commonest failure - trace with teacher and ASHA`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Treatment completed and recorded on health card`,detail:`Output indicator: referral completion`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`What a school gives, how often`,columns:[`Intervention`,`Who`,`When`],rows:[[`Pink IFA (5-9 y) / blue IFA (10-19 y)`,`Teacher, after meal`,`Weekly`],[`Albendazole 400 mg`,`Teacher with ANM`,`10 February and 10 August`],[`Td`,`ANM`,`10 and 16 years`],[`HPV single dose (girls)`,`Health team, school mobilises`,`Age 14 (from February 2026)`],[`SHWP sessions`,`Two Health and Wellness Ambassadors`,`Weekly`]]}]},"preventive-cancer-screening-india":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Screening** searches for unrecognised disease in apparently healthy people; it sorts, never diagnoses.`,`India had about **13.9 lakh new cancers in 2020**, projected 15.7 lakh in 2025.`,`Men: **lung and mouth** lead; women: **breast, then cervix**.`,`**Tobacco-related cancers** form about 27% of the burden.`,`Coverage is tiny: only **1.9% of women 30-49** ever had a cervical examination.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Mass** versus **high-risk (selective)** screening.`,`**Opportunistic** (attending for another reason) versus **organised** (call-recall, quality control).`,`**Multiphasic** screening applies several tests at one sitting.`,`Wilson-Jungner: important problem, latent stage, acceptable test, effective early treatment.`]},{title:`Natural history and biases`,points:[`Screening needs a **detectable pre-clinical phase** where treatment changes outcome.`,`**Lead-time bias**: survival looks longer only because diagnosis is earlier.`,`**Length-time bias**: slow-growing tumours are over-detected.`,`**Overdiagnosis** is the main harm of PSA and mammography.`,`Benefit must be proved by **falling mortality**, not survival.`,`Persistent **HPV 16 and 18** infection causes CIN and cancer over 10-20 years.`]},{title:`Indian trial evidence`,points:[`**Trivandrum OVE trial** cut oral cancer deaths in tobacco and alcohol users.`,`**Mumbai VIA trial** cut cervical cancer mortality about **31%**.`,`**Osmanabad trial**: a single HPV test beat cytology and VIA.`,`**Mumbai CBE trial** cut breast cancer mortality in women 50 and over.`]},{title:`Examination`,points:[`**Oral visual examination**: good light, remove dentures, inspect all surfaces, palpate the neck.`,`**Leukoplakia** is the commonest premalignant lesion; **erythroplakia** has the highest malignant potential.`,`**CBE**: inspect in three arm positions, then palpate in a **vertical strip pattern**.`,`**VIA**: apply **3-5% acetic acid**; look after 1 minute for an acetowhite area.`,`A breast lump needs **triple assessment**: examination, imaging and core biopsy.`,`Imaging: **ultrasound under 35, mammography over 35**.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Screen-and-treat**: thermal ablation or cryotherapy at the same visit.`,`Refer for colposcopy and **LEEP** if the lesion covers over 75% of the zone or enters the canal.`,`Never ablate a visible growth; **refer for biopsy**.`,`Oral lesion: stop tobacco, then **biopsy** at the district NCD clinic.`]},{title:`Prevention and programmes`,points:[`**NP-NCD** screens all adults **30 and over** at Ayushman Arogya Mandirs.`,`Oral, breast and cervical screening **once every 5 years**; women 30-65.`,`WHO 2021: **HPV DNA from 30, every 5-10 years**; WLHIV from 25, every 3-5 years.`,`**Self-collected HPV samples** raise coverage.`,`WHO elimination target **90-70-90 by 2030**; below 4 per 1,00,000 women.`,`**Single-dose HPV vaccine** for girls aged 14 since 28 February 2026.`,`PSA, mammography, colonoscopy and low-dose CT are **not national screens**.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Ask **tobacco and areca nut** use and examine the mouth in every user over 30.`,`**Never screen a symptomatic woman**; postcoital bleeding needs colposcopy and biopsy.`,`Keep a **practice register** with recall, and track positives to treatment start.`,`Counsel about **false positives and overdiagnosis**; consent before PSA.`,`Track **coverage, VIA positivity (5-10%), stage at diagnosis and mortality**.`]}],tables:[{heading:`NP-NCD cancer screening`,columns:[`Cancer`,`Test`,`Who and interval`,`Next step if positive`],rows:[[`Oral`,`Oral visual examination`,`All 30+, every 5 years`,`Stop tobacco; biopsy`],[`Breast`,`Clinical breast examination`,`Women 30-65, every 5 years`,`Triple assessment`],[`Cervix`,`VIA with 3-5% acetic acid`,`Women 30-65, every 5 years`,`Thermal ablation or colposcopy`],[`Cervix (HPV)`,`HPV DNA, clinician or self-sample`,`30-65 every 5-10 years`,`VIA triage, then treat`]]},{heading:`Biases in cancer screening`,columns:[`Bias`,`What happens`,`Remedy`],rows:[[`Lead-time`,`Survival seems longer`,`Use mortality`],[`Length-time`,`Slow tumours over-represented`,`Randomised trials`],[`Overdiagnosis`,`Harmless cancers treated`,`Restrict ages, shared decisions`],[`Selection`,`Healthy volunteers attend`,`Compare randomised arms`]]},{heading:`Screens not offered nationally in India`,columns:[`Cancer`,`Test (USPSTF)`,`Why not national`],rows:[[`Breast`,`Mammography`,`Cost, dense breasts, few machines`],[`Colorectal`,`FIT or colonoscopy 45-75`,`Not a national programme`],[`Prostate`,`PSA, shared decision 55-69`,`Overdiagnosis`],[`Lung`,`Low-dose CT 50-80, 20 pack-years`,`TB nodules give false positives`]]}],remember:[`NP-NCD: **30+, oral, breast, cervix, every 5 years**.`,`**Erythroplakia** most malignant; leukoplakia commonest.`,`Prove benefit by **mortality, never survival**.`,`HPV DNA **from 30, every 5-10 years**.`,`WHO **90-70-90** by 2030.`,`**Never screen the symptomatic.**`]},updated:[`MoHFW 2026: free single-dose quadrivalent HPV vaccination (Gardasil-4) for girls aged 14 launched 28 February 2026 - replaces 'girls 9-14, Cervavac being introduced'`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Cancer screening in India (NP-NCD)`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Screening** = search for unrecognised disease or precursor in **apparently healthy** people - sorts, does not diagnose; organised (call-recall, QA) vs opportunistic.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`ICMR-NCRP: **13.9 lakh (2020), 15.7 lakh projected 2025**; 1 in 9 by 74; men **lung, mouth**; women **breast, cervix**; **tobacco-related about 27%**; areca nut Group 1; NFHS-5 ever screened: cervix 1.9%, breast 0.9%.`],[`Natural history`,`Needs a **detectable pre-clinical phase** and effective early treatment; **HPV 16, 18** persistence -> CIN -> cancer over 10-20 years; leukoplakia commonest, **erythroplakia highest malignant potential**, OSMF.`],[`Biases`,`**Lead-time** (prove by mortality), **length-time**, **overdiagnosis** (PSA, mammography), **selection** - only randomised trials valid.`],[`Indian trials`,`**Trivandrum OVE**, **Mumbai VIA (31% fall in cervical mortality)**, **Osmanabad single HPV test**, **Mumbai CBE** (benefit 50+).`],[`How - oral`,`Good light, remove dentures, inspect all surfaces incl. lateral tongue and floor, palpate neck; **biopsy red/white patch or ulcer over 2-3 weeks**.`],[`How - breast`,`CBE: inspect (arms by side, raised, on hips), palpate lying with **vertical strip**, axilla, supraclavicular; **triple assessment** (US under 35, mammography over 35, core biopsy); breast self-awareness.`],[`How - cervix`,`**VIA 3-5% acetic acid, read at 1 min, acetowhite at TZ**; **screen-and-treat thermal ablation** same visit; colposcopy/LEEP if over 75% TZ, into canal or suspicious; **HPV DNA from 30 every 5-10 y (WLHIV 25, every 3-5 y)**, self-sampling.`],[`NP-NCD programme`,`**PBS 2016; NPCDCS renamed NP-NCD 2023**; ASHA - CBAC; CHO/ANM - OVE, CBE, VIA at AAM; PHC MO confirms; district NCD clinic -> TCCC/SCI; **every 5 years, 30+ (breast, cervix 30-65)**; NCD portal; day-care cancer centres (Budget 2025-26).`],[`Not national screens`,`Colorectal (FIT/colonoscopy 45-75), **PSA by shared decision 55-69**, **LDCT 50-80 with 20 pack-years** (TB nodules = false positives) - USPSTF, not Indian programme.`],[`Primary prevention`,`**HPV single dose for girls 14 (national, 28 February 2026)**; HepB birth dose; **tobacco control (COTPA, quitline 1800-112-356, mCessation)**; alcohol, weight, activity, clean air.`],[`Global targets`,`**WHO 90-70-90 by 2030** (vaccinated by 15, screened at 35 and 45, treated); elimination **under 4/1,00,000 women**; **Global Breast Cancer Initiative** - 2.5%/year mortality fall, diagnosis within 60 days.`],[`Family physician role`,`Ask tobacco/areca at every 30+ visit; **60-second mouth exam**; CBE 30-65; VIA/HPV for never-screened; **never screen the symptomatic**; practice register with recall; track positives to treatment; PM-JAY enrolment.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`**Coverage 70% (WHO)**, VIA positivity 5-10%, confirmation and treatment completion, stage shift, cancer-specific mortality, PBCR/HBCR trends.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Oral patch/ulcer over 2-3 weeks; breast lump, dimpling, bloody discharge; **postcoital or postmenopausal bleeding - colposcopy not VIA**; visible cervical growth - do not ablate; hard neck node; rectal bleeding over 45.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**National HPV vaccination - single dose, girls aged 14, from 28 February 2026**.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Mammography is not a population screen in India - CBE is; benefit is proved by mortality, never survival.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Cervical screen-and-treat at the AAM`,steps:[{label:`Woman 30-65, asymptomatic`,detail:`Symptomatic = diagnostic work-up, not screening`,tone:`decision`},{label:`VIA (3-5% acetic acid, 1 min) or HPV DNA`,detail:`Self-sample acceptable for HPV`},{label:`Positive and eligible for ablation?`,detail:`Lesion under 75% TZ, not into canal, no suspicion of cancer`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Thermal ablation same visit`,detail:`Re-screen at 1 year`,tone:`good`},{label:`Not eligible or suspicious`,detail:`Colposcopy, biopsy, LEEP at district NCD clinic`,tone:`warn`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`NP-NCD cancer screens`,columns:[`Cancer`,`Test`,`Who and interval`,`Positive -> next`],rows:[[`Oral`,`Oral visual examination`,`All 30+, every 5 years`,`Stop tobacco; biopsy`],[`Breast`,`Clinical breast examination`,`Women 30-65, every 5 years`,`Triple assessment`],[`Cervix`,`VIA (HPV DNA where available)`,`Women 30-65, every 5 years`,`Thermal ablation or colposcopy`]]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Cervical cancer elimination - WHO 90-70-90`,steps:[{label:`90% of girls HPV-vaccinated by 15`,detail:`India: single dose for girls aged 14 from February 2026`},{label:`70% of women screened at 35 and 45`,detail:`High-performance test - HPV DNA`},{label:`90% of detected disease treated`,detail:`Precancer and invasive cancer`},{label:`Elimination`,detail:`Under 4 per 1,00,000 women`,tone:`good`}]}]},"preventive-travel-medicine":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Travel medicine** prevents and manages health problems of travellers by risk assessment.`,`See the traveller ideally **4-8 weeks before departure**.`,`**Road injuries and drowning**, not infections, kill most travellers.`,`**VFR travellers** carry the highest malaria and typhoid risk.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Vaccines are **routine, required or recommended**.`,`**Required**: yellow fever, polio, and meningococcal ACWY for Hajj and Umrah.`,`**Recommended**: hepatitis A, typhoid, rabies, JE and cholera.`,`Malaria prevention is **ABCD**: Awareness, Bite avoidance, Chemoprophylaxis, Diagnosis.`]},{title:`History in the OPD`,points:[`**Traveller profile**: age, pregnancy, chronic disease, immunosuppression, medicines, allergies.`,`**Trip profile**: itinerary, season, duration, urban or rural, activities.`,`Flights over **4 hours** raise DVT risk.`,`Altitude above **2500 m** risks AMS, HACE and HAPE.`,`Traveller's diarrhoea is mostly **enterotoxigenic E. coli**.`]},{title:`Vaccines and advice`,points:[`**Yellow fever**: one dose, lifelong, valid from **10 days after vaccination**.`,`India **quarantines up to 6 days** arrivals from endemic areas without a certificate.`,`Polio: travellers from affected countries need a dose **4 weeks to 12 months** before leaving.`,`Meningococcal ACWY at least **10 days before** arriving for Hajj.`,`Rabies pre-exposure: **days 0 and 7**.`,`Bite avoidance: **DEET 20-50%**, permethrin clothing and treated nets.`,`Food rule: **boil it, cook it, peel it or forget it**.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Atovaquone-proguanil** daily: start 1-2 days before, stop **7 days after**.`,`**Doxycycline 100 mg** daily: start 1-2 days before, stop **4 weeks after**.`,`**Mefloquine 250 mg weekly**: start 2-3 weeks before; choice if a pregnant woman must travel.`,`Never use **chloroquine** for sub-Saharan Africa.`,`Diarrhoea: ORS first; **standby azithromycin** for moderate-severe or bloody diarrhoea.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`**Acetazolamide 125 mg twice daily** from the day before ascent prevents AMS.`,`HACE: **descend**, oxygen and **dexamethasone 8 mg then 4 mg 6-hourly**.`,`HAPE: descend, oxygen and **nifedipine SR 30 mg twice daily**.`,`High-risk DVT: **compression stockings or one LMWH dose** before the flight.`,`Bite abroad: wash **15 minutes** and start PEP the same day.`]},{title:`Special groups`,points:[`Carry **twice the medicine supply** with a doctor's letter.`,`**Insulin in cabin baggage**; eastward reduce basal, westward may need extra.`,`**SpO2 below 92%** at rest needs fitness-to-fly assessment.`,`Pregnancy: avoid malaria and Zika areas; flying usually allowed to **36 weeks**.`,`Students: MMR, meningococcal ACWY, HPV; **TB chest X-ray** for UK, Canada, Australia visas.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Fever within a year of a malarious area is **malaria until proven otherwise**.`,`Need **three negative smears or RDTs** over 24-48 hours to exclude it.`,`Fever **over 21 days after return** excludes dengue and VHF, not malaria.`,`VHF area plus bleeding within 21 days: **isolate and notify**.`,`Eosinophilia after travel suggests **schistosomiasis or strongyloides**.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Travel drugs`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Atovaquone-proguanil`,`250/100 mg oral daily with food`,`1-2 days before to 7 days after`,`GI upset; avoid severe renal impairment`],[`Doxycycline`,`100 mg oral daily`,`1-2 days before to 4 weeks after`,`Photosensitivity, oesophagitis; not in pregnancy`],[`Mefloquine`,`250 mg oral weekly`,`2-3 weeks before to 4 weeks after`,`Avoid psychiatric illness, epilepsy`],[`Azithromycin (standby)`,`1 g single dose, or 500 mg daily`,`Once, or 3 days`,`Nausea, QT prolongation`],[`Loperamide`,`4 mg, then 2 mg per loose stool`,`Max 16 mg a day`,`Not with fever or blood`],[`Acetazolamide`,`125 mg oral twice daily`,`Day before ascent onwards`,`Paraesthesia; sulfonamide allergy`],[`Dexamethasone (HACE)`,`8 mg, then 4 mg 6-hourly`,`With descent`,`Hyperglycaemia`],[`Nifedipine SR (HAPE)`,`30 mg oral twice daily`,`With descent`,`Hypotension`]]},{heading:`Travel vaccines`,columns:[`Category`,`Vaccines`,`Why`],rows:[[`Routine`,`MMR, Td, varicella, hepatitis B, influenza`,`Update adult immunisation`],[`Required`,`Yellow fever, polio, meningococcal ACWY`,`Legal entry requirement`],[`Recommended`,`Hepatitis A, typhoid, rabies, JE, cholera`,`Destination and activity risk`]]},{heading:`Fever after return`,columns:[`Timing after return`,`Still possible`,`Excluded`],rows:[[`Within 21 days`,`All, including dengue and VHF`,`None`],[`Over 21 days`,`Malaria, typhoid, hepatitis, TB, amoebic abscess`,`Dengue, chikungunya, rickettsia, VHF`],[`Up to 1 year`,`Malaria`,`Treat as malaria until excluded`]]}],remember:[`Yellow fever: **one dose, lifelong, valid after 10 days**.`,`Malaria **ABCD**.`,`After leaving: **A-P 7 days; doxy and mefloquine 4 weeks**.`,`Mefloquine if a **pregnant woman must travel**.`,`**Insulin in the cabin.**`,`Road accidents kill **more travellers than infections**.`]},master:{heading:`Master revision table - Travel medicine: the pre-travel consultation`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Prevention and management of travellers' health problems through **risk assessment and risk management** before, during and after travel; ideally **4-8 weeks** before departure.`],[`Relevant classification`,`Vaccines **routine, required, recommended**; malaria **ABCD** (Awareness, Bite avoidance, Chemoprophylaxis, Diagnosis); **VFR travellers highest risk**; road injury and drowning kill most.`],[`Risk assessment`,`**Traveller** (age, pregnancy, disease, drugs, allergies, prior vaccines) x **trip** (itinerary, season, duration, rural/urban, activities).`],[`Required vaccines`,`**Yellow fever 17D**: one dose, **lifelong**, certificate valid **10 days after** (IHR 2016), designated centres; India **quarantines up to 6 days** without it; **polio OPV/IPV 4 weeks-12 months** before leaving affected countries; **MenACWY for Hajj/Umrah 10 days before**.`],[`Recommended`,`Hepatitis A, **typhoid conjugate**, rabies **PrEP days 0 and 7**, JE (long rural Asia stays), cholera; routine MMR, Td/Tdap, varicella, HepB, influenza; live vaccines same day or 4 weeks apart.`],[`Malaria prophylaxis`,`**Atovaquone-proguanil 1 daily: 1-2 days before to 7 days after**; **doxycycline 100 mg: 1-2 days before to 4 weeks after**; **mefloquine 250 mg weekly: 2-3 weeks before to 4 weeks after** (choice in pregnancy); primaquine only after normal G6PD; never chloroquine for Africa.`],[`Traveller's diarrhoea`,`Mostly ETEC; ORS first; **loperamide 4 mg then 2 mg/stool (max 16)** if no fever/blood; **standby azithromycin 1 g single** (South/SE Asia, FQ-resistant Campylobacter); rifaximin non-invasive only.`],[`Altitude`,`AMS above 2500 m; **acetazolamide 125 mg BD** from day before; **HACE - descend, O2, dexamethasone 8 mg then 4 mg 6-hourly**; **HAPE - descend, O2, nifedipine SR 30 mg BD**.`],[`Other advice`,`DEET 20-50%, permethrin clothing, nets; boil-cook-peel; seat belts, helmets; insurance with evacuation; **DVT - stockings or single LMWH dose** for high risk on flights over 4 h; melatonin for jet lag.`],[`Chronic disease and special groups`,`**Double supply** in hand luggage with doctor's letter; **insulin in cabin**, eastward reduce basal; SpO2 under 92% at rest - fitness-to-fly; controlled drugs - check rules; **pregnancy fly to 36 w**, avoid Zika/malaria areas; students - MenACWY, TB CXR for visas.`],[`Post-travel fever`,`**Malaria until 3 negative smears/RDTs over 24-48 h** if in malarious area within 1 year; fever over 21 days after return excludes dengue, chikungunya, rickettsia, VHF - **not malaria, typhoid, hepatitis, amoebic abscess, TB**; eosinophilia - schistosomiasis, strongyloides.`],[`Rabies abroad`,`Wash 15 min, start PEP same day; previously vaccinated - **2 boosters days 0 and 3, no RIG**.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Fever after malarious area; VHF-area fever within 21 days with bleeding - isolate, notify; ataxia/confusion at altitude; breathlessness at rest at altitude; bloody diarrhoea with fever; leg swelling after flight; any mammal bite.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`No change needed - already aligned with **CDC Yellow Book 2026**, UKHSA 2024, WMS 2024, ADA 2026.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`Mefloquine is the prophylaxis of choice if a pregnant woman must travel; road accidents kill more travellers than infections.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Pre-travel consultation in order`,steps:[{label:`Traveller and trip profile`,detail:`Itinerary, season, duration, activities; pregnancy, disease, drugs`},{label:`Required vaccines?`,detail:`Yellow fever (lifelong), polio, MenACWY for Hajj`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Routine and recommended vaccines`,detail:`HepA, typhoid conjugate, rabies PrEP, JE`},{label:`Malaria ABCD and chemoprophylaxis`,detail:`AP, doxycycline or mefloquine by trip and person`},{label:`Standby kit and chronic-disease plan`,detail:`ORS, azithromycin 1 g, double medicines, letter, insurance`,tone:`good`},{label:`Return instruction`,detail:`Fever within a year = tell doctor about travel`,tone:`warn`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Malaria chemoprophylaxis`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose`,`Start`,`Continue after`],rows:[[`Atovaquone-proguanil`,`250/100 mg daily with food`,`1-2 days before`,`7 days`],[`Doxycycline`,`100 mg daily, upright, sunscreen`,`1-2 days before`,`4 weeks`],[`Mefloquine`,`250 mg weekly`,`2-3 weeks before`,`4 weeks`],[`Primaquine`,`30 mg base daily (normal G6PD only)`,`1-2 days before`,`7 days`]]}]},"preventive-air-pollution-environmental-health":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Air pollution**: substances in the air at concentrations harmful to man or environment.`,`It caused about **1.67 million Indian deaths in 2019**, 17.8% of all deaths.`,`**PM2.5** reaches the alveoli and blood and best predicts harm.`,`Outdoor air pollution and particulate matter are **IARC Group 1 carcinogens**.`,`Only **58.6% of households** used clean cooking fuel (NFHS-5).`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`**Ambient (outdoor)** versus **household (indoor)** pollution.`,`**Primary pollutants** are emitted directly: PM, SO2, NOx, CO.`,`**Secondary pollutants** form in air: ozone and secondary aerosols.`,`**PM10** lodges in upper airways; **PM2.5** reaches the alveoli.`,`Indian AQI has **six categories** from up to **eight pollutants**; the worst sub-index sets it.`]},{title:`How it happens (pathophysiology)`,points:[`Deposited PM2.5 causes **oxidative stress** and airway inflammation.`,`Inflammation spreads systemically and injures the **endothelium**.`,`Autonomic imbalance and a **prothrombotic state** follow.`,`So one exposure links to **asthma, MI, stroke and diabetes**.`,`**CO** binds haemoglobin 200-250 times more avidly than oxygen.`]},{title:`Sources and health effects`,points:[`Urban: **vehicles, road dust, industry, power plants** and waste burning.`,`Rural: **chulha smoke**, crop residue burning and brick kilns.`,`North India smog: **stubble burning plus winter inversion** in October-November.`,`**Biomass COPD** occurs in never-smoking rural women.`,`Pregnancy: **low birth weight, preterm birth and stillbirth**.`,`Noise causes a **4 kHz notch**, sleep disturbance and hypertension.`]},{title:`Standards and control`,points:[`WHO 2021 PM2.5: **5 annual, 15 daily** microgram/m3.`,`India NAAQS 2009 PM2.5: **40 annual, 60 daily** microgram/m3.`,`Legal base: **Air Act 1981** and Environment Protection Act 1986.`,`Control: **containment, replacement, dilution and legislation**.`,`A fitted **N95** filters most PM2.5; cloth masks give little protection.`,`Occupational noise action level: **85 dB(A) over 8 hours**.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`Optimise asthma and COPD **before the October-January smog season**.`,`Give a written action plan and **influenza and pneumococcal vaccines**.`,`At AQI very poor, **step up the asthma controller** and avoid outdoor activity.`,`CO poisoning: remove from source and give **100% oxygen**.`,`Refer severe or pregnant CO cases for **hyperbaric oxygen**.`]},{title:`Prevention and programmes`,points:[`**NCAP (2019)**: 131 non-attainment cities; target **40% PM10 cut by 2025-26**.`,`**PMUY (2016)** gave over 10 crore LPG connections to poor women.`,`**GRAP** escalates Delhi-NCR measures by AQI stage, enforced by CAQM.`,`**BS-VI** fuel and vehicles from April 2020.`,`**NPCCHH** runs air-pollution illness surveillance in sentinel hospitals.`,`Noise Rules 2000: residential **55 day, 45 night**; silence zone 50 and 40.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Take an **exposure history**: cooking fuel, ventilation, occupation, commute.`,`Do **spirometry** in non-smoking rural women with chronic cough.`,`Counsel **exclusive LPG use**; keep infants away from the chulha.`,`SpO2 below 92% on a high-AQI day: **treat and refer**.`,`Family with headache and vomiting near an angithi: **suspect CO poisoning**.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Treatment in pollution-related illness`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Oxygen 100% (CO poisoning)`,`Non-rebreather mask`,`Until symptom-free`,`Hyperbaric if severe or pregnant`],[`Reliever inhaler`,`As per action plan`,`On bad-AQI days`,`Keep supply ready`],[`Asthma controller`,`Stepped up per action plan`,`Through smog season`,`Check adherence`],[`Influenza vaccine`,`Yearly`,`Before smog season`,`For asthma and COPD`],[`Pneumococcal vaccine`,`As per adult schedule`,`Once`,`For chronic lung disease`]]},{heading:`Indian AQI and advice`,columns:[`AQI`,`Category`,`Advice`],rows:[[`0-50`,`Good`,`Normal activity`],[`51-100`,`Satisfactory`,`Sensitive groups watch symptoms`],[`101-200`,`Moderately polluted`,`Sensitive groups limit outdoor exertion`],[`201-300`,`Poor`,`Everyone reduces outdoor exercise`],[`301-400`,`Very poor`,`Avoid outdoors; N95 if outside`],[`401-500`,`Severe`,`Stay indoors; GRAP emergency`]]},{heading:`WHO 2021 versus Indian NAAQS 2009`,columns:[`Pollutant`,`WHO 2021`,`India NAAQS`],rows:[[`PM2.5 annual`,`5`,`40`],[`PM2.5 24-hour`,`15`,`60`],[`PM10 annual`,`15`,`60`],[`PM10 24-hour`,`45`,`100`],[`NO2 annual`,`10`,`40`]]}],remember:[`**PM2.5** matters most: alveoli and blood.`,`**1.67 million deaths** in India in 2019.`,`PM2.5: WHO **5 and 15**; India **40 and 60**.`,`AQI: **six categories, eight pollutants**.`,`**Biomass COPD** in non-smoking women is missed.`,`Noise: residential **55 day, 45 night**.`]},updated:[`GINA 2026: tag updated for pre-smog-season asthma optimisation (current asthma guideline)`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Air pollution, noise and environmental health`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`Air pollution = substances in air at concentrations harmful to man or environment; **ambient vs household**; **primary** (PM, SO2, NOx, CO) vs **secondary** (ozone, secondary aerosols).`],[`Relevant classification`,`**PM10** (upper airways) vs **PM2.5** (alveoli and blood - best predictor); **Indian AQI 2014: 6 categories, 8 pollutants, worst sub-index sets AQI**; Park's classical indicators: SO2, smoke/SPM, grit, CO, oxidants.`],[`Aetiology & risk factors`,`**1.67 million Indian deaths (17.8%) in 2019**; outdoor air pollution and PM **IARC Group 1 (2013)**; household deaths falling with LPG, ambient rising.`],[`Sources`,`Urban: vehicles, road/construction dust, industry, power, DG sets, waste burning; rural: **chulha biomass**, crop residue, brick kilns; **stubble burning Oct-Nov + winter inversion**; Diwali crackers; clean fuel only **58.6% households (NFHS-5)**.`],[`Pathophysiology`,`PM2.5 -> **oxidative stress, airway and systemic inflammation, endothelial dysfunction, autonomic imbalance, thrombosis** -> asthma/COPD exacerbation, MI, stroke, diabetes, LBW, preterm birth; CO binds Hb 200-250x.`],[`Effects`,`Respiratory (exacerbations, LRTI, impaired lung growth, **biomass COPD in non-smoking women**, lung cancer); cardiovascular/metabolic; pregnancy (LBW, preterm, stillbirth); children most vulnerable.`],[`Standards`,`**WHO AQG 2021 PM2.5 5 annual / 15 daily; PM10 15/45; NO2 10**; **India NAAQS 2009 PM2.5 40/60; PM10 60/100**; CO 2 mg/m3 (8 h).`],[`Control`,`**Containment, replacement, dilution, legislation**; Air Act 1981, EPA 1986; NAMP and CAAQMS, SAMEER app; individual - **fitted N95**, windows shut, HEPA purifier, chimney.`],[`Indian programmes`,`**NCAP 2019 - 131 cities, 40% PM10 cut by 2025-26**, performance-linked funding, Swachh Vayu Survekshan; **PMUY 2016 - 10 crore+ LPG**; **GRAP** (CAQM 2021); BS-VI 2020; e-mobility; NPCCHH sentinel surveillance.`],[`Treatment - exacerbation`,`Salbutamol via spacer, ICS-formoterol reliever in asthma, prednisolone 40-50 mg x 5-7 days; **CO poisoning - 100% O2**, HBO if severe or pregnant.`],[`Noise`,`Auditory (TTS, **NIHL 4 kHz notch**) and non-auditory (sleep, BP, IHD); **Noise Rules 2000: industrial 75/70, commercial 65/55, residential 55/45, silence 50/40 dB(A)**, silence zone 100 m; loudspeakers banned 10 pm-6 am; occupational action 85 dB(A).`],[`Family physician role`,`Exposure history (fuel, kitchen, commute); **suspect biomass COPD - spirometry**; optimise asthma/COPD before **October-January smog**; AQI-based advice, N95; promote exclusive LPG; recognise CO; audiometry for noise-exposed.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`PM2.5/PM10 vs NAAQS, days poor or worse, NCAP attainment; NPCCHH admissions and deaths; clean fuel use; Swachh Vayu ranking.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`SpO2 under 92% or unable to speak on high-AQI day; chest pain in cardiac patient during smog; family with headache and confusion near brazier (CO); infant with chest indrawing from biomass home; sudden hearing loss after crackers.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`No substantive change - GINA 2026 tag; NAAQS 2009 still the legal standard.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`PM2.5 matters most; WHO 2021 PM2.5 5/15 vs India 40/60.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Counselling a patient with asthma/COPD by AQI`,steps:[{label:`Check today's AQI (SAMEER app)`,detail:`Worst sub-index sets the band`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Moderately polluted 101-200`,detail:`Limit prolonged outdoor exertion`},{label:`Poor 201-300`,detail:`Reduce outdoor exercise; reliever at hand`},{label:`Very poor 301-400`,detail:`Avoid outdoor activity; N95 if out; step up controller`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Severe 401-500`,detail:`Stay indoors, HEPA; act on written action plan; seek care if SpO2 under 92%`,tone:`warn`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`WHO AQG 2021 against Indian NAAQS 2009`,columns:[`Pollutant`,`WHO 2021`,`India NAAQS`],rows:[[`PM2.5 annual`,`5`,`40`],[`PM2.5 24-hour`,`15`,`60`],[`PM10 annual`,`15`,`60`],[`PM10 24-hour`,`45`,`100`],[`NO2 annual`,`10`,`40`]]}]},"preventive-demography-family-planning":{checked:`2026-10`,notes:{sections:[{title:`Definition and key facts`,points:[`**Demography** studies population size, composition and distribution.`,`Population changes through **fertility, mortality and migration**.`,`India became the **most populous country in 2023**.`,`TFR **2.0 (NFHS-6)** and **1.9 (SRS 2023)**: below replacement.`,`India launched the **world's first family planning programme in 1952**.`]},{title:`Classification that matters`,points:[`Demographic cycle: **high stationary, early expanding, late expanding, low stationary, declining**.`,`India is in **stage 3, late expanding**.`,`**1921** is the year of the great divide.`,`**Spacing methods**: barrier, hormonal, Chhaya, IUCD, natural and LAM.`,`**Terminal methods**: tubectomy and vasectomy.`]},{title:`Key numbers and indicators`,points:[`Census 2011: **121.09 crore**, decadal growth 17.7%, literacy 74.04%.`,`SRS 2023: **CBR 18.4, CDR 6.4**; life expectancy 70.0 years.`,`**TFR** is the sum of age-specific fertility rates.`,`**NRR 1 = replacement**, about TFR 2.1 and **CPR above 60%**.`,`**Pearl index**: failures per 100 woman-years.`,`**Population momentum** keeps numbers rising; India peaks around 2060.`,`Demographic dividend window lasts to the **mid-2050s**.`]},{title:`Policy and programme`,points:[`Renamed **Family Welfare Programme in 1977**; target-free approach in **1996**.`,`**NPP 2000**: TFR to replacement by 2010; **stable population by 2045**.`,`**Eligible couple**: wife aged **15-45**; 150-180 per 1000 population.`,`Counsel with **GATHER**; choose by **WHO MEC** categories 1-4.`,`Camps were replaced by fixed-day services after **Devika Biswas (2016)**.`,`**Mission Parivar Vikas (2016)**: 146 districts in 7 states with TFR 3 or more.`]},{title:`Treatment - first line`,points:[`**Mala-N**: one daily for 21 days, then 7 iron tablets; start on day 1-5.`,`Missed 3 or more pills: take one, **use condoms for 7 days**.`,`**Chhaya**: twice weekly for 3 months, then weekly; safe in breastfeeding.`,`**Antara (DMPA 150 mg)** deep IM every 3 months; fertility returns slowly.`,`Progestin-only pill: daily **within 3 hours** of the same time.`,`**Cu-T 380A** lasts 10 years; **Cu 375** lasts 5 years.`]},{title:`Treatment - next steps`,points:[`**PPIUCD** within 10 minutes of placenta or within 48 hours.`,`Emergency: **levonorgestrel 1.5 mg within 72 hours**, or Cu-IUD within 5 days.`,`**LAM** protects up to 6 months if exclusively breastfeeding and amenorrhoeic.`,`After **vasectomy**, use condoms until semen shows azoospermia.`,`Combined pill is **MEC 4** in migraine with aura, past VTE, smokers over 35.`]},{title:`Complications and red flags`,points:[`Fever or severe pain after sterilisation: **peritonitis or bowel injury**.`,`Missed period after tubectomy: **exclude ectopic pregnancy**.`,`Pain or heavy bleeding with IUCD: **expulsion, perforation or PID**.`,`Leg swelling or chest pain on the pill: **thrombosis**.`,`Indemnity: **Rs 4 lakh** for death within 7 days; Rs 60,000 for failure.`]},{title:`Referral and follow-up`,points:[`Ask **fertility intention** at every contact; keep an eligible couple list.`,`Offer **postpartum contraception before discharge**.`,`**Promote male participation** and non-scalpel vasectomy.`,`Follow up continuation at **1 and 3 months**.`,`Unmet need **9.4%**; female sterilisation **37.9% versus male 0.3%** (NFHS-5).`,`NFHS-6: modern method use **fell to 52.7%**.`]}],tables:[{heading:`Contraceptives`,columns:[`Drug`,`Dose and route`,`Duration`,`Key side effects / cautions`],rows:[[`Combined pill (Mala-N)`,`1 tablet oral daily for 21 days, then 7 iron`,`Every cycle`,`Nausea, spotting; rare VTE`],[`Progestin-only pill`,`1 tablet oral daily, no break`,`Continuous`,`Irregular bleeding`],[`Chhaya (centchroman 30 mg)`,`Oral twice weekly, then weekly`,`3 months, then ongoing`,`Delayed periods`],[`Antara (DMPA 150 mg)`,`Deep IM`,`Every 3 months, 4-week grace`,`Amenorrhoea, weight gain, delayed fertility`],[`Levonorgestrel ECP`,`1.5 mg oral single dose`,`Within 72 hours`,`Nausea; not a regular method`],[`Cu-T 380A IUCD`,`Inserted once`,`10 years (Cu 375: 5)`,`Heavier periods, expulsion`]]},{heading:`Demographic cycle`,columns:[`Stage`,`Birth rate`,`Death rate`,`Example`],rows:[[`1 High stationary`,`High`,`High`,`India before 1921`],[`2 Early expanding`,`High`,`Falling`,`Many African countries`],[`3 Late expanding`,`Falling`,`Falls further`,`India now`],[`4 Low stationary`,`Low`,`Low`,`Europe`],[`5 Declining`,`Below death rate`,`Low`,`Japan, Germany`]]},{heading:`Fertility indicators`,columns:[`Indicator`,`Meaning`],rows:[[`CBR`,`Live births per 1000 mid-year population`],[`GFR`,`Live births per 1000 women 15-44 or 15-49`],[`TFR`,`Children per woman over reproductive life`],[`GRR`,`Daughters per woman, ignoring mortality`],[`NRR`,`Daughters per newborn girl; 1 = replacement`],[`CPR`,`Per cent of eligible couples protected`]]}],remember:[`India is **late expanding**; 1921 the great divide.`,`**NRR 1 = TFR 2.1 = CPR above 60%.**`,`Eligible couple: wife **15-45**.`,`NPP 2000: **stable population by 2045**.`,`**Chhaya twice weekly then weekly; Antara every 3 months.**`,`Counsel with **GATHER**.`]},updated:[`SRS 2023: TFR 1.9 (below replacement), CBR 18.4, CDR 6.4 - replaces SRS 2021 (TFR 2.0, CBR 19.3, CDR 7.5)`,`NFHS-6 (2023-24): TFR 2.0, any method 69.1%, modern methods fell to 52.7%, traditional 16.4%, female sterilisation 36.5%`,`WHO MEC 6th edition (November 2025) replaces the 5th edition (2015) as the eligibility reference`],master:{heading:`Master revision table - Demography and family planning`,columns:[`Aspect`,`High-yield points`],rows:[[`Definition`,`**Demography** = study of population **size, composition, distribution** and change by **fertility, mortality, migration**.`],[`Demographic cycle`,`**High stationary -> early expanding -> late expanding (India now) -> low stationary -> declining**; **1921 = year of the great divide**; India most populous since **2023** (UN WPP 2024); peak about 2060.`],[`Numbers to quote`,`Census 2011: **121.09 crore, growth 17.7%, sex ratio 943, literacy 74.04%, density 382, 31.2% urban**; **SRS 2023: CBR 18.4, CDR 6.4, TFR 1.9**; **NFHS-6 TFR 2.0**; LE 70.0 (2016-20); Census 2027 reference 1 March 2027.`],[`Fertility indicators`,`CBR, GFR (15-44/49), ASFR (peak 20-29), **TFR = sum of ASFRs**, **GRR** (daughters, no mortality), **NRR 1 = replacement = TFR about 2.1 = CPR over 60%**; **Pearl index = failures x 1200 / months of exposure**.`],[`Concepts`,`**Dependency ratio**; **demographic dividend** to mid-2050s; **population momentum**; ageing (60+ about 10% in 2021, doubling by 2050).`],[`Contraceptive classification`,`**Spacing** (barrier, OCP, POP, **Chhaya**, **Antara**, IUCD, natural, LAM) vs **terminal** (tubectomy, NSV) + **emergency**.`],[`Policy evolution`,`**1952 world's first FP programme**; 1977 Family Welfare; **1996 target-free**; 1997 RCH; **NPP 2000** (immediate unmet need; TFR 2.1 by 2010; **stable population 2045**); NHP 2017 TFR 2.1 by 2025 - achieved; FP2030.`],[`Method mix`,`NFHS-5: any 66.7%, modern 56.5%, **female sterilisation 37.9% vs male 0.3%**, unmet need **9.4%**; **NFHS-6: any 69.1%, modern 52.7%, traditional 16.4%, female sterilisation 36.5%**.`],[`Schemes`,`**Mission Parivar Vikas 2016** (146 districts TFR 3+, 7 states; extended 2021) - Nayi Pehel, Saas Bahu Sammelan, Saarthi; compensation (higher for NSV); **FPIS 2016** (death Rs 4 lakh); ASHA doorstep delivery; FP-LMIS; **Devika Biswas 2016** - no camps.`],[`How to use - hormonal`,`**Mala-N** 21 + 7 iron, start day 1-5, missed-pill rules; **Chhaya 30 mg twice weekly x 3 months then weekly**; **Antara 150 mg IM every 3 months (4-week grace)**; POP within 3 h same time daily.`],[`How to use - others`,`**Cu-T 380A 10 y, Cu 375 5 y**; **PPIUCD within 10 min of placenta or 48 h**; **LNG 1.5 mg within 72 h** or **Cu-IUD within 5 days**; LAM 6 months; NSV - condoms until azoospermia (about 3 months).`],[`Eligibility`,`**WHO MEC 6th edition (2025)** categories 1-4; COC MEC 4 - migraine with aura, BP 160/100+, VTE/stroke, smoker over 35 (15+/day), breastfeeding under 6 weeks, breast cancer; counsel by **GATHER**.`],[`Family physician role`,`Ask **fertility intention** each contact; eligible couple list (wife 15-45; 150-180 per 1000); MEC + GATHER; **postpartum contraception before discharge**; male participation and NSV; informed written consent; follow up continuation at 1 and 3 months.`],[`Follow-up & monitoring`,`**CPR**, modern CPR, **unmet need**, method mix, PPIUCD acceptance, 12-month continuation, higher-order births, sterilisation failures and deaths, stock-outs.`],[`Red flags & referral`,`Pain/fever after sterilisation; missed period after tubectomy (ectopic); bleeding/pain with IUCD; leg swelling, chest pain, severe headache on COC; coercion or no consent.`],[`Latest guideline change`,`**SRS 2023 TFR 1.9**; **NFHS-6 modern CPR down to 52.7%**; **WHO MEC 6th edition 2025**.`],[`Exam one-liners`,`NRR 1 = TFR about 2.1 = CPR over 60%; India launched the world's first national FP programme in 1952.`]]},flows:[{kind:`flow`,heading:`Choosing a contraceptive - counselling sequence`,steps:[{label:`Fertility intention`,detail:`Space, limit, or emergency need?`,tone:`decision`},{label:`Medical eligibility - WHO MEC 2025`,detail:`Category 1-2 use; 3 caution; 4 do not use`},{label:`GATHER counselling`,detail:`Greet, Ask, Tell, Help, Explain, Return`},{label:`Postpartum or breastfeeding?`,detail:`PPIUCD within 48 h, POP, LAM, Chhaya; no COC before 6 weeks`,tone:`warn`},{label:`Follow up at 1 and 3 months`,detail:`Side effects drive discontinuation`,tone:`good`}]},{kind:`ladder`,heading:`Demographic cycle - five stages`,steps:[{label:`1 High stationary`,detail:`High births, high deaths - India before 1921`},{label:`2 Early expanding`,detail:`Deaths begin to fall`},{label:`3 Late expanding`,detail:`Births falling - India now (TFR 1.9-2.0)`,tone:`decision`},{label:`4 Low stationary`,detail:`Low births and deaths - Europe`},{label:`5 Declining`,detail:`Births below deaths - Japan, Germany`,tone:`warn`}]},{kind:`compare`,heading:`Method mix - NFHS-5 against NFHS-6`,columns:[`Indicator`,`NFHS-5 (2019-21)`,`NFHS-6 (2023-24)`],rows:[[`Any method`,`66.7%`,`69.1%`],[`Modern methods`,`56.5%`,`52.7%`],[`Female sterilisation`,`37.9%`,`36.5%`],[`TFR`,`2.0`,`2.0`]]}]}};export{e as default};